588:* *** &#x27;No witness of either kind&#x27; IS STALE and must not read as &#x27;unreachable&#x27;. *** regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py is COMMITTED and drives TIMER_1_LOAD through real guest instructions on an unmodified CATS, and the MIPS-compare shape is boot-travelled on every pmax/arc boot. The path IS witnessable. What is absent is a SYMPTOM, because #427&#x27;s clamp removed the harmful half. Precise classification: REACHABLE, SYMPTOMLESS-BY-CONSTRUCTION -- correctly filed as design, not round-eligible. Disposition unchanged.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — REAL AS A SHAPE, CORRECTLY FILED AS DESIGN, AND NOT WORTH A ROUND -- close as a standing note. timer.h:84 timer_add(double freq,...) has NO one-shot form at all; footbridge reads a FREERUN/PERIODIC bit (dc21285reg.h:365-366) and ignores it, reload_timer_value (:224-248) always installs a repeating timer, and the TIMER_1_CLEAR distinction at :590 is a no-op because timer_update_frequency early-outs on an unchanged freq (timer.c:204). The author&#x27;s own comment at :250-256 admits it. BUT THE DANGEROUS HALF IS ALREADY FIXED (#427&#x27;s timer_clamp_freq turned the guest-writable zero divisor -&gt; +INF -&gt; 0.0 interval -&gt; in-handler hang into a bounded rate), so what remains is pure fidelity. AND IT HAS NO WITNESS OF EITHER KIND: none of the four bootable rigs (pmax, arc, luna88k, landisk) instantiates an ISA bus or a footbridge. Self-corrected mid-analysis: nearly asserted the 8253 is instantiated by nothing, but it is reachable via bus_isa.c:241 -- just not from a rig we can boot.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-26 — *** VERDICT: THE DEFECT IS REAL, BUT THE CITED WITNESS DOES NOT WITNESS IT -- AND THE ROW IS INTERNALLY CONFLATED. DOCUMENTED-ONLY/CLOSE under the stopping rule. *** EVERY ELEMENT RE-VERIFIED HERE BEFORE IT WAS ACTED ON, because this answer was used to correct the carrier: the row&#x27;s `witness` field is None; its closure names &#x27;the MIPS compare write ... converts the distance to a deadline into a frequency&#x27;; the row mentions ARM Footbridge four times and MIPS CP0 zero times; and `footbridge_sites_probe.py:111` execs `-E cats`, an ARM/SA110 machine that executes no MIPS instruction and cannot reach CP0 Compare. That probe returned entirely CORRECT values (vendor 0x1011; 0x00345678 after storing 0x12345678 -- the missing byte is the Footbridge&#x27;s deliberate 24-bit mask signature, not an error). Its cited `dev_footbridge.c:548` is stale: :548 is `console_putchar` and the timer mask is at :640. **So a witness WAS built, and it witnesses a different path from the one this row&#x27;s closure defines.** THE MIPS MECHANISM IS SOURCE-DEMONSTRABLE AND WORSE THAN &#x27;a different internal representation&#x27;: a Compare write installs a PERIODIC host timer (`cpu_mips_coproc.c:796`), every expiry increments `compare_interrupts_pending` (:418), and a guest Compare write acknowledges only ONE pending event while clearing Cause.IP7 (:822). If two or more callbacks have accumulated, the next dyntrans update re-asserts IP7 before any new Count==Compare event -- **a wrong guest-readable Cause value and an extra interrupt exception**, not an observationally equivalent model. IT ALSO KEPT THE EVIDENCE LADDER HONEST: the ~615,384-callbacks-per-signal figure came from a timer-core SCRATCH probe, not an end-to-end MIPS guest observation; and the 40.2-second guest clock freeze that was measured belongs to the separate `fbpending` overflow defect, whose #442 bound is in today&#x27;s source. Neither witnesses MIPS `dfreq`. *** CONSEQUENCE ACTED ON: CLAUDE.md&#x27;s line &#x27;dfreq&#x27;s [no-witness claim] was measured false by building the witness in one session&#x27; is a WRONG RECORD and was corrected in both carrier copies. The RULE -- that &#x27;no witness of either kind&#x27; is checkable and must be checked -- is untouched and remains one of the most useful lines in the file. *** — evidence: codex 5.6-SOL xhigh, fired against the row&#x27;s own hold_question (_scratchpad/holdq_briefs/holdq_dfreq.md; answer at scratchpad/holdq_out/dfreq.txt)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — DROP / close as a design note. Unanimous across all six seats that reached a verdict, reading and packet alike.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — CLOSE as a defect, keep as design: &#x27;do not schedule a correction round&#x27;. Unanimous across every seat that reached a verdict.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — DROP / close as a design note. Unanimous across all six seats that reached a verdict, reading and packet alike.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — DROP / close as a design note. Unanimous across all six seats that reached a verdict, reading and packet alike.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — DROP / close as a design note. Unanimous across all six seats that reached a verdict, reading and packet alike.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-17 — DROP / close as a design note. Unanimous across all six seats that reached a verdict, reading and packet alike.  [panel_20260817_174701]</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-held"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><