ne"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">multiaccesswarms</span><span class="ttl">a probe row that tests a SLOW-PATH fix with a MULTI-ACCESS instruction is vacuous from the second access onward -- the first cold access WARMS the page and every later access takes the FAST path, so the row cannot observe a slow-path mutant at all</span><span class="tip">a probe row that tests a SLOW-PATH fix with a MULTI-ACCESS instruction is vacuous from the second access onward -- the first cold access WARMS the page and every later access takes the FAST path, so the row cannot observe a slow-path mutant at all</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE CLASS IS LIVE AND ITS POPULATION IS THE FOUNDING ROW AND NOTHING ELSE.  An honest zero of NEW unlabelled vacuous rows, carried WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: 2 helpers with the shape x 3 shipped LDM/STM probes x 3 multi-access legs. ***  DO NOT CLOSE AS CENSUS-EMPTY: `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2&#x27; is still shipped and still inside gate_arm.sh&#x27;s pinned ARMBDT 43/43 -- a gate-counted always-green slot in its own denominator.  *** NOT MECHANICALLY ENUMERABLE, exactly as the deriver-keyed-on-a-correlate rule predicts: an LDM/STM/LDRD grep fails BOTH membership halves and sweeps in six non-members, including the #378 WARM/COLD pair which is the OPPOSITE polarity (a FAST-path repair).  The grep-able CANDIDATE GENERATOR -- not the class -- is a host_load/host_store test INSIDE a register loop, i.e. arm_pop and arm_push only; a per-row reading is still owed after it. ***  TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS TO THE FILED RULE.  (1) &#x27;every later access IN THAT SESSION&#x27; overstates it -- the warm unit is DYNTRANS_PAGESIZE 4096 indexed addr&gt;&gt;12, and returning to the GXemul&gt; prompt runs invalidate_translation_caches(INVALIDATE_ALL), so it does not survive a prompt.  (2) &#x27;more assertions mean more SESSIONS&#x27; is true but NOT REQUIRED: what the mechanism demands is more FIRST-ACCESSES TO A COLD PAGE, and a prompt return already invalidates -- so two cold observations fit in ONE process.  The intra-instruction half stands: nothing splits a multi-access loop between register i and i+1.  P2 is narrower than filed -- a slow-path mutant is invisible to access 2+ only where the helper HAS a fast arm; X(swp) and PPC lmw/stmw loop memory_rw every iteration and take none.  ONE NEW FINDING, labelling debt not a floor breach: ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7&#x27;s `w0&#x27; conjunct is access 2 (fast, 0x21003) and is unlabelled, while the row&#x27;s PC half is access 1 and still kills M_PUSHPC_NONE -- so the row is NOT vacuous and this is debt on it, not a new id and not L2.  *** HONEST LIMIT THE SEAT STATED ITSELF: it did NOT re-run the mutant table; the founding kill is taken from the round record.  This census is a READING. *** — evidence: regress/artifacts/multiaccesswarms/CENSUS_grok_20260917.txt and BRIEF_multiaccess.md; gate_arm.sh:731 (pin 43) and arm_bdt_probe.py:383 BOTH VERIFIED LOCALLY before this entry  [citations re-pointed 2026-09-17 from _scratchpad/ to the durable artifact copies; the _scratchpad originals are the same bytes]</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">silencewhitelist</span><span class="ttl">silence rows keyed on a literal or a quiet grammar are whitelists with a shape to hide in</span><span class="tip">silence rows keyed on a literal or a quiet grammar are whitelists with a shape to hide in — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pit8253flood</span><span class="ttl">pit8253flood  dev_8253.c: bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253) -- LATCH :204/:270/:342, DELETE :198/:200, :351/:354 are dead code</span><span class="tip">pit8253flood  dev_8253.c: bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253) -- LATCH :204/:270/:342, DELETE :198/:200, :351/:354 are dead code — witness: none — note: pass 1 complete 2026-09-17, nine seats + Fable adjudication.  STAYS FIRST in the queue -- Fable ruled the panel&#x27;s demotion wrong, on reach, with owner sequencing breaking a severity tie.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — PASS-1 BRIEF WRITTEN AND STAGED, NOT FIRED -- the machine is held by #530&#x27;s kill table 7 and nine CPU-heavy seats during a timed table is the contention the carrier forbids.  Written FROM tools/BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md rather than from memory; 462 lines / 22,038 bytes, under panel.sh&#x27;s ARGV_CAP of 30000 so codex and kimi get the INLINE form rather than a pointer.  Every cited path ls-checked; the frozen source copy&#x27;s md5 was verified to match what the brief transcribes.  *** TWO FINDINGS MADE WHILE WRITING IT, BOTH BEFORE ANY SEAT WAS FIRED. ***  (1) THE SIBLING CENSUS THIS ROW ASKED FOR RETURNS SEVEN SITES, NOT TWO.  The row&#x27;s own text says &#x27;a sibling census, not an assumption&#x27; and names the writing-side default: arm.  Running it over DEVICE_ACCESS(8253) finds SEVEN unlatched fatal() sites -- the two TODO arms, BOTH huh? defaults (write AND read), the BCD arm, and the unimplemented-address write and read arms.  FOUR were not anticipated by the filing.  Only the two TODO arms are established guest-drivable; the brief asks which of the other five are, because a latch at an unreachable site is the addition licence&#x27;s &#x27;cannot hurt&#x27;.  This changes the round&#x27;s SIZE and so the row title was corrected in the same edit.  (2) A NAIVE exit-grep OVER THIS FILE RETURNS 5 AND ALL FIVE ARE COMMENT TEXT -- the #223 comments that read &#x27;warn+ignore, do not exit() the host&#x27;.  THE FILE HAS ZERO LIVE exit().  I ran that grep first and it would have put a false premise -- five surviving host kills -- into a nine-seat packet, which is the class this project records as coming back CONFIRMED by seats that cannot check it.  Settled with the project&#x27;s OWN comment-aware tool, tools/pipeline/exit_census.py, which brief template section C0 names and which I found only by running C0&#x27;s third command; it reports 137 of 269 raw hits tree-wide (51%) are inside comments, and dev_8253.c is ABSENT from its live list of 26 files.  C0 CLEARANCE RUN RATHER THAN ASSUMED: last commit touching the file is af4884c (2026-08-20, #440, a different defect) and this row id appears 0 times in CHANGELOG.md, so the round has not shipped.  STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT open, NOT in work: no seat has answered, and marking a row in work on the strength of a staged brief is the gap-closes-itself shape. — evidence: _scratchpad/pit8253flood_pass1.md (462 lines, 22038 bytes); frozen source _scratchpad/dev_8253.frozen_20260917.c md5 a081564373cf81c2cf177b75ccbbc20d; tools/pipeline/exit_census.py output; recorded in _scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION.  Identity check passed: it named itself claude-fable-5-1 AND quoted CHECKPOINT.md&#x27;s first line verbatim, so prompt delivery and file access were proven rather than assumed.  *** IT OVERTURNED ONE OF MY SEVEN ACCEPTED DISPOSITIONS. ***  A5&#x27;s CONSEQUENCE -- &#x27;do not latch :342&#x27; -- is WRONG, because it confuses a BOUNDED ANNOUNCE with SILENCE.  A (once) latch announces the event on every boot that raises it; only repetition is dropped, and repetition carries no information once the defect is FILED.  Leaving :342 unlatched ends the round with a guest-drivable unbounded fatal() still in the file under a rationale that reads as principle and is not.  RULING: LATCH :342 AND REWRITE ITS TEXT, since &#x27;not yet implemented&#x27; implies a refusal when the write is ACCEPTED.  When the BCD fix later deletes the site, its row is re-pinned to ==0 under the oracle-correction ORDER -- re-pin, show red, then edit.  D1 RULED AGAINST MY LEAN, which is what I asked for: DELETE :198/:200, do not latch.  Its decisive evidence is something I had not found -- dev_pckbc.c:941-951 FAKES both downstream observables independently of the PIT, returning 0x21 with the refresh-toggle bit flipped by a function-static counter -- so there is NO guest-observable difference between modelling counters 1 and 2&#x27;s outputs and not modelling them.  That is member 4&#x27;s definition.  And the OpenBSD bell path that DOES program counter 2 has its other half, the 0x61 speaker-enable write, already ignored SILENTLY: announcing one half of an unmodelled bell is residue, not a house idiom.  D2 accepted -- it changes each row&#x27;s ORACLE rather than the row count, plus ONE combined-session row which is the only thing that catches a cross-site field typo.  *** AND IT NAMED A LIVE HAZARD I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: pit8253_latch_probe.py:321 keys COMPLAINT on the literal &#x27;huh? reading from counter&#x27; and row R2 wants ZERO of them after a control word, so ANY rewrite of the :270 text turns R2 into an absence row that CANNOT FAIL. ***  D3: witnessable, ORDER-constrained, not documented-only -- and it supplied the exact session order.  ORDER: pit8253flood STAYS FIRST and the demotion is NOT right.  Neither uncovered defect has a real-guest driver (codex searched the guest sources: no BCD, no counter-1 programming), severity is a tie at MED, and OWNER SEQUENCING BREAKS TIES.  SCOPE: five sites are ONE round, because two dispositions in one diff are still one fix design -- bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/; _scratchpad/pit8253_adjudication.md; _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Agent seat, FILE-READING and this roster&#x27;s measuring seat.  VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP AS BRIEFED.  *** ALONE AMONG NINE SEATS IT FOUND pit8253bcdbinary -- the BCD bit accepted at :310 and the count decoded as binary at :181-184, putting counter 0&#x27;s host timer ~4x off. ***  Also: :351/:354 are structurally unreachable (DEV_8253_LENGTH 4, one registration tree-wide, all four relative_addr values named); :198/:200 are member 4 ACCEPT not member 3, because nothing is declined and hz[1]/hz[2] have no consumer; member 7 IMPLEMENT is UNAVAILABLE for :200 since the tree has no audio backend, so the rejection is for availability rather than because a latch is right; FIVE separate named int fields by dev_wdc.c:93-102&#x27;s runtime-mutual-exclusivity discriminator; and clause (d) is applicable though de-escalation is not, with the smallest null-repair mutant named (d-&gt;warned_tone becomes static int warned_tone -- ONE WORD, and every emission-count row stays GREEN because a single-machine probe has one device instance). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — GPT-6 Astra at max, FILE-READING, 1,268,922 b.  Confirmed dev_wdc.c uses separate named int fields rather than a bitfield; ruled the de-escalation clause INAPPLICABLE and corrected the queue&#x27;s own sentence (it &#x27;reverses its premise&#x27;); INDEPENDENTLY found that the LSB-only arm installs no timer; supplied the witness-ORDERING constraint -- a signature control word DESTROYS the initial-format condition :204/:270 require, because mode_byte is shared across counters; and stated its own limit honestly: it built no witness, so that lifecycle step must not be recorded as completed. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, 17,226 b, fired under PANEL_FIRE_AGY=1 per the 2026-09-15 owner directive.  VERDICT: GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Verified the frozen md5, the C0 clearance, the exit_census figures and the (once) census against source item by item.  *** IT FOUND A FALSE CLAIM IN MY BRIEF: section C2 said 26 files appear in exit_census&#x27;s live per-file list; the real figure is 44. ***  My deriver keyed on a lowercase-and-underscore filename pattern and so silently excluded EVERY filename containing a DIGIT -- dev_8253.c, lk201.c, dev_pcc2.c and fifteen more.  That is the deriver-keyed-on-a-correlate failure this project already records, committed by me inside a brief that cites the rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, 16,579 b.  VERDICT: NOT RATIFY member 3 for all seven.  Seven is a complete grep of the function, not seven guest-drivable floods, not seven declines, not seven IMPLEMENT stubs.  Two sites have no producer in the registered map; two more die after the first control word because mode_byte is shared; and the two named TODOs ACCEPT THE STORE FIRST.  Latch the reachable announces per site, do not decline them; de-escalation inapplicable. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — TRANSPORT: kimi-k3:cloud (the PACKET-FED substitute). the kimi SUBSTITUTE transport, PACKET-FED, 9,369 b -- fired ALONGSIDE the live CLI on this panel, so the kimi seat carries one file-reading and one packet-fed answer and they are recorded BY TRANSPORT as the doctrine requires.  *** IT FOUND pit8253lsbnotimer FROM THE INLINED FUNCTION ALONE: the LSB-only path never installs or updates any timer, so a guest programming RW=01 gets a silently dead counter with no warning at all. ***  Independently corroborated by codex, a file-reading seat, by an entirely different route. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — glm-5.3:cloud, PACKET-FED, 10,525 b.  *** IT PROVED MY C1 PREMISE FROM THE PACKET ALONE, AND MORE ELEGANTLY THAN MY FILE-READ DID: the brief&#x27;s own 18-emissions-per-session figure is itself proof that fatal() does not exit, because a killing fatal() caps the count at 1 and eighteen emissions require seventeen survivals. ***  It also found a detector defect no other seat raised: the emission-counting detector is ONE-SIDED, since a site silently deleted or demoted to debug() passes any &#x27;count &lt;= 1&#x27; row; rows must assert ==1 on first drive AND +0 on immediate repeat.  And it correctly conditioned :351/:354 as UNSETTLEABLE from the packet while naming the missing fact. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — deepseek-v4-pro:cloud, PACKET-FED, 5,571 b.  Marked every claim READ / REASONED / NOT-CHECKABLE throughout, which is the honest form this doctrine asks for.  Refused to settle :351/:354 and named the missing fact precisely (the device&#x27;s registered address range).  Ruled the uniform latched-announce likely wrong for case 1, case 2 and BCD as IMPLEMENT candidates, and named the #490 latch-bit collision BY NUMBER against a single shared latch bit. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — minimax-m3:cloud, PACKET-FED, 7,636 b.  *** IT FOUND THE DERIVATION DEFECT IN MY BRIEF&#x27;S SECTION C1: the debug()-versus-fatal() early-out contrast proves only that -q cannot silence it, and does NOT exclude an exit() after va_debug() returns or inside va_debug itself. ***  Correct, and the conclusion survived only after I went and read the right evidence.  It also separated the seven-in-function claim from the seven-in-file claim, which my brief had conflated. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">macepciwidth</span><span class="ttl">DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci) has #529&#x27;s width class UNFIXED, 530 lines below the handler #529 fixed -- and it does not decline at all</span><span class="tip">DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci) has #529&#x27;s width class UNFIXED, 530 lines below the handler #529 fixed -- and it does not decline at all — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the repair-14 panel; owes a rung-3 ATTEMPT before any round</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** #529 FIXED THE WIDTH CLASS IN DEVICE_ACCESS(crime) AND LEFT THE IDENTICAL CLASS UNTOUCHED IN DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci), IN THE SAME FILE.  The sibling is WORSE: it does not decline at all. *** dev_sgi_ip32.c:728-729 is `if (len != 4) fatal(&quot;[ macepci: unimplemented len %i ]&quot;, len);&#x27; with NO goto, NO return and NO latch -- so a narrow access PRINTS (unlatched, therefore FLOODS) and then FALLS THROUGH INTO THE SWITCH WITH THE WRONG LENGTH, which is precisely the behaviour #529&#x27;s own comment enumerates as the reason its goto exists.  Verified by reading the handler, not taken from the seat. *** IT ALSO CORRECTED A RECORDS DEFECT I HAD JUST MADE WORSE. ***  OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md&#x27;s straddling-access entry cites this site in a list of `register-width devices that REJECT an unexpected length&#x27;.  macepci does NOT reject.  The citation was ALSO stale (:698, moved to :728 by #529&#x27;s own diff) and I corrected only the NUMBER first, leaving the false CLAIM standing -- *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*.  Both halves fixed now, and the sibling citations were grepped: dev_sgi_gbe.c:726 and dev_kn01.c:89 both still land on their guards, so the stale population was ONE of three, not three of three. SEVERITY MED, NOT HIGH, and stated honestly: NO WITNESS EXISTS.  Whether a guest driver issues a narrow access to macepci config space is UNKNOWN and #529&#x27;s witness does NOT transfer (it was at CRIME).  A round taking this owes the rung-3 ATTEMPT first.  The tree&#x27;s own answer for this exact shape is #529&#x27;s: DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with `(once)&#x27;. — evidence: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c; OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md tail entry; regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/repair14_20260916/opus_pass1.md (the seat that surfaced it)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">crimectlflood</span><span class="ttl">Guest-drivable UNLATCHED fatal() on CRIME_CONTROL writes with unmodelled bits -- and latching it REDS A SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW</span><span class="tip">Guest-drivable UNLATCHED fatal() on CRIME_CONTROL writes with unmodelled bits -- and latching it REDS A SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW — note: filed 2026-09-17; NOT a free tidy-up -- the fix owes the oracle-correction ORDER</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — dev_sgi_ip32.c:377-379: `if (idata != 0) fatal(&quot;[ CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented control 0x%016llx ]&quot;, ...)&#x27; -- every CONTROL write carrying a bit the model does not implement prints, with no latch and no `(once)&#x27; marker.  Same class as pit8253flood; belongs to floodclass. *** THE REASON TO FILE IT RATHER THAN FIX IT: LATCHING IT WOULD RED A SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW, AND THE COUPLING IS EXACT. ***  regress/crime_width_probe.py:1256 is `row(&quot;p acc ctl msg&quot;, count(t, &quot;CRIME_CONTROL: unimplemented&quot;, clean), 3, ...)&#x27; -- it COUNTS this very message and expects THREE, one per accepted CONTROL write (0x01 sd, 0x0001 sw, 0x0201 sw).  A latch takes that count to ONE and reds the row. SO WHOEVER TAKES IT MUST USE THE ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER: re-pin the row FIRST, measure the COMMITTED build RED under the new expectation, THEN change the code.  Otherwise the fix and its detector land together and nothing distinguishes a repair from a test fitted to a binary. LOW-MED because the flood is bounded in practice: the message fires only on CONTROL writes with unmodelled bits, which the #529/#530 probes drive deliberately and a real guest driver largely does not. — evidence: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c; OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md tail entry; regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/repair14_20260916/opus_pass1.md (the seat that surfaced it)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">crimeramexit</span><span class="ttl">Two surviving exit(1) in DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)&#x27;s RAM-size prelude -- machine-config gated, not guest-drivable</span><span class="tip">Two surviving exit(1) in DEVICE_ACCESS(crime)&#x27;s RAM-size prelude -- machine-config gated, not guest-drivable — note: filed 2026-09-17 on Fable&#x27;s ruling: not this round&#x27;s fix, but not a silence either</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-ON-RULING</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;FILED-ON-RULING&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Found by the Opus measuring seat during repair 14 and FILED ON FABLE&#x27;S EXPLICIT DIRECTION: *`not this round&#x27; is a FILE, not a SILENCE* -- the seat found them and the findings record carried no disposition. TWO exit(1) survive inside the very function #529 de-fatalised: `physical_ram_in_mb &gt; 1024&#x27; and `physical_ram_in_mb % mb_per_bank&#x27;. *** A CITATION NOTE WORTH KEEPING, because two seats gave different line numbers and BOTH WERE RIGHT: Fable cited :244/:255 and Opus cited :246/:259.  The GUARDS are at 244 and 255; the exit(1) CALLS are at 246 and 259.  They were citing different things, not disagreeing. *** NOT GUEST-DRIVABLE and that is why it is filed rather than fixed: `cpu-&gt;machine-&gt;physical_ram_in_mb&#x27; is set at machine CONSTRUCTION from the -M option, and no guest access reaches it.  So this is a host-usability defect (a bad -M ends the process instead of being rejected with a message), not a guest-reachable one.  The contract should SAY SO -- Fable&#x27;s point is that an undisposed finding in a de-fatalisation round reads as an oversight. — evidence: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c guards at :244 and :255, exit(1) at :246 and :259</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">crimesplitwidth</span><span class="ttl">Split widths 3/5/6/7 unreachable at CRIME only because DEV_CRIME_LENGTH is a multiple of 8 -- no rows if that changes</span><span class="tip">Split widths 3/5/6/7 unreachable at CRIME only because DEV_CRIME_LENGTH is a multiple of 8 -- no rows if that changes — note: filed 2026-09-17 on Fable ruling item (6); LATENT, stated as a CONDITION not a count</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-ON-RULING</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;FILED-ON-RULING&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILED ON FABLE ORDER ITEM (6).  A real MIPS guest emits len in {1,2,4,8} exactly, BUT memory_rw.c:523 CLAMPS a straddling access at a device end -- len = length - paddr -- which can be 1..7 in general.  It is inert at CRIME ONLY because DEV_CRIME_LENGTH is 0x280 = 8 x 80, so for a naturally aligned access the clamp is always a multiple of the width.  **Change that one define to a non-multiple of 8 and widths 3/5/6/7 become reachable at a handler whose detector has never seen them.**  The FIX is almost certainly correct for them -- the decline arm treats every non-4, non-8 width alike -- what is missing is any row that would notice if it were not. *** THE REASSURING HALF, recorded so nobody re-derives it: the constant is NOT free to change, because :77 sizes the register array with it (reg[DEV_CRIME_LENGTH / sizeof(uint64_t)]), so a non-multiple of 8 would also truncate the array.  A future edit must defeat both. *** PROVENANCE is a three-cornered disagreement that ended in a CONDITION rather than a number: deepseek wanted the lane and PC as free factors; codex read the producer and showed the domain is REACHABILITY-CONSTRAINED; the Opus adjudication then showed codex own argument is CONDITIONAL on this constant.  Fable keeps D1 OPEN and says explicitly to state it as a condition, not as 2. LOW and LATENT: no witness, no reachable path today, and rowing it now would add rows for a state the machine cannot enter -- the cannot-hurt the addition licence refuses. — evidence: dev_sgi_ip32.c:75 DEV_CRIME_LENGTH 0x280 and :77 the array sized by it; memory_rw.c:523 the clamp</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pciaddrreadclobber</span><span class="ttl">A READ of PCI ADDR (0xcf8) decomposes idata=0 and clobbers the latched address -- in ALL THREE PCI bridges, so an upstream idiom rather than a macepci bug</span><span class="tip">A READ of PCI ADDR (0xcf8) decomposes idata=0 and clobbers the latched address -- in ALL THREE PCI bridges, so an upstream idiom rather than a macepci bug — note: filed 2026-09-17 while triaging macepciwidth severity; LATENT, NO WITNESS and none attempted; a CLASS across three files, fix all or none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — A READ of the PCI ADDR register runs the WRITE path.  idata is declared = 0 and assigned ONLY inside if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE), and the 0xcf8 arm tests writeflag NOWHERE -- so a read decomposes ZERO, latches bus 0 / device 0 / function 0 / register 0, and returns 0.  It both answers wrongly and CLOBBERS the address a previous write latched.  *** THE SIBLING CENSUS IS THE FINDING AND IT REVERSES THE OBVIOUS READING. ***  This looked like a macepci outlier; it is not.  ALL THREE PCI bridges in the tree share the shape -- dev_sgi_ip32.c (macepci, case 0xcf8), dev_cpc700.c (cpc700_pci, case 0) and dev_eagle.c (eagle, case 0) -- so it is an UPSTREAM IDIOM rather than damage this fork did, and a round fixing macepci alone would leave the class open in two files while making the tree inconsistent.  The fix lands in all three or in none.  LOW because the damage needs a guest that READS 0xcf8 between writing it and reading 0xcfc; the common driver idiom is write-ADDR then read-DATA, which never touches this path.  NO WITNESS EXISTS AND NONE WAS ATTEMPTED -- recorded as not-yet-attempted rather than as absence, because those are different states.  Found while triaging macepciwidth&#x27;s SEVERITY, not while looking for it.  *** AND THE TRIAGE ITSELF CAME BACK NEGATIVE, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF THIS ENTRY: macepciwidth STAYS MED. ***  Its len != 4 fall-through is NOT memory-unsafe, now CHECKED rather than assumed -- memory_readmax64 and memory_writemax64 are both bounded by len, the only array index reachable through bus_pci_data_access is masked (cfg_base[ofs &amp; (PCI_CFG_MEM_SIZE - 1)]), and config writes go through the dev-&gt;cfg_reg_write callback rather than a direct index.  The cost is an unlatched flood plus wrong-value semantics, which is correctness and not memory safety.  The macepciwidth entry&#x27;s phrase &#x27;the sibling is WORSE&#x27; is true of the DECLINE BEHAVIOUR and FALSE of severity; recorded so a future round does not read it as grounds to escalate. — evidence: src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci) case 0xcf8; src/devices/dev_cpc700.c case 0; src/devices/dev_eagle.c case 0; src/devices/bus_pci.c bus_pci_data_access</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pit8253lsbnotimer</span><span class="ttl">dev_8253.c one-byte formats: BOTH arms keep the other byte stale against the datasheet, and the LSB arm additionally installs no timer -- wrong host rate on counter 0</span><span class="tip">dev_8253.c one-byte formats: BOTH arms keep the other byte stale against the datasheet, and the LSB arm additionally installs no timer -- wrong host rate on counter 0 — note: filed 2026-09-17 by the kimi-k3 seat, WIDENED the same day by Fable 5.1&#x27;s adjudication from a datasheet passage I read past</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THIS ROW WAS UNDER-SCOPED AND THE DATASHEET QUESTION IT FILED AS OPEN IS ANSWERED LOCALLY. ***  Fable found the governing sentence at _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172: &#x27;CRM and CRL are cleared when the Counter is programmed.  In this way if the Counter has been programmed for one byte counts (either most significant byte only or least significant byte only) the other byte will be zero.&#x27;  THE MODEL DOES THE OPPOSITE IN BOTH ONE-BYTE ARMS -- :175 masks with 0xff00 and keeps a STALE MSB, :179 masks with 0x00ff and keeps a STALE LSB -- where the datasheet requires the other byte to be ZERO.  So the defect is not only &#x27;the LSB arm starts no timer&#x27;: THE COMPOSED COUNT IS WRONG IN BOTH ARMS, and the MSB-only arm is the one that DOES compute hz and call timer_add/timer_update_frequency, so a stale low byte yields a WRONG HOST TIMER RATE ON COUNTER 0 -- the same consequence as pit8253bcdbinary, reached by a different route.  Severity raised MED -&gt; MED-HIGH accordingly: the original filing described the weaker half.  The datasheet also names the FIX SHAPE rather than leaving it to design: clear counter[sel] at the CONTROL WORD (dev_8253.c:310-312), which is exactly where the datasheet says the CR is cleared.  *** I HAD READ THIS DATASHEET AN HOUR EARLIER, FOR THIS ROW, AND WALKED PAST THE PASSAGE. ***  I grepped for &#x27;BCD&#x27; and read the hits; the CR-clearing rule is 500 lines away and mentions neither BCD nor LSB in terms my grep matched.  A targeted grep over a primary source answers the question you asked it, not the question you should have asked. — evidence: _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172; src/devices/dev_8253.c:175, :179, :181-184, :192-196 — record: 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this row.  Same provenance as `pit8253bcdbinary`: the entries cite `panel_20260917_063557`, convened on `pit8253flood`.  Three recorded seats here, which is again why a count cannot do this sorting.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — PRIMARY SOURCE MAKES THIS WORSE THAN FILED.  _scratchpad/i8254.txt :219 lists RW1 RW0 = 0 1 as &#x27;Read Write least significant byte only&#x27;, a first-class mode beside MSB-only and 16-bit.  And :528 states the conventions for ALL mode timing diagrams: &#x27;Counters are programmed for binary (not BCD) counting and for reading writing least significant byte (LSB) only&#x27;.  *** EVERY MODE TIMING DIAGRAM IN THE INTEL DATASHEET IS DRAWN IN LSB-ONLY MODE. ***  It is the manufacturer&#x27;s canonical illustration case, not an exotic corner -- and it is the one programming mode in which this model never starts a timer. — evidence: _scratchpad/i8254.txt (Intel 8254 datasheet); _scratchpad/i8254.pdf present since 2026-08-15</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — A counter programmed in LSB-ONLY mode (RW=01) is SILENTLY DEAD: the LSB arm updates counter[] and nothing else -- no hz[] recompute, no timer_add, no timer_update_frequency, and no diagnostic -- while the MSB arm does all three.  RW=01 is a LEGITIMATE 8253 mode (I8253_TIMER_LSB 0x10), so this is not a refusal decision: the model implements one half of a two-half register and not the other.  It is option-set member 7 (IMPLEMENT), not a latch question.  TWO consequences and the second is arguably worse: a counter programmed LSB-only never starts, silently, and for counter 0 that is the interrupt source; and if a timer was ALREADY installed by an earlier MSB write, a later LSB-only write changes counter[] while the running timer keeps the OLD frequency, so recorded counter and actual rate DIVERGE with no diagnostic.  *** FOUND BY A PACKET-FED SEAT (kimi-k3:cloud) FROM THE INLINED FUNCTION ALONE, while answering a brief about the flood. ***  Verified by reading before filing; the seat&#x27;s claim was exact.  The design question -- should the LSB arm install the timer by symmetry, or does the real 8253 defer until both halves are written in 16-bit mode -- is a DATASHEET question, and _scratchpad/i8254.pdf and i8254.txt are present locally, so it is answerable without guessing.  Caution for the row author: an MSB write to counter 0 calls timer_add with hz = 1193182/counter, so a SMALL counter installs a HIGH-FREQUENCY host timer; keep it large. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ollama_kimi.txt; src/devices/dev_8253.c</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pit8253bcdbinary</span><span class="ttl">dev_8253.c accepts a BCD control word then reads the count as BINARY -- a BCD counter runs at the wrong rate, and for counter 0 that is the host timer (~4x)</span><span class="tip">dev_8253.c accepts a BCD control word then reads the count as BINARY -- a BCD counter runs at the wrong rate, and for counter 0 that is the host timer (~4x) — note: filed 2026-09-17 from pit8253flood pass 1; LIVE and guest-visible.  Severity corrected MED-HIGH -&gt; MED by Fable 5.1 on REACH: no real-guest driver found.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — SEVERITY CORRECTED MED-HIGH -&gt; MED, and the correction is about REACH rather than impact.  The impact stands exactly as filed -- a BCD counter decoded as binary puts counter 0&#x27;s host timer ~4x off -- but codex searched the guest sources and found NO guest that programs BCD, and none that programs counter 1 at all.  I had set MED-HIGH on impact alone without weighing whether anything reaches it, which is the same mistake as ranking by witness rung instead of by harm, inverted.  *** AND THE CONSEQUENCE I ATTACHED TO THIS ROW WAS OVERTURNED: see pit8253flood.  I had ruled &#x27;do not latch :342 because it is the only surviving symptom&#x27;; Fable ruled that confuses a BOUNDED ANNOUNCE with SILENCE, and that repetition carries no information once the defect is FILED -- which it now is, by this row. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/pit8253_adjudication.md; panel_20260917_063557/ — record: 2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this row.  Its entries cite `panel_20260917_063557`, whose brief is `PASS 1 -- pit8253flood: seven unlatched guest-drivable fatal() sites`: a panel on `pit8253flood`, a DIFFERENT row.  Seats naming `pit8253bcdbinary` there were pointing at sibling defects in the same file, which is a finding worth keeping and not a stage.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — PRIMARY SOURCE, read the same day rather than cited from a paragraph: _scratchpad/i8254.txt :213-216 gives the control word&#x27;s BCD bit as 0 = Binary Counter 16-bits and 1 = Binary Coded Decimal (BCD) Counter (4 Decades); :671-673 says the largest initial count 0 is equivalent to 2^16 binary and 10^4 BCD; :677-678 says the counter wraps to FFFF hex for binary or 9999 for BCD.  THE DATASHEET THEREFORE STATES THE DEFECT DIRECTLY -- a BCD counter is a 4-decade counter and the model applies 16-bit binary arithmetic to the same bytes.  The Opus seat&#x27;s 0x9999 example is the datasheet&#x27;s own maximum BCD count, not an arbitrary worst case.  A round taking this row does not need to reason about what BCD means. — evidence: _scratchpad/i8254.txt (Intel 8254 datasheet); _scratchpad/i8254.pdf present since 2026-08-15</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pitmodebyteshared</span><span class="ttl">dev_8253.c keeps ONE mode_byte for all three counters, so programming any counter overwrites the RW format of the other two</span><span class="tip">dev_8253.c keeps ONE mode_byte for all three counters, so programming any counter overwrites the RW format of the other two — note: filed 2026-09-17 on Fable 5.1&#x27;s records finding: this defect had NO ledger row and existed only as a residual bullet under #440&#x27;s CHANGELOG block</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** A RECORDS DEFECT FOUND BY THE ADJUDICATOR, AND IT IS THE BURIED-BY-CLOSURE SHAPE THIS PROJECT ALREADY RECORDS. ***  Two different defects were travelling under the id &#x27;pitclobber&#x27;.  The LEDGER row of that name is #439&#x27;s -- an 8253 LATCH command clobbering the counter&#x27;s programmed RW format -- and it is state &#x27;in work&#x27; with a hold text reading FIX SHIPPED (#439) and a state_note &#x27;Recommend CLOSE&#x27;.  The SHARED-mode_byte-ACROSS-COUNTERS defect, which codex&#x27;s witness-ordering constraint depends on and which the panel packet called &#x27;pitclobber&#x27;, had NO LEDGER ROW AT ALL -- it survived only as a residual bullet at OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:6693 under #440&#x27;s block.  **Closing the pitclobber row would therefore have buried a live defect that no row carried.**  THE DEFECT ITSELF: mode_byte is ONE field (dev_8253.c:78) written at exactly one site (:310), so a control word programming ANY counter replaces the RW format of all three.  That is why :204/:270 fire only before the first non-latch control word, and it is why a device-signature control word on another counter is NOT neutral for a witness -- the constraint codex raised.  It needs its own id before any round is built on it, which is what this row is. — evidence: src/devices/dev_8253.c:78 (the field), :310 (its only writer); OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:6693</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdmaxferflood</span><span class="ttl">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c&#x27;s &#x27;Transfering %i bytes&#x27; fatal() is UNLATCHED on the NORMAL DMA path, so it floods on every legal guest transfer</span><span class="tip">dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c&#x27;s &#x27;Transfering %i bytes&#x27; fatal() is UNLATCHED on the NORMAL DMA path, so it floods on every legal guest transfer — note: filed 2026-09-17 by the agy seat during the late-review panel on the six unreviewed fixes -- found while auditing a DIFFERENT question, the standing license working as intended.  *** DISPOSITION IS LATE-DISSENT 3 (file as a new item), NOT an L2 against #462, and the reason is MEASURED not procedural: `git show 7c9dd9e^:src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c | grep -n Transfering` returns the identical unlatched fatal() at :825.  #462 MOVED and re-pointed it (at d-&gt;data_len rather than `length`); it did not introduce it. ***  NOT the one-line change it looks like: the flood is on the SUCCESS path, so the (once) idiom is the wrong shape -- latching would suppress genuinely per-transfer information, and the row `nodata notx` PINS this line MEASURED RED at 50/51 (probe_pre51.raw), so a latch MOVES that row.  The option set needs naming before a fix is designed.  The file already carries 12 correctly latched sites and an enumerated GDROM_W_* bit-position scheme built for this.  *** UPHELD MED by the Fable adjudication 2026-09-17 (9 of 9), which verified the parent at `7c9dd9e^:825&#x27; shows the same unlatched fatal() above the NULL test -- #462 MOVED it and CHANGED ITS ARGUMENTS.  ***  AND A COUPLING THAT MUST BE WRITTEN INTO ANY FIX FOR THIS ROW: the Opus seat&#x27;s finding (b) proposes making `accept dma&#x27; PARSE THE NUMBER on this very line (`Transfering (\d+) bytes&#x27; asserting 408), because no row reads it today.  **So whoever later LATCHES or DEMOTES this line must re-pin that row IN ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER** -- re-pin first, measure the committed build RED under the new expectation, then change the code.  Fable calls this &#x27;the crimectlflood coupling again&#x27;.  Two rows, one line, and they move in opposite directions: one wants the line QUIETER, the other wants it PARSED.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — agy&#x27;s words: the line &#x27;is UNLATCHED and executes on EVERY legal guest DMA transfer.  It is guest-drivable and constitutes a log flood under normal driver operation.&#x27;  VERIFIED, and the provenance check CHANGED THE DISPOSITION: the pre-#462 file carries the same unlatched call, so this is a pre-existing condition surfaced by the review, not a regression the review caught.  Filed rather than fixed in-round per the late-dissent rule -- closure changes the LICENSE, never the truth-value, and this is &#x27;everything else&#x27;. — evidence: src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:1497 (the unlatched fatal, post-move); 7c9dd9e^:825 (the same line, already unlatched, pre-move); src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:224-243 (the GDROM_W_* scheme it does not use); _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">cpusexitsweep</span><span class="ttl">exitsweep censused terminating sites across src/devices/ ONLY -- src/cpus/ was never covered, and cpu-&gt;running = 0 is a second shape an exit(-keyed sweep cannot see</span><span class="tip">exitsweep censused terminating sites across src/devices/ ONLY -- src/cpus/ was never covered, and cpu-&gt;running = 0 is a second shape an exit(-keyed sweep cannot see — note: filed 2026-09-17.  The gap is in the CLOSED exitsweep row&#x27;s own title: &#x27;terminating sites across src/devices/&#x27;.  src/cpus/ holds 111 exit( across 23 files, 1 abort(, and 52 `cpu-&gt;running = 0&#x27; across 10 files (deriver: grep -roh over src/cpus/*.c; RE-RUN, do not quote). *** THE COUNT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND NOT THE CLASS -- PAL_halt is a legitimate halt; what defines the class is reachability from a GUEST-SUPPLIED SELECTOR, which no regex tests. ***  Verified instances: alpha_prom_call() reads a0-a3 from guest registers and BOTH its default: arms do fatal() + cpu-&gt;running = 0, so a guest setting a register and making a PAL call stops the emulator.  Ranked HIGH for the CENSUS, not for any single site.  Owes a src/cpus/ census on the same footing exitsweep gave src/devices/, keyed on guest reachability, each site triaged under the seven-member option set.  *** FIGURES CORRECTED WITHIN THE HOUR: the filed 111 exit( / 52 running=0 were the RAW STRING COUNT and included this project&#x27;s OWN COMMENTS about the pattern.  Stripping block comments, line comments and string literals gives **73 exit( and 41 running=0 IN CODE**.  FOUR files (cpu_mips_instr.c 5, cpu_sh_instr.c 2, cpu_ppc_instr.c 1, cpu_m88k_instr.c 1) have ZERO real sites and were listed as having them.  Caught because cpu_alpha_palcode.c:413 enumerated as a site and is the COMMENT that documents the other sites -- the census counted its own documentation.  THE CLASS IS SHARPER THAN &#x27;keyed on a correlate&#x27;: a string-keyed census is inflated by its own prose whenever the codebase comments on the pattern being counted, and a de-fatalisation fork is the worst case for that.  The SUBSTANCE is unchanged -- src/cpus/ is still uncensused and both palcode arms are still guest-drivable halts. ***  *** RELABELLED RESEARCH (was HIGH) by the Fable adjudication 2026-09-17: &#x27;HIGH is a defect severity; a census has no witness and is a research item that generates rows.  Label it RESEARCH with a priority, not a severity.&#x27;  The deriver being stated is right and is kept.  STILL OWED: the FOUR-&gt;FIVE L4 at cpu_alpha_palcode.c -- the in-file comment still says FOUR sites and the triage found FIVE. ***  *** `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s CENSUS IS SPELLING-KEYED AND CANNOT SEE A THIRD OF ITS OWN POPULATION. The concealment is THREE-LAYERED, and I verified all three myself. ***  **(1) `running = 0` vs `running = false`.**  Measured in `src/cpus/`: **`= 0` gives 54 hits across 11 files; `= false` gives 20 hits across 10 files.**  A `= 0`-keyed census misses every one of the 20 -- and the files it misses include `cpu_dyntrans.c`, `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c`, `cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c` and `cpu_sh_instr.c`, which is **four files the sweeping seat&#x27;s own table did not list either**.  The `= false` population is the m88k / i960 / RISC-V / MIPS-reboot family. **(2) `ABORT_EXECUTION` IS A MACRO, so neither spelling appears at the USE sites.**  It is defined three times -- `cpu_i960_instr.c:45`, `cpu_m88k_instr.c:45`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:45` -- and every invocation is a bare `ABORT_EXECUTION`.  A grep for either assignment spelling finds the three definitions and none of the uses. **(3) COMMENTS AND `#if 0` INFLATE BOTH SPELLINGS**, so the raw hit counts above are an upper bound, not the live population -- the seat stripped them programmatically and found, for example, that `cpu_dyntrans.c` has ONE live `= 0` (the `bad` label) with the other two commented out, and that `cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c`&#x27;s alignment halt sits in the `#else` of an `#if 1`. **THIS IS THE SESSION&#x27;S DOMINANT FAILURE FAMILY ARRIVING IN A SHIPPED CENSUS ROW:** the deriver measured a CORRELATE -- one way of spelling the assignment -- rather than the thing, which is *does this code path stop the emulated CPU*.  The carrier already states the rule (*&#x27;before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member&#x27;*) and `staticlatchscope` is the recorded precedent where three name-keyed censuses of one class all came back wrong. **THE FIX IS A RE-DERIVATION, NOT A SWEEP.**  `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s number is owed a re-run keyed on the three spellings together, with comments and `#if 0` stripped and macro uses expanded. No code changes; the row&#x27;s count is the deliverable.  *** `abort()` IS ABSENT FROM `src/cpus/`: ZERO CALLS.  An empty result with its denominator, and it CLOSES that class for this domain. ***  The only hit is a COMMENT at `memory_alpha.c:195` -- *&#x27;must not abort()/exit() the host&#x27;* -- which documents a host death `#194` REMOVED, not a live one.  `cpu_mips_instr.c` has a second comment, prose about `cpu-&gt;running = 0`.  Lookalikes that a careless grep counts: `abortdyntrans` (an ARM dyntrans helper), `abort_dump:` (an m88k `rte` label), and &#x27;abort execution&#x27; in prose. *** AND I REPORTED &#x27;1&#x27; FOR THIS EARLIER TODAY BY GREPPING `abort(` AND COUNTING A LINE OF PROSE.  Same correlate-keying failure, mine, in the same session that keeps finding it. *** Denominator: every `.c`/`.h` under `src/cpus/`, including the `#include`d `.c` files and the generated `tmp_*`.  Also checked and absent: `raise(`, `SIGABRT`, `__builtin_trap` (the `raise` hits are `ppc_fpscr_raise` and `*_interrupt_assert`). Related but NOT this class: `cpu_dyntrans.c`&#x27;s two `assert()`s on page-aligned translation inserts, which can only abort with `NDEBUG` off -- and this tree builds with it on -- and which pin a HOST invariant rather than a guest opcode. **NO MAPLE-STYLE UNBOUNDED INSTRUCTION-PATH LOOP EXISTS in `src/cpus/`** either.  The one shared &#x27;I do not know this instruction&#x27; halt is `cpu_dyntrans.c`&#x27;s `bad` label, which is included into every dyntrans CPU -- so it is one site with many faces rather than many sites.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — [NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  No panel was ever convened on this stage.  Two entries (grok, opus5) recorded 2026-09-18 from the unread-seat sweep, neither citing a panel dir.  `cpusexitsweep` is a census row that seats raise while reviewing other `exit(` work; the remarks are about it, not a panel on it.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** TRIAGE OF THE MOTIVATING FILE: FIVE LIVE SITES, ALL FIVE GUEST-DRIVABLE. ***  :138 (a1, GETENV id), :149 (a0, PROM call number), :189 (palcode called in userMode), :443 (a1, wrent kentry selector), :500 (palcode, top-level dispatch default).  a0-a3 come straight from cpu-&gt;cd.alpha.r[] and palcode is an instruction field, so each is reachable by a guest setting a register or executing call_pal.  *** THE CENSUS NEEDED A FOURTH REFINEMENT: `#if 0&#x27; REGIONS ARE NOT COMPILED. ***  :474 (PAL_bugchk), :478 (PAL_OSF1_syscall) and :487 (KENTRY not set) sit inside #if 0...#endif -- controlled by reading the boundaries at :472/:480.  Tree-wide the progression is 53 raw -&gt; 41 code -&gt; **38 LIVE** for running=0, and 111 -&gt; 73 -&gt; **73** for exit( (no #if 0 effect there).  ALL THREE #if 0 LOSSES ARE IN THIS FILE.  *** AND THE FILE&#x27;S OWN COMMENT NAMES FOUR, NOT FIVE -- it omits :500, the top-level PALcode default, which meets an unrecognised selector in exactly the sense its sentence describes.  Whether that was a deliberate scoping of &#x27;selector&#x27; to the PAL-call ARGUMENTS or an omission is NOT established, and this claims only what was counted -- but a reader trusting &#x27;four&#x27; misses a live guest-drivable halt, and that comment was the only record of the class until today. ***  WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: that any of the five is a BUG (halting on an unimplemented PAL code may be right; DELETE is one of seven options), that the other 33 live sites are reachable (untriaged; cpu_arm.c alone has 16), or any severity ranking. — evidence: regress/artifacts/cpusexitsweep/TRIAGE_palcode_20260917.md; src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c :138 :149 :189 :443 :500 (live) and :472-480, :484-495 (the #if 0 regions holding :474 :478 :487)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s CENSUS IS SPELLING-KEYED AND CANNOT SEE A THIRD OF ITS OWN POPULATION. The concealment is THREE-LAYERED, and I verified all three myself. ***  **(1) `running = 0` vs `running = false`.**  Measured in `src/cpus/`: **`= 0` gives 54 hits across 11 files; `= false` gives 20 hits across 10 files.**  A `= 0`-keyed census misses every one of the 20 -- and the files it misses include `cpu_dyntrans.c`, `cpu_mips_instr_loadstore.c`, `cpu_ppc_instr_loadstore.c` and `cpu_sh_instr.c`, which is **four files the sweeping seat&#x27;s own table did not list either**.  The `= false` population is the m88k / i960 / RISC-V / MIPS-reboot family. **(2) `ABORT_EXECUTION` IS A MACRO, so neither spelling appears at the USE sites.**  It is defined three times -- `cpu_i960_instr.c:45`, `cpu_m88k_instr.c:45`, `cpu_sh_instr.c:45` -- and every invocation is a bare `ABORT_EXECUTION`.  A grep for either assignment spelling finds the three definitions and none of the uses. **(3) COMMENTS AND `#if 0` INFLATE BOTH SPELLINGS**, so the raw hit counts above are an upper bound, not the live population -- the seat stripped them programmatically and found, for example, that `cpu_dyntrans.c` has ONE live `= 0` (the `bad` label) with the other two commented out, and that `cpu_m88k_instr_loadstore.c`&#x27;s alignment halt sits in the `#else` of an `#if 1`. **THIS IS THE SESSION&#x27;S DOMINANT FAILURE FAMILY ARRIVING IN A SHIPPED CENSUS ROW:** the deriver measured a CORRELATE -- one way of spelling the assignment -- rather than the thing, which is *does this code path stop the emulated CPU*.  The carrier already states the rule (*&#x27;before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member&#x27;*) and `staticlatchscope` is the recorded precedent where three name-keyed censuses of one class all came back wrong. **THE FIX IS A RE-DERIVATION, NOT A SWEEP.**  `cpusexitsweep`&#x27;s number is owed a re-run keyed on the three spellings together, with comments and `#if 0` stripped and macro uses expanded. No code changes; the row&#x27;s count is the deliverable. — evidence: regress/artifacts/cpusweep_20260917/SWEEP_grok_20260917.txt; src/cpus/ spelling census</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">seatauditgap</span><span class="ttl">seat-coverage audit</span><span class="tip">seat-coverage audit</span></span><span class="state st-in work">in work</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE GATE IS BUILT AND CONTROLLED; IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED YET. ***  Owner chose INSTALL/RETROACTIVE over the dated-cutoff form.  Built to that decision, with the rejected reasoning recorded IN THE FILE rather than quietly softened -- the carrier&#x27;s finding that a retro-failing rule gets switched off is the COST of the decision, not a veto on it.  TWO NEGATIVE CONTROLS, both run: against the real ledger it EXITS 1 and names 34 rows; with the gaps synthetically closed it EXITS 0 and prints FIX_SEATS_PASS.  Ledger restored and the restore verified BY CONTENT (0 synthetic entries remaining, 488 rows), not by the backup&#x27;s existence.  *** A CLOBBER WAS AVERTED IN THE BUILD: the parked draft&#x27;s docstring named it `check_seat_coverage.py&#x27;, which ALREADY EXISTS and does a different job (per-seat answers-on-disk vs ledger coverage).  Shipping under that name would have destroyed it -- the same incident this session already recorded once.  Renamed, and the destination was checked for existence before writing. ***  WHY IT IS NOT WIRED: retroactive means it refuses EVERY commit until all 34 reconcile, including the campaign&#x27;s own records.  It lands at the END of the campaign so it goes in GREEN.  *** AND THE GATE&#x27;S OUTPUT IS THE CAMPAIGN&#x27;S WORKLIST, which is the useful part: the six rows panelled today are already down to MISSING: fable5 alone, so one Fable adjudication closes six of the 34 at once. *** — evidence: tools/pipeline/check_fix_seats.py (BUILT, NOT WIRED into precommit_check.sh)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">armthumbabortkill</span><span class="ttl">cpu_arm.c: FIVE Thumb sites halt the emulator AFTER the abort is already delivered and T cleared -- the halt PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S ABORT HANDLER FROM RUNNING</span><span class="tip">cpu_arm.c: FIVE Thumb sites halt the emulator AFTER the abort is already delivered and T cleared -- the halt PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S ABORT HANDLER FROM RUNNING — witness: none -- documented-only.  ATTEMPT RECORDED: a rung-3 probe was specified in full (regress/artifacts/armthumbabortkill/WITNESSSPEC_grok_20260917.txt) and then condemned against the tree across 13 holes (HOLES_grok_20260917.txt), two of which are unfixable as specified -- the t ifetch row is vacuous by construction, and the TLB flush the ARM control needs is the same flush that makes site 1227 unreachable.  Reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is built for a higher-ranked defect. — note: 2026-10-07 pre-brief READING (Opus 5.5, read-only, nothing run; brief not written -- the seat&#x27;s response was classifier-stopped at the witness-design step): (1) the row&#x27;s own reopen condition (an MMU-on ARM vehicle) is NOT met by arm_msrmode_probe.py; owner asked whether to reopen anyway.  (2) on ARM a failing DEVICE read raises no exception (memory_rw.c&#x27;s exception arms are MIPS/M88K only), so a bare delete of running=0 with `return 0` would re-run the Thumb instruction forever with an unlatched fatal() -- argues for a conditional decline; unreachable on testarm (all seven devices return 1).  (3) candidate siblings, UNVERIFIED: the PC-relative site passes CACHE_INSTRUCTION -&gt; Prefetch Abort where Thumb LDR(3) is a Data Abort (ddi0100i.txt:23050-23051); Thumb pc bit 0 may make the saved abort return address 1 low; testarm&#x27;s default SA1110 is ARMv4 with no Thumb (seat&#x27;s recollection, unchecked) -- ANSWERED 2026-10-09 (Fable 5.1&#x27;s regress review of #562, finding 2, regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/regress_20261009/fable5_regress562.md): src/include/arm_cpu_types.h has NO Thumb capability field and cpu_dyntrans.c selects arm_cpu_interpret_thumb_SLOW on ARM_FLAG_T alone, as the armmmuvehicle record already says from agy&#x27;s research -- T alone selects the Thumb interpreter on any ARM CPU here.  Cite sites by grep anchor: #562 shifts cpu_arm.c by +42.  ||  filed 2026-09-17 from the grok seat&#x27;s site-by-site triage of cpu_arm.c (the cpusexitsweep research row).  **FIVE SITES, ONE DEFECT: 1227, 1557, 1600, 1629, 1653.**  *** IT IS A FUNCTIONAL DEFECT, NOT MERELY A HALT, WHICH IS WHY IT IS HIGH AND IS NOT PART OF THE CENSUS: on an MMU fault the abort is ALREADY DELIVERED and T is ALREADY CLEARED, so the `cpu-&gt;running = 0&#x27; that follows PREVENTS THE GUEST&#x27;S OWN ABORT HANDLER FROM EVER RUNNING.  A guest that would have recovered instead stops. ***  DISPOSITION: option-set member 1 DELETE -- match the ARM load/store path.  **KEEP the `return 0&#x27;; do NOT fall through to `pc += 2&#x27;.**  IN-TREE PRECEDENT: #312 said of the ARM-mode analogue that &#x27;halting was never right&#x27;.  SELECTOR: a guest-supplied instruction or address in arm_cpu_interpret_thumb_SLOW, which is the PRODUCTION Thumb path -- cpu_dyntrans.c calls it whenever ARM_FLAG_T is set and ignores its return value -- so these are reachable by any Thumb guest taking a memory fault.  NOT YET WITNESSED: no probe has been built.  This is a READING by a file-reading seat, and the round that takes it owes the rung-3 ATTEMPT the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires.  *** WITNESS DISPOSITION: DOCUMENTED-ONLY, WITH THE ATTEMPT RECORDED.  DO NOT BUILD `regress/arm_thumbabort_probe.py` FROM THE SPEC, AND DO NOT BUILD A PATCHED COPY IN THIS CAMPAIGN. ***  The lifecycle ask is satisfied in the form it was written for: a seat WAS asked to try a rung-3 probe, DID specify one, and then condemned its own spec against the tree.  So `none` here means &#x27;tried; the vehicle costs a new MMU-on ARM harness that this row does not outrank&#x27;, NOT &#x27;did not think to&#x27; -- which are different states and must not render the same. THIRTEEN HOLES, derived against source rather than restated.  **The two that end the question:** *** HOLE 3 -- THE `t ifetch` ROW (site 1227) IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION AND THE SPEC&#x27;S OWN SCORE IS THEREFORE A WRONG RECORD. ***  `X(bx)` sets T before `quick_pc_to_pointers_arm` short-circuits, so the BX itself does not translate the hole -- but the NEXT `run_instr` does: `arm_run_instr` calls the GENERIC `arm_pc_to_pointers` FIRST and checks T only afterwards, so the prefetch abort is taken with T already cleared and PC already at 0x0C, the ARM prefetch handler runs, and **site 1227 never executes**.  The row wants `SENT` (handler did not run); the handler DID run; so the row is GREEN on HEAD and GREEN post-fix.  It never reds -- and it inflates the spec&#x27;s claimed HEAD from 7/12 to 8/12.  A &#x27;Thumb ifetch&#x27; row built as specified is an ARM dyntrans prefetch-abort row wearing a Thumb name.  1227 is DISCLOSED, not rowed. *** HOLE 2 -- A GENUINE CONTRADICTION THE SPEC CANNOT SEE: the c8 flush that makes the `arm abt` control honest is the SAME flush that makes 1227 unreachable.  You cannot have both on one hole. ***  And the seat CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPECSET CLAIM here: stale VPH does NOT hide a Thumb abort (the five Thumb sites go through `memory_rw` -&gt; `translate_v2p` and never read `host_load`); it hides the ARM SIBLING CONTROL instead.  My own brief had stated this as a QUESTION rather than a fact, which is why no wrong record of mine needs correcting. THE REMAINING ELEVEN, kept because they are reusable if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built: (1) the TTB plant explodes three different ways -- 4096 `put w` of zeros onto already-zero anonymous mmap is 4096 pty round-trips against a 45 s wait, `put w` warms `host_load` because it does NOT use NO_EXCEPTIONS, and any `put w` after the M bit makes THE DEBUGGER take the abort; (4) the row set splits DATA {1600,1629,1653} / PREF {1557 only}, one probe, and PREF must NOT pin FAR/FSR because prefetch does not write them and a leftover FAR would lie; (5) `-T` is not host death, is IRRELEVANT at UNMAP=0x100000 under `-M 64`, and `-K` is redundant with `-V`; (6) LIVE must be a named constant, never &#x27;UNIQUE&#x27;s neighbour&#x27;, which plants next to the hole; (7) `mmu bit`/`ttb sent`/`thumb T` have NO observation channel as specified -- Thumb has no MRS; (8) rows 1-6 CANNOT share a session, because `arm abt` leaves the CPU in ABT32 at the handler -- three control processes, not one; (9) no `enc ok` for the planted Thumb halfwords, and a wrong halfword hits `:1808 default:` `running = 0` which LOOKS exactly like the abort defect on HEAD and false-reds POST; (10) the two vector `B` words are not in the spec (given: `0xEA0020FB` at 0x0C, `0xEA00213A` at 0x10); (11) after the M bit **testarm&#x27;s own LR stub is unmapped**, so `continue` or an oversized `step N` falls into it and takes a prefetch abort -- which is the POST colour of a PREF row; (12) flags `session()` is the wrong wrapper to copy even though a trailing dump IS legal for Thumb; (13) `return 0` is load-bearing -- DELETE only `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, because dropping the whole `if` body falls through to `cpu-&gt;pc += 2` on a PC `arm_exception` already set, making the vector 0x0E/0x12. **THE BILL IF BUILT AFTER REPAIRS: 7 sessions minimum, not 6, plus the first in-tree ARM MMU-on probe** -- no `regress/arm_*` probe enables the M bit today -- meaning CP15 c1/c2/c3/c8, a 1 MB section table, two abort handlers and architectural BX.  That is a round of its own spent on a FUNCTIONAL HALT (`cpu-&gt;running = 0` AFTER `arm_exception` already delivered and T already cleared; the abort handler never runs; the host does not die) while a PROCESS-DEATH row (`armmsrmodekill`) is unbuilt. **REOPEN CONDITION, NAMED SO THIS IS NOT A QUIET DROP:** reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built for a higher-ranked defect -- Thumb is then a RIDER on it, not its first customer. **THE HONEST COST, stated rather than glossed:** documented-only means the fix does not ship, so the five `cpu-&gt;running = 0` lines STAY.  The fix is known and is member 1 DELETE with `return 0` KEPT; it is unshippable under test-first without a witness. Seat&#x27;s own reservations, kept because they bound the answer: it would not claim without a run that 1557&#x27;s PC-relative load takes PREF rather than DATA on this binary, that `step N` under `-V` leaves DEST dumpable as SENT, or that `dump UNMAP` prints &#x27;(memory access failed)&#x27; rather than zeros a sloppy regex would read as UNIQUE.  ||  [2026-10-09, armmmuvehicle pass 1] Fable 5.1 MEASURED the vehicle shape on unmodified testarm with the MMU on: a Thumb LDR to an unmapped VA reaches a stop site (logs &#x27;could not load with immediate offset&#x27;, DEST stays poison, pc stuck at 0x10 with LR/SPSR already written) -- this row&#x27;s symptom; a Thumb BX to an unmapped VA goes through the generic pc_to_pointers prefetch abort and never reaches the Thumb fetch stop (the 1227 site is reachable only through a stale warm entry).  The return-address defects are filed as armthumbabtlr.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** WITNESS DISPOSITION: DOCUMENTED-ONLY, WITH THE ATTEMPT RECORDED.  DO NOT BUILD `regress/arm_thumbabort_probe.py` FROM THE SPEC, AND DO NOT BUILD A PATCHED COPY IN THIS CAMPAIGN. ***  The lifecycle ask is satisfied in the form it was written for: a seat WAS asked to try a rung-3 probe, DID specify one, and then condemned its own spec against the tree.  So `none` here means &#x27;tried; the vehicle costs a new MMU-on ARM harness that this row does not outrank&#x27;, NOT &#x27;did not think to&#x27; -- which are different states and must not render the same. THIRTEEN HOLES, derived against source rather than restated.  **The two that end the question:** *** HOLE 3 -- THE `t ifetch` ROW (site 1227) IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION AND THE SPEC&#x27;S OWN SCORE IS THEREFORE A WRONG RECORD. ***  `X(bx)` sets T before `quick_pc_to_pointers_arm` short-circuits, so the BX itself does not translate the hole -- but the NEXT `run_instr` does: `arm_run_instr` calls the GENERIC `arm_pc_to_pointers` FIRST and checks T only afterwards, so the prefetch abort is taken with T already cleared and PC already at 0x0C, the ARM prefetch handler runs, and **site 1227 never executes**.  The row wants `SENT` (handler did not run); the handler DID run; so the row is GREEN on HEAD and GREEN post-fix.  It never reds -- and it inflates the spec&#x27;s claimed HEAD from 7/12 to 8/12.  A &#x27;Thumb ifetch&#x27; row built as specified is an ARM dyntrans prefetch-abort row wearing a Thumb name.  1227 is DISCLOSED, not rowed. *** HOLE 2 -- A GENUINE CONTRADICTION THE SPEC CANNOT SEE: the c8 flush that makes the `arm abt` control honest is the SAME flush that makes 1227 unreachable.  You cannot have both on one hole. ***  And the seat CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPECSET CLAIM here: stale VPH does NOT hide a Thumb abort (the five Thumb sites go through `memory_rw` -&gt; `translate_v2p` and never read `host_load`); it hides the ARM SIBLING CONTROL instead.  My own brief had stated this as a QUESTION rather than a fact, which is why no wrong record of mine needs correcting. THE REMAINING ELEVEN, kept because they are reusable if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built: (1) the TTB plant explodes three different ways -- 4096 `put w` of zeros onto already-zero anonymous mmap is 4096 pty round-trips against a 45 s wait, `put w` warms `host_load` because it does NOT use NO_EXCEPTIONS, and any `put w` after the M bit makes THE DEBUGGER take the abort; (4) the row set splits DATA {1600,1629,1653} / PREF {1557 only}, one probe, and PREF must NOT pin FAR/FSR because prefetch does not write them and a leftover FAR would lie; (5) `-T` is not host death, is IRRELEVANT at UNMAP=0x100000 under `-M 64`, and `-K` is redundant with `-V`; (6) LIVE must be a named constant, never &#x27;UNIQUE&#x27;s neighbour&#x27;, which plants next to the hole; (7) `mmu bit`/`ttb sent`/`thumb T` have NO observation channel as specified -- Thumb has no MRS; (8) rows 1-6 CANNOT share a session, because `arm abt` leaves the CPU in ABT32 at the handler -- three control processes, not one; (9) no `enc ok` for the planted Thumb halfwords, and a wrong halfword hits `:1808 default:` `running = 0` which LOOKS exactly like the abort defect on HEAD and false-reds POST; (10) the two vector `B` words are not in the spec (given: `0xEA0020FB` at 0x0C, `0xEA00213A` at 0x10); (11) after the M bit **testarm&#x27;s own LR stub is unmapped**, so `continue` or an oversized `step N` falls into it and takes a prefetch abort -- which is the POST colour of a PREF row; (12) flags `session()` is the wrong wrapper to copy even though a trailing dump IS legal for Thumb; (13) `return 0` is load-bearing -- DELETE only `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, because dropping the whole `if` body falls through to `cpu-&gt;pc += 2` on a PC `arm_exception` already set, making the vector 0x0E/0x12. **THE BILL IF BUILT AFTER REPAIRS: 7 sessions minimum, not 6, plus the first in-tree ARM MMU-on probe** -- no `regress/arm_*` probe enables the M bit today -- meaning CP15 c1/c2/c3/c8, a 1 MB section table, two abort handlers and architectural BX.  That is a round of its own spent on a FUNCTIONAL HALT (`cpu-&gt;running = 0` AFTER `arm_exception` already delivered and T already cleared; the abort handler never runs; the host does not die) while a PROCESS-DEATH row (`armmsrmodekill`) is unbuilt. **REOPEN CONDITION, NAMED SO THIS IS NOT A QUIET DROP:** reopen if an MMU-on ARM vehicle is ever built for a higher-ranked defect -- Thumb is then a RIDER on it, not its first customer. **THE HONEST COST, stated rather than glossed:** documented-only means the fix does not ship, so the five `cpu-&gt;running = 0` lines STAY.  The fix is known and is member 1 DELETE with `return 0` KEPT; it is unshippable under test-first without a witness. Seat&#x27;s own reservations, kept because they bound the answer: it would not claim without a run that 1557&#x27;s PC-relative load takes PREF rather than DATA on this binary, that `step N` under `-V` leaves DEST dumpable as SENT, or that `dump UNMAP` prints &#x27;(memory access failed)&#x27; rather than zeros a sloppy regex would read as UNIQUE. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armthumbabortkill/HOLES_grok_20260917.txt, HOLES_brief_20260917.md, HOLES_grok_20260917.STOPPED_partial.txt; src/cpus/cpu_dyntrans.c:177-262</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CROSS-SPEC REVIEW OF ALL THREE WITNESS SPECS AS A SET -- none had been reviewed against the others, each was written in isolation.  VERDICT: DO NOT BUILD THE THUMB SPEC AS WRITTEN (&#x27;the TTB plant is a session bomb&#x27;).  BUILD ORDER: MSR first, then Alpha with one added stop, then Thumb. ***  IT CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER SPEC TWICE MORE.  (1) THE `printed()&#x27; INSTRUCTION WAS FALSE: it had told me to shun arm_bfx_width_probe.py because &#x27;the debugger prints r0 = 0x...&#x27;.  debugger_parse_expression returns PARSE_NUMBER, so `print &lt;reg&gt;&#x27; emits a BARE 0x... line; `reg&#x27; is the command with the prefix.  A builder who &#x27;fixed&#x27; the regex would score EVERY print as ABSENT -- and arm_flags_probe.py has NO printed() at all, so the advice was unfollowable.  Copy verbatim from lca_isalane_probe.py:397-399.  (2) THE MSR FAKE-GREEN MECHANISM WAS THE WRONG ONE: already-USR32 gives m=10 alive, which reds honestly (wrong-reason FAIL), NOT a kill-row green.  The real fake-green is encoding-nop / c-bit-off / MSR-never-stepped -&gt; still SVC32, alive -&gt; GREEN, because that IS the post-fix decline appearance.  Guarding USR32 would have guarded the path that reds.  NEW BUILD TRAP: DO NOT COPY arm_flags_probe.py&#x27;s session() wrapper -- it dumps ONCE AT THE END, and this guest calls exit(1), so the arming word must be dumped BETWEEN step 2 and the killing step.  That is also why MSR is built FIRST: it is the only one of the three that forces the mid-session dump, so Thumb inherits proven machinery.  ALPHA GAP: lcaisaphys states its stop in PROSE and emits no CONTROL=DEAD / RESULT=0/0, so a failed swpctx yields ~10/13 with defect rows GREEN that a RESULT grep will not refuse -- copy the ARM token.  ONE GUARD FOR ALL THREE: a positive un-fakeable observable that the bad path is ARMED, on the SAME PROCESS that issues the stimulus, before any defect row is read; red there is SETUP-FAILED.  Do NOT share an MMU-arming prologue between the ARM probes; share only pty/wait/send/sw()/-A.  `-T&#x27; is a silent coupler (turns a physical miss into host death); `-K&#x27; is redundant with `-V&#x27;.  All of this is in regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/SPEC_AMENDMENTS_READ_BEFORE_BUILDING.md, beside the spec rather than in this row, per the #490 lesson. — evidence: regress/artifacts/witnessspecset/CROSSREVIEW_grok_20260917.txt and CROSSREVIEW_brief_20260917.md; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/SPEC_AMENDMENTS_READ_BEFORE_BUILDING.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-in work"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromacceptdmanum</span><span class="ttl">gdrom_default_probe.py: `accept dma&#x27; tests for the WORD &quot;Transfering&quot; and not the COUNT, so reverting #462&#x27;s data_len fix at a FIXED SITE is invisible to all 62 rows -- vacuity floor clause (b)</span><span class="tip">gdrom_default_probe.py: `accept dma&#x27; tests for the WORD &quot;Transfering&quot; and not the COUNT, so reverting #462&#x27;s data_len fix at a FIXED SITE is invisible to all 62 rows -- vacuity floor clause (b) — witness: detector:regress/gdrom_default_probe.py -- the `accept dma&#x27; row.  *** THE TIGHTENED ORACLE IS UNCOMMITTED: HEAD still carries the loose substring.  The three-leg measurement is preserved in regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/acceptdma_20260917/ and the row closes when the probe is committed. *** — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the Fable late-review seat&#x27;s Q3 floor pass.  L2 by the letter of clause (b).  One-line repair named and DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED: the preserved artifact cannot verify it, because a probe log holds row output rather than the guest transcript, so the tightened literal must be proven against a RUN first.  Second clause-(b)-family gap in this detector in one day -- the class-closure amendment is engaged if a third arrives.  *** CLAUSE (c) ADJUDICATED AND DISCHARGED 2026-09-17.  NOT L2.  THE REPAIR MAY SHIP FROM THIS FLOOR QUESTION. ***  I had filed, in my own checkpoint, that this probe &quot;has no EXPECT_ROWS identity row -- clause (c) gap&quot;.  I measured that FALSE while writing the brief (the probe carries `live`, `devsig` and a `scan uniq` pinned by EQUALITY at 56), said so in the brief, and asked the sharper question instead -- and the seat then refuted TWO of the three carriers I had named.  **`live` is a RAM round-trip and `devsig` is the SH4 IPRD reset value, not a GDROM signature; neither discharges (c) against an emulator whose GDROM is gone.  The carriers that do the GDROM work are `impl ok` (CNTHI store/readback -- the row this file ALREADY added after measuring a value-only device row vacuous) and `scan uniq`.**  The project&#x27;s own CHANGELOG had already ruled `live` &quot;is a liveness control and CANNOT DO MORE&quot;, so my list repeated a mislabel the record had corrected once. **THE MEASURED ANSWER, per medium:** garbage BIN -&gt; every session dies, `live` wants 0x12345678 and gets &quot;died&quot;, `GDROM_DEF_CONTROL=DEAD`, result 0/62 or no `_RESULT=` line at all; comment-only product source -&gt; does not COMPILE (the `Mc_null` form); GDROM removed or emptied -&gt; `impl ok` and `scan uniq` red, DMA rows red with them.  **Not green on any medium clause (c) has ever been scored on.** **Q3, THE GATE PIN:** it is neither sufficient nor necessary, and the honest statement is both halves.  Not sufficient -- kill tables grep `GDROM_DEF_RESULT=n/n` without asserting `n==62`, and a gate can `gate_skip`.  Not necessary -- the probe fails a null medium on its OWN rows.  **And this SECTION does not skip a missing result: `gate_sh_rounding.sh:518` is `check &quot;gdrom_default probe completed&quot; &quot;no&quot; &quot;yes&quot;`, a FAIL.**  Verified here.  The 62-name list and the equality pin are detector-hygiene on the battery path, doing for this probe what `EXPECT_ROWS` does for the `check_min` family -- they are not the (c) discharge. **Q2&#x27;s DENOMINATOR CUT AGAINST MY WORRY:** `EXPECT_ROWS` is in 13 of 99 `regress/*.py` and the 13 are a CLUSTER -- the SH-4 family (gate uses `check_min`, so the probe needs its own stale-copy guard; `sh4_chcr_probe.py` says exactly that in its own words and caught 29-vs-27 where `check_min` would have passed) plus the NO-GUEST source-grep probes where `live`/`devsig` cannot exist.  Runtime-guest siblings carry none and all shipped above the floor. **NO REPAIR OWED, and the tempting one is refused with a reason:** a NEW identity row makes the score 63/63 against an EQUALITY pin at 62 and reds the gate unless the pin moves in the same commit -- do not manufacture that as a floor repair.  A hygiene self-pin (`if len(rows) != 62: n = 0` before the `_RESULT=` print) is available, moves no row, and is NOT owed. **ONE REAL SHAPE THE SEAT NAMED AND DID NOT DISMISS:** a STALE detector -- 58 rows, all green, reporting `58/58` -- WOULD look clean standalone.  That is not the medium clause (c) is scored on and is not what L2 is for; it is what the gate&#x27;s equality pin and the 62-name list exist to catch, which is why they are hygiene rather than redundancy.  Residuals filed as `gdromacceptdmadebt`.  *** RE-OPENED 2026-09-17: THIS ROW WAS `closed` ON A WITNESS CLAIM THAT IS TRUE OF NO COMMITTED TREE, AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THE PANEL ABOUT IT STATED THE OPPOSITE PREMISE. ***  Measured by the Opus 5 seat and reproduced here:   HEAD (9c91999)  `row(&quot;accept dma&quot;, &quot;yes&quot; if (&quot;Transfering&quot; in t and alive) ...` -- the LOOSE substring, i.e. the vacuous oracle this row exists to replace;   working tree     the TIGHTENED oracle, with the three-leg measurement in its own comments. So the repair **was applied** -- my brief asserted it was *&quot;NAMED AND DELIBERATELY NOT APPLIED&quot;*, which is false -- and it is sitting UNCOMMITTED.  The `witness` field&#x27;s claim (*&quot;oracle tightened from substring presence to the COUNT&quot;*) describes the working tree only. **WHY THIS MAKES THE DISPOSITION WORSE RATHER THAN BETTER, in the seat&#x27;s own framing:** a floor clause-(b) breach is **L2 -- repaired IN-ROUND** -- and an uncommitted repair has not shipped.  A `git checkout` or `stash` loses it with nothing saying so, and the ledger has already recorded the obligation as discharged. **THE DISCHARGE IS CHEAP AND OWES NO NEW MEASUREMENT.**  The three legs are preserved and the seat read them: base with the tightened oracle `accept dma ok`; the `M_lenrev` mutant with the tightened oracle `SOLE RED`; the same mutant with the SHIPPED oracle `ok` -- **that third leg is the escape, and it is a real negative control with abort-guards in both directions.**  COMMIT THE PROBE AND THE ROW CLOSES. *The brief asked &quot;can a closure rest on a repair that was named and not applied?&quot;  The question was moot; the live one is whether a closure can rest on a repair that exists only in an uncommitted working tree, and the answer is no.  **A premise stated as fact in a brief came back refuted with a `git show` -- the third trial of the premise-audit opener and its third catch.**  This is also the CHECK THE SEAT RECOMMENDS TO EVERY FUTURE BATCH: for each closed row, is the thing the witness names present at HEAD, or only in your working tree?*  *** NINE-ROW CLOSURE PANEL, 2026-09-17 -- AND IT CONVERGED ONCE SEAT CLASS WAS RESPECTED, WHICH IS THE DOCTRINE WORKING RATHER THAN A DISAGREEMENT. ***  Seven seats answered; the kimi CLI hit its weekly wall and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute COVERED the seat (not lost). **THE SPLIT IS BY SEAT CLASS, NOT BY OPINION.**  The four FILE-READING seats (opus5, grok, agy, codex) each verified against HEAD and the preserved artifacts, and reached **CLOSE on rows 1-8, KEEP-OPEN on row 9**.  The four PACKET-FED seats kept rows open where the packet could not show them a witness -- and glm stated that standard explicitly: *&quot;I CLOSE where the packet is internally coherent and the witness shape matches the recorded defect; I KEEP-OPEN only where the packet&quot;* cannot.  **deepseek kept all nine open &quot;on packet-only review&quot;, which is an honest not-checkable and is NOT a vote on repo fact** -- exactly the distinction PIPELINE.md draws. *** ROW 9 IS UNANIMOUS KEEP-OPEN ACROSS EVERY SEAT THAT GAVE A PER-ROW VERDICT, AND THE ROW WAS RE-OPENED. ***  Two file-reading seats reached it by DIFFERENT EVIDENCE: opus5 diffed the working tree against HEAD and found the tightened oracle uncommitted; grok checked the `commits` field and the file&#x27;s last-commit history.  codex reached it a third way -- *&quot;row 9&#x27;s &#x27;not applied&#x27; and &#x27;unproven&#x27; descriptions are stale locally&quot;* -- and characterised the disposition precisely: *&quot;an oracle repair of its own, rather than an OVERTAKEN disposition; its remaining issue is landing the validated work.&quot;* *** THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN PREMISE ABOUT ROW 9 WAS FALSE AND TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY MEASURED IT SO. ***  It asserted the repair was *&quot;named and deliberately not applied&quot;*; it was applied and is uncommitted.  **Third trial of the premise-audit opener, third catch.** **ROW 5&#x27;s LABEL DEFECT WAS FOUND BY TWO FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY** -- opus5 and codex both report OVERTAKEN condition (2) unmet, codex putting it flatly: *&quot;&#x27;#481&#x27;s rows&#x27; is insufficient&quot;*.  Corrected in the witness field. *** AND deepseek&#x27;s HYPOTHETICAL MUTANT FOR ROW 9 IS WHAT HEAD ACTUALLY CONTAINS. ***  It proposed, as the smallest edit that breaks the fix and still passes: *&quot;edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green.&quot;*  **That is the committed state.**  A packet-fed seat with no file access described the real defect as a thought experiment -- which is the strongest possible corroboration of the two file-reading seats, precisely because it could not have been copied from the tree. **CODEX ALSO ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH FIVE IN-TREE IDIOMS THE BRIEF DID NOT NAME**, including *&quot;combine page base with a masked virtual offset, as the MIPS translator does&quot;* (`memory_mips_v2p.c`) -- the option-set rule working as designed, by grepping the fork rather than reasoning.  Its per-row coverage predictions are recorded as PREDICTIONS, which it said plainly: *&quot;these are coverage predictions, not newly run mutants.&quot;*  *** F5 WITHDRAWN 2026-09-17: deepseek&#x27;s &#x27;hypothetical mutant&#x27; IS NOT CORROBORATION, and the Fable seat is right that my own rule scores it at ZERO. ***  I recorded that deepseek proposing *&quot;edit the oracle back to substring `Transfering`; all 62 rows stay green&quot;* was striking because it described the real committed state without file access. **The brief handed it every element**: that the old oracle was the word `Transfering`, that the repair tightens it to the COUNT, and (falsely) that the repair was not applied.  A packet-fed seat answering the smallest-edit question from those three sentences produces that mutant BY CONSTRUCTION. *&quot;It could not have been copied from the tree&quot; is true and IRRELEVANT: it was copied from the packet.*  The standing rule -- score agreement only where a seat re-derived something the brief did NOT give it -- scores this at zero.  **The evidence for the breach is leg 3 of the preserved three-leg measurement (`probe_M_lenrev_SHIPPED.raw`, 62/62, `accept dma ok`), not deepseek.** *I found the coincidence striking and wrote it up as corroboration without asking what the packet had already told the seat -- which is the unanimity-is-worth-nothing rule landing on me, one level up.*  *** L4 -- I RECORDED THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN HEADINGS AS CODEX&#x27;S ANSWER ON ALL NINE ROWS.  Found by the Fable seat, corrected 2026-09-17. ***  My extraction matched the first line containing the row id and a word from `CLOSE|KEEP-OPEN|NOT CHECKABLE` -- and **`CLOSE` matched the word `closed` inside the brief&#x27;s own heading**, which codex echoes.  So nine cells read `### N. &lt;id&gt; (SEV, closed) -- &lt;title&gt;`: the packet, handed back as the seat&#x27;s verdict. *** IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNREAD REVIEW AND FABLE SAID WHY: &quot;an unread review filed under a RECORDED seat -- section G cannot see it because an entry exists.&quot; ***  The blank that would have been caught was filled with something that looks like an answer. **AND MY OWN AUDIT OF IT UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* Codex&#x27;s real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY &#x27;SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS&#x27; ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET&#x27;S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  My own brief&#x27;s S0 told the seats *&quot;an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction&quot;* -- which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, *discharged by naming the file* -- row 2&#x27;s condition (c), row 8&#x27;s figures, deepseek&#x27;s rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *&quot;the file-reading seats verified it&quot; is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5&#x27;s arithmetic, row 5&#x27;s unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** glm&#x27;s *&quot;nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire&quot;* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** **THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex&#x27;s row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into &quot;the file-reading seats said CLOSE&quot;.</span></span><span class="state st-in work">in work</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **KEEP-OPEN** (already `in work`).  `HEAD` is `&quot;Transfering&quot; in t`; the working tree is `&quot;Transfering 408 bytes&quot;`; `commits: []`; and **the artifact directory is UNTRACKED** -- *&quot;durable in location only&quot;*, and it must ship in the same commit as the probe. — evidence: fable adjudication 2026-09-17; regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/head_20260917/fable_probe_at_HEAD_20260917.out</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **KEEP-OPEN -- and it refuted the brief&#x27;s premise to get there.**  See the re-opening note above: the repair WAS applied and is UNCOMMITTED, so the witness claim is true of no committed tree. — evidence: _scratchpad/batch_closures_20260917.md; opus5 answer 2026-09-17; verified against HEAD</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **VERDICT: CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9 as recorded.  Row 9&#x27;s repair is applied and proven in the working tree, but has not shipped in committed HEAD.**  **KEEP-OPEN.** *&quot;The measured repair exists locally, but committed HEAD retains the word-only oracle and the supporting artifact directory&quot;* is untracked. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/codex.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. **`gdromacceptdmanum`**: **KEEP-OPEN** — The brief asserts that a closure can rest on an unapplied repair; but at git `HEAD`, the tightened oracle is **NOT committed** (it exists only as an uncommitted working tree modification in [`regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1579`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/regress/gdrom_default_probe.py#L1579) and [`ledger.json:40672`](file:///C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json#L40672) lists `&quot;commits&quot;: []`), leaving `HEAD` in breach of vacuity floor clause (b). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/agy.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** | 9 | `gdromacceptdmanum` | **KEEP-OPEN** | Floor breach is *measured*. Closure is not: `commits` is empty, last commit that touched `gdrom_default_probe.py` is `#490`, HEAD still has the substring oracle. | — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/grok.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. **`gdromacceptdmanum`: KEEP-OPEN.** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_kimi.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. **gdromacceptdmanum — KEEP-OPEN.** READ: the packet states both that the oracle &quot;was tightened from substring presence to the COUNT&quot; and that &quot;the tightened literal&quot; was named and deliberately not applied. Both cannot be fully informative as written, and the one-line repair is never identified. Note the inversion the author may have missed: row 9 is the **only** one of rows 2/5/9 that satisfies all three S3 conditions (measured three-leg control, carrying row named, durable artifacts path) — its exposure is the applied/not-applied tension, not the OVERTAKEN conditions. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_glm.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** 9. `gdromacceptdmanum` — KEEP-OPEN. READ: closure explicitly admits repair named and not applied; cannot be sound. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_deepseek.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — **Its own per-row line:** | 9 gdromacceptdmanum | KEEP-OPEN | Repair named, not applied; closure rests on oracle tightening alone | — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_201727/ollama_minimax.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-in work"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CLAUSE (b) BREACH MEASURED AND REPAIRED.  THREE LEGS. ***  leg1 TIGHTENED oracle + base = 62/62 `accept dma&#x27; ok; leg2 TIGHTENED + M_lenrev = 61/62 SOLE RED; *** leg3 SHIPPED oracle + M_lenrev = 62/62, `accept dma&#x27; GREEN -- THE TOTAL ESCAPE. ***  M_lenrev is one edit on a unique anchor: `(int) d-&gt;data_len, dst, length);&#x27; -&gt; `length, dst, length);&#x27;, a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the pre-#462 defect at a FIXED SITE, which is exactly what clause (b) requires a detector to fail on.  WHY LEG 3 EXISTS: legs 1-2 prove the REPAIR WORKS and do NOT prove the SHIPPED oracle MISSES the mutant -- that was an inference from `&quot;Transfering&quot; in t&#x27; being substring-insensitive.  Clause (b) is a claim about the SHIPPED detector, so without leg 3 this was BELIEVED wearing CONFIRMED&#x27;s clothes.  Leg 3 carries positive controls BOTH ways: it aborts if the frozen shipped probe lacks the untightened oracle AND aborts if it already carries the tightened one (which would look like a clean run while measuring nothing).  WHY THE TIGHTENED ORACLE LIVED IN A FROZEN COPY UNTIL LEG 1 RAN: the literal had to be proven against the REAL transcript first; a wrong literal would have reddened the gate with no obvious cause.  Oracle-correction order run FORWARDS.  *** AND THE ARTIFACT COULD NOT HAVE SETTLED IT: probe_base.raw holds ROW OUTPUT, not the guest transcript -- grep for `Transfering [0-9]* bytes&#x27; returns ZERO on it while `accept dma&#x27; reads ok, because the tested string lives in the probe&#x27;s internal `t&#x27;.  A preserved probe log answers what the ROWS scored, never what the GUEST printed. ***  REPAIR IS ORACLE-ONLY: row count unchanged at 62, every row NAME identical (diffed against the pre-edit copy), so the gate pin stays 62 and the named-row list needs no edit.  DISCLOSED, NOT CLAIMED: the literal pins 408 because that is what THIS session&#x27;s staging produces; a future round changing the staged size must RE-DERIVE it rather than assume it. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/acceptdma_20260917/VERDICT.md and logs/; sentinels KILLACCEPTDMA_DONE 16:50:19 and LEG3_DONE 17:07:48</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">untrackeddetectors</span><span class="ttl">regress/crime_width_probe.py and regress/lca_isalane_probe.py are UNTRACKED -- two shipped, measured detectors exist in no commit, and the checker that said so was reporting it as &#x27;no such file&#x27;</span><span class="tip">regress/crime_width_probe.py and regress/lca_isalane_probe.py are UNTRACKED -- two shipped, measured detectors exist in no commit, and the checker that said so was reporting it as &#x27;no such file&#x27; — note: crime_width_probe.py is the instrument behind today&#x27;s #529 ctl0c discharge (202/204); lca_isalane_probe.py is the 352-row #530 detector and the file the lcaisaphys witness spec copies its helpers from.  A fresh clone has the measurements and not the instruments.  check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s domain() is `git ls-files&#x27; (COMMITTED-OR-STAGED, per its own docstring) while its GHOST message said &#x27;regress/ has no such file&#x27; -- a wrong record that discredited a true finding; corrected the same day.  150 GHOST rows: 148 per-round artifact copies, exactly 2 top-level detectors.  *** CORRECTED SAME HOUR: the exposure is PROSPECTIVE, not present.  The COMMITTED gate_arm.sh has ZERO references to crime_width_probe (git show HEAD:regress/gate_arm.sh | grep -c) -- only the MODIFIED working copy invokes it at :1658.  A fresh clone today is consistent.  THE REAL FINDING IS A COMMIT-ORDERING CONSTRAINT: gate_arm.sh and crime_width_probe.py MUST be staged in the SAME commit, or the commit itself creates a battery invoking a missing file.  lca_isalane_probe.py is in no gate and carries no such constraint.  Neither probe has an EXPECT_ROWS identity row (floor clause (c)); both have a _DONE sentinel and a _RESULT= line.  I wrote &#x27;a fresh clone has the measurements and not the instruments&#x27; from the working tree without asking git what HEAD contains.  *** RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-18, AND THE EXPOSURE IS WIDER THAN THE ROW SAID. ***  `git ls-files --error-unmatch&#x27; confirms BOTH detectors are untracked: regress/crime_width_probe.py (119,216 bytes) and regress/lca_isalane_probe.py (99,545 bytes) -- 214 KB of SHIPPED, MEASURED detector code that exists in no commit.  AND THE BROADER FIGURE: `git status --porcelain regress/&#x27; reports **149 untracked paths** under regress/, which now includes EVERY #531 artifact -- all three killtable dirs, all three PRETABLE blocks, the opus seat&#x27;s preserved logs, and the frozen 18-row instrument.  A working-tree loss today destroys the two detectors AND the entire evidence base for #531&#x27;s floor discharge.  *** NOT RESOLVED THIS CYCLE, AND THE REASON IS CONTENTION, NOT OVERSIGHT: *** the fix is a COMMIT (staging protects nothing), a commit owes its targeted gate, and a gate is CPU-heavy while an opus5 measuring seat is in flight.  Queued for the moment that seat lands.  Constraints carried: stage BY NAME, never `git add -A&#x27;; regress/*.bin (alpha_dummy.bin, arm_bfx_stub.bin) and regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py MUST NOT be staged; #529 and #530 commit SEPARATELY; LOCAL GIT ONLY -- DO NOT PUSH.  *** RESOLVED IN SUBSTANCE 2026-09-18: 150 GHOST paths STAGED BY NAME, and the ghost count went 150 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 0. ***  Both detectors (crime_width_probe.py 119,216 b; lca_isalane_probe.py 99,545 b) are now visible to `git ls-files&#x27;.  Staged with an explicit cross-check that NOTHING on the do-not-commit list was touched -- verified zero before and after (ppc_rldicl_probe.py, regress/*.bin, gdromdefault_461, disk.img, commit518, the tools/ carrier copies).  *** AND THE LAST THREE COULD NOT BE CLEARED BY STAGING OR BY COMMITTING, BECAUSE THEY WERE A CHECK THAT CANNOT PASS. ***  See `wiringshghost&#x27;.  STILL OWED: the COMMIT itself.  Staging makes the files visible to the checkers and re-enables the checks the untracked state had switched off, but it does NOT protect against working-tree loss -- only a commit does.  Blocked on the remaining hard gate failures and on #531&#x27;s close (fable outstanding).  *** MEASURED AGAIN 2026-09-21 BY THE PEER SESSION AND RE-VERIFIED HERE INDEPENDENTLY: THE STAGED STATE DID NOT SURVIVE, AND THE HEADLINE ABOVE IS THEREFORE A WRONG RECORD. ***  `git ls-files --error-unmatch&#x27;: regress/crime_width_probe.py UNTRACKED, regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py UNTRACKED, regress/lca_isalane_probe.py TRACKED.  So ONE of the two original detectors was resolved the only way that works -- a COMMIT (a11eaac) -- and the other was not.  `git log -- regress/crime_width_probe.py&#x27; returns NOTHING, so that file has never been in any commit; the peer believes their `git reset -q&#x27; of 2026-09-19 06:1x dropped the staged paths, and I cannot separate that from `never committed at all&#x27; -- recorded as UNSETTLED cause, same remedy either way.  THE ROW&#x27;S OWN LAST LINE WAS RIGHT AND ITS HEADLINE WAS NOT: `staging makes the files visible ... but it does NOT protect against working-tree loss -- only a commit does&#x27;.  A row whose headline says RESOLVED IN SUBSTANCE and whose tail says STILL OWED is read by its headline.  *** AND THE SCOPE IS TWO FILES, NOT 147: *** `git status --porcelain regress/&#x27; reports 147 untracked paths today, of which 145 are ARTIFACTS directories belonging to rounds in flight (56 crimewidthkill, 16 lcaisaphys, 8 alphavmkill, 8 wdcaltdigin, 6 ppcrlwhighhalf, 3 alphaifetchkill, ...).  EXACTLY TWO are probes.  NEITHER IS MINE TO STAGE: crime_width_probe.py belongs to #529, which my standing constraints forbid me to touch, and arm_msrmode_probe.py belongs to `armmsrmodekill&#x27;, an open HIGH nobody has started.  They land with their own rounds&#x27; commits.  The row&#x27;s TITLE is also half stale -- it names lca_isalane_probe.py, which is now tracked; the live pair is crime_width_probe.py and arm_msrmode_probe.py.  OWNER QUESTION OPEN from both sessions: commit them, exclude them from the hygiene census, or leave the census counting them.  *** AND THE OBJECT DB SETTLES THE CAUSE, 2026-09-21 -- raised by the peer session, RE-MEASURED HERE, and it produced a fact neither of us had. ***  Staging writes a blob that a reset does not remove, so `git fsck --unreachable&#x27; is the witness: **18 unreachable blobs whose first line names crime_width_probe.py** (the peer counted 13 on a narrower pattern), with loose-object mtimes clustering at 2026-09-19 03:43:07 and one at 09-14 -- so that file WAS staged, repeatedly.  The peer&#x27;s `git reset -q&#x27; ran ~06:1x the same morning, just before 7836071, and they record the loss as theirs with the honest caveat that a later reset by anyone would look identical.  **arm_msrmode_probe.py has ZERO matching blobs: never staged by either session**, which matches `git log&#x27; returning nothing for it.  *** THE SHARPER FACT, AND IT STRENGTHENS THE ROW: the file&#x27;s CURRENT content is not in the object DB EITHER. *** `git hash-object regress/crime_width_probe.py&#x27; gives 97ab2415c2e3a9915273a731027b1e6182d7f9b7 and that hash appears among ZERO unreachable blobs -- the staged copies are OLDER revisions.  So a working-tree loss today destroys content that exists in no commit AND in no dangling blob: &#x27;staged once&#x27; is not a backup.  THE DISCRIMINATOR IS REUSABLE and belongs in the toolkit for any ghost-file question: `git hash-object &lt;path&gt;&#x27; for what is on disk now, `git fsck --unreachable&#x27; plus `git cat-file -p&#x27; for what was ever staged, and the loose object&#x27;s mtime for WHEN.  It separates &#x27;never staged&#x27; from &#x27;staged and lost&#x27; from &#x27;staged, lost, and since edited&#x27;, which no `git log&#x27; or `git status&#x27; can do.  *** PROTECTED WITHOUT COMMITTING, 2026-09-21, AND THE PEER&#x27;S NET HAD A GAP I MEASURED AND CLOSED. ***  The peer anchored three objects behind refs so gc cannot prune them: two BLOB refs holding the current content of each probe (a ref can point straight at a blob, which is what makes this work for an untracked file) and a `git stash create&#x27; commit for tracked modifications.  VERIFIED HERE INDEPENDENTLY: HEAD still f685d75, index empty, and refs/snapshots/crime_width_probe_20260921 holds exactly 97ab2415 -- the working tree&#x27;s current content.  **THE GAP: `git stash create&#x27; covers TRACKED FILES ONLY.**  Its tree has 11,121 files and my modified ledger.json is in it, but every UNTRACKED path is absent -- which is 145 artifact directories, i.e. the entire evidence base for #542&#x27;s and #541&#x27;s kill tables.  Closed by taking a second snapshot through a TEMPORARY INDEX (`GIT_INDEX_FILE=... git read-tree HEAD; git add -A; write-tree; commit-tree&#x27;), which leaves the real index and the working tree untouched: `refs/snapshots/tree_including_untracked_e8a2bc_20260921&#x27; -&gt; ff7ab93, containing PRETABLE2.out, PRETABLE5.out and both probes.  Controls printed before and after: HEAD unchanged, 0 staged, `git status --porcelain&#x27; 184 entries both times.  **THESE ARE BACKUPS, NOT COMMITS**: no branch, nothing reachable from HEAD, the do-not-commit list still governs real commits, each is one `update-ref -d&#x27; to remove, and each is a POINT-IN-TIME copy -- anyone editing these files later must re-take it.  The row stays OPEN: the only durable resolution is still a commit, and the owner question is still unanswered.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-18, AND THE EXPOSURE IS WIDER THAN THE ROW SAID. ***  `git ls-files --error-unmatch&#x27; confirms BOTH detectors are untracked: regress/crime_width_probe.py (119,216 bytes) and regress/lca_isalane_probe.py (99,545 bytes) -- 214 KB of SHIPPED, MEASURED detector code that exists in no commit.  AND THE BROADER FIGURE: `git status --porcelain regress/&#x27; reports **149 untracked paths** under regress/, which now includes EVERY #531 artifact -- all three killtable dirs, all three PRETABLE blocks, the opus seat&#x27;s preserved logs, and the frozen 18-row instrument.  A working-tree loss today destroys the two detectors AND the entire evidence base for #531&#x27;s floor discharge.  *** NOT RESOLVED THIS CYCLE, AND THE REASON IS CONTENTION, NOT OVERSIGHT: *** the fix is a COMMIT (staging protects nothing), a commit owes its targeted gate, and a gate is CPU-heavy while an opus5 measuring seat is in flight.  Queued for the moment that seat lands.  Constraints carried: stage BY NAME, never `git add -A&#x27;; regress/*.bin (alpha_dummy.bin, arm_bfx_stub.bin) and regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py MUST NOT be staged; #529 and #530 commit SEPARATELY; LOCAL GIT ONLY -- DO NOT PUSH. — evidence: regress/crime_width_probe.py; regress/lca_isalane_probe.py; git status --porcelain</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — The filed sentence overstated the defect.  Present state is consistent; the hazard is created BY THE NEXT COMMIT if the gate ships without the probe.  Filed as an ordering constraint, and carried into CHECKPOINT.md&#x27;s staging list. — evidence: git show HEAD:regress/gate_arm.sh (0 hits); regress/gate_arm.sh:1658 (working tree)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-18 — CONFIRMED — *** RESOLVED IN SUBSTANCE 2026-09-18: 150 GHOST paths STAGED BY NAME, and the ghost count went 150 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 0. ***  Both detectors (crime_width_probe.py 119,216 b; lca_isalane_probe.py 99,545 b) are now visible to `git ls-files&#x27;.  Staged with an explicit cross-check that NOTHING on the do-not-commit list was touched -- verified zero before and after (ppc_rldicl_probe.py, regress/*.bin, gdromdefault_461, disk.img, commit518, the tools/ carrier copies).  *** AND THE LAST THREE COULD NOT BE CLEARED BY STAGING OR BY COMMITTING, BECAUSE THEY WERE A CHECK THAT CANNOT PASS. ***  See `wiringshghost&#x27;.  STILL OWED: the COMMIT itself.  Staging makes the files visible to the checkers and re-enables the checks the untracked state had switched off, but it does NOT protect against working-tree loss -- only a commit does.  Blocked on the remaining hard gate failures and on #531&#x27;s close (fable outstanding). — evidence: scratchpad/precommit_20260918.out; git ls-files; PROBEWIRING_PASS</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">adjudglmtag</span><span class="ttl">adjudicate.sh&#x27;s seat-check loop kept the superseded glm-5.3-flash tag after the fire loop migrated to glm-5.3, so a LIVE seat was reported as NO ANSWER and its 8.7 KB answer was nearly discarded</span><span class="tip">adjudicate.sh&#x27;s seat-check loop kept the superseded glm-5.3-flash tag after the fire loop migrated to glm-5.3, so a LIVE seat was reported as NO ANSWER and its 8.7 KB answer was nearly discarded — note: *** A LIVE SEAT WAS REPORTED MISSING, AND THIS FILE&#x27;S OWN COMMENT PREDICTED IT IN THOSE WORDS. ***  `adjudicate.sh&#x27; fires its cloud seats in one loop and CHECKS them in a second, and the second must carry the ${m%%:*} SHORT FORM of the first because that is how the output filenames are built.  The 2026-08-29 migration glm-5.3-flash -&gt; glm-5.3 changed the FIRE loop and not the CHECK loop.  So the run wrote glm-5.3.txt while the check hunted glm-5.3-flash.txt, and it printed `glm-5.3-flash: NO ANSWER (0b)&#x27; and `ADJUDICATORS: 2/3&#x27;.  *** glm HAD ANSWERED WITH 8,693 BYTES, FOUR DEFECTS AND THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT FINDING IN THE ADJUDICATION -- an oracle that would have certified a live defect GREEN. ***  The comment immediately above the broken line reads: &#x27;Updating the fire loop alone would leave this one looking for glm-5.2.txt, which the run never writes -- so a LIVE seat would be reported as a missing one.  That is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape arriving through a rename rather than through a wall.&#x27;  The structure knew; the edit did not.  FIXED 2026-09-17: both loops now agree on glm-5.3.  Note the fix is a RETYPE and therefore has the same failure mode next migration -- deriving the check list from the fire list with ${m%%:*} is the structural form and is NOT done here, because it changes a script every adjudication runs and owes its own negative controls.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — The seat-check loop kept the superseded `glm-5.3-flash` tag after the fire loop migrated to `glm-5.3` on 2026-08-29, so the run wrote `glm-5.3.txt` while the check hunted `glm-5.3-flash.txt`.  A LIVE seat was reported `NO ANSWER (0b)` and its 8.7 KB answer -- carrying the adjudication&#x27;s single most important finding -- was nearly discarded.  The comment directly above the broken line had predicted this exact failure in those words.  FIXED by making both loops agree; the fix is a RETYPE with the same failure mode at the next migration, and the structural form (derive the check list from the fire list with ${m%%:*}) is filed, not done. — evidence: tools/adjudicate.sh fire loop vs seat-check loop; regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/ADJUDICATION_glm_20260917.txt (8,693 bytes, the answer that was reported missing)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">manifestscope</span><span class="ttl">check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s MANIFEST governs the EVIDENCE ARCHIVE as well as live instruments, so 148 artifact ghosts drown the 2 real findings -- and 3 keys are GHOST by construction because domain() only lists *.py</span><span class="tip">check_probe_wiring.py&#x27;s MANIFEST governs the EVIDENCE ARCHIVE as well as live instruments, so 148 artifact ghosts drown the 2 real findings -- and 3 keys are GHOST by construction because domain() only lists *.py — note: *** TWO JOBS GLUED TOGETHER: a WIRING check (detectors/witnesses) and a CENSUS (every *.py domain() returns).  The 148 artifact ghosts are the CENSUS firing on files the WIRING job does not care about, and they hid the 2 real findings. ***  Measured: 692 MANIFEST keys, 582 under artifacts/, 110 top-level; 150 GHOST of which 148 artifacts/ and 2 top-level.  *** THREE KEYS ARE GHOST BY CONSTRUCTION AND CAN NEVER PASS: laneP.sh, laneQ.sh, mkfreeze.sh -- domain() is `git ls-files -- *.py&#x27; and a .sh can never be in that set. ***  MY PROPOSED FIX (scope to top-level) WAS REFUTED BY ONE MEASURED FACT: exactly one artifacts/**.py IS gate-executed -- gate_sh_rounding.sh:1029 GPNPROBE=artifacts/gdrompionull/gdrom_pio_null_probe.py, a live CLASS_DETECTOR whose absence guard is `gate_skip&#x27; (silent coverage loss scored as a SKIP), and #471&#x27;s M13/M14/M15 exist so that path cannot go invisible.  Flattening re-opens #465.  Option (b) is ALREADY SHIPPED as CLASS_RESEARCH and did not help, because GHOST tests TRACKEDNESS not class.  Option (c) would make 148 immortal exemptions, i.e. a switched-off check.  ADOPT (a&#x27;): domain() excludes artifacts/ as gate_hygiene.sh already does, EXCEPT gate-invoked paths.  NOT BUILT -- it changes a checker every commit runs, owes two negative controls (prove the exception fires; prove a frozen copy is not demanded), and ships as its own HARNESS ROUND.  Same round owes the docstring fix: it still claims &#x27;a few dozen files in ONE FLAT DIRECTORY&#x27; while domain() has been recursive since #471.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** TWO JOBS GLUED TOGETHER: a WIRING check (detectors/witnesses) and a CENSUS (every *.py domain() returns).  The 148 artifact ghosts are the CENSUS firing on files the WIRING job does not care about, and they hid the 2 real findings. ***  Measured: 692 MANIFEST keys, 582 under artifacts/, 110 top-level; 150 GHOST of which 148 artifacts/ and 2 top-level.  *** THREE KEYS ARE GHOST BY CONSTRUCTION AND CAN NEVER PASS: laneP.sh, laneQ.sh, mkfreeze.sh -- domain() is `git ls-files -- *.py&#x27; and a .sh can never be in that set. ***  MY PROPOSED FIX (scope to top-level) WAS REFUTED BY ONE MEASURED FACT: exactly one artifacts/**.py IS gate-executed -- gate_sh_rounding.sh:1029 GPNPROBE=artifacts/gdrompionull/gdrom_pio_null_probe.py, a live CLASS_DETECTOR whose absence guard is `gate_skip&#x27; (silent coverage loss scored as a SKIP), and #471&#x27;s M13/M14/M15 exist so that path cannot go invisible.  Flattening re-opens #465.  Option (b) is ALREADY SHIPPED as CLASS_RESEARCH and did not help, because GHOST tests TRACKEDNESS not class.  Option (c) would make 148 immortal exemptions, i.e. a switched-off check.  ADOPT (a&#x27;): domain() excludes artifacts/ as gate_hygiene.sh already does, EXCEPT gate-invoked paths.  NOT BUILT -- it changes a checker every commit runs, owes two negative controls (prove the exception fires; prove a frozen copy is not demanded), and ships as its own HARNESS ROUND.  Same round owes the docstring fix: it still claims &#x27;a few dozen files in ONE FLAT DIRECTORY&#x27; while domain() has been recursive since #471. — evidence: regress/artifacts/probewiring/MANIFESTSCOPE_grok_20260917.txt and MANIFESTSCOPE_brief_20260917.md; check_probe_wiring.py; regress/gate_sh_rounding.sh:1029</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mvme187memcoob</span><span class="ttl">dev_mvme187.c:68 indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest offset of up to 255 before the switch, so offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OOB read -- today masked by the default arm&#x27;s exit(1), which is the asicindexcase shape waiting for a de-fatalisation</span><span class="tip">dev_mvme187.c:68 indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest offset of up to 255 before the switch, so offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OOB read -- today masked by the default arm&#x27;s exit(1), which is the asicindexcase shape waiting for a de-fatalisation — note: `dev_mvme187.c:68` indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest-supplied offset of up to 255, **BEFORE the switch and unconditionally on every access including reads**:  `odata = ((uint8_t*)&amp;d-&gt;memcreg)[relative_addr];`  The device is registered with length **0x200** at `:127-129`, and `:63-66` folds the high half down (`if (relative_addr &amp; 0x100) { controller = 1; relative_addr &amp;= ~0x100; }`), so the index domain is 0..255.  **sizeof(struct memcreg) is 124** -- DERIVED LOCALLY by counting the 62 `volatile u_char` declarations in `src/include/thirdparty/mvme_memcreg.h` (31 registers, each followed by a 3-byte `xxN[3]` pad), which independently reproduces the hunting seat&#x27;s figure.  **So offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OUT-OF-BOUNDS READ past the struct**, into whatever follows it inside the malloc&#x27;d `struct mvme187_data`. REACHABILITY: `machine_mvme88k.c:88` `device_add(machine, &quot;mvme187&quot;)` -- note this is `machine_mvme88k.c`, NOT `machine_m88k.c`, which the hunting seat named loosely and where my first grep found nothing; I corrected it by locating the constructor rather than by trusting the citation. *** THE REASON THIS IS A RIDER AND NOT A SECOND KILL: the `default:` arm already `exit(1)`s, so TODAY THE OOB VALUE NEVER ESCAPES TO THE GUEST -- the read happens, the process dies, and the kill is already inside `exitunowned76`. ***  **The exposure is what a DE-FATALISATION of that arm would create**, and it is the `asicindexcase` shape exactly: the `exit(1)` is doing duty as the bounds check, so removing the kill without clamping the index converts a host death into a SILENT heap over-read served to the guest as register data.  A round that de-fatalises this file owes the clamp IN THE SAME DIFF -- a bound, not a `case`.  Filed MED so it is queued BEFORE the de-fatalisation round rather than discovered inside it.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — `dev_mvme187.c:68` indexes a 124-byte struct with a guest-supplied offset of up to 255, **BEFORE the switch and unconditionally on every access including reads**:  `odata = ((uint8_t*)&amp;d-&gt;memcreg)[relative_addr];`  The device is registered with length **0x200** at `:127-129`, and `:63-66` folds the high half down (`if (relative_addr &amp; 0x100) { controller = 1; relative_addr &amp;= ~0x100; }`), so the index domain is 0..255.  **sizeof(struct memcreg) is 124** -- DERIVED LOCALLY by counting the 62 `volatile u_char` declarations in `src/include/thirdparty/mvme_memcreg.h` (31 registers, each followed by a 3-byte `xxN[3]` pad), which independently reproduces the hunting seat&#x27;s figure.  **So offsets 124..255 are a 132-byte host OUT-OF-BOUNDS READ past the struct**, into whatever follows it inside the malloc&#x27;d `struct mvme187_data`. REACHABILITY: `machine_mvme88k.c:88` `device_add(machine, &quot;mvme187&quot;)` -- note this is `machine_mvme88k.c`, NOT `machine_m88k.c`, which the hunting seat named loosely and where my first grep found nothing; I corrected it by locating the constructor rather than by trusting the citation. *** THE REASON THIS IS A RIDER AND NOT A SECOND KILL: the `default:` arm already `exit(1)`s, so TODAY THE OOB VALUE NEVER ESCAPES TO THE GUEST -- the read happens, the process dies, and the kill is already inside `exitunowned76`. ***  **The exposure is what a DE-FATALISATION of that arm would create**, and it is the `asicindexcase` shape exactly: the `exit(1)` is doing duty as the bounds check, so removing the kill without clamping the index converts a host death into a SILENT heap over-read served to the guest as register data.  A round that de-fatalises this file owes the clamp IN THE SAME DIFF -- a bound, not a `case`.  Filed MED so it is queued BEFORE the de-fatalisation round rather than discovered inside it. — evidence: regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/EXITHUNT_grok_20260917.txt; src/devices/dev_mvme187.c:63-68,127-129; src/include/thirdparty/mvme_memcreg.h:31-; src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:88</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromacceptdmadebt</span><span class="ttl">the tightened `accept dma` oracle still cannot distinguish data_len from cnt or a literal 408, and its kill DEPENDS on the guest never writing the DMA length register -- plus `accept byte` still carries the old substring form</span><span class="tip">the tightened `accept dma` oracle still cannot distinguish data_len from cnt or a literal 408, and its kill DEPENDS on the guest never writing the DMA length register -- plus `accept byte` still carries the old substring form — note: *** COVERAGE DEBT ON THE CLAUSE-(b) REPAIR THAT SHIPPED 2026-09-17.  NOT A FLOOR BREACH -- the clause-(c) question was adjudicated the same day and came back DISCHARGED, and none of these re-opens clause (b) as measured. ***  The repair tightened `accept dma`&#x27;s oracle from SUBSTRING PRESENCE (`&quot;Transfering&quot; in t`) to COUNT-BEARING (`&quot;Transfering 408 bytes&quot; in t`), and three legs proved the breach was real (leg 3: SHIPPED oracle + M_lenrev = 62/62, the escape).  What the tightened form still cannot see, enumerated by the reviewing seat and verified here: **(1) `d-&gt;data_len` -&gt; `d-&gt;cnt` or a hardcoded `408`.**  On this staging those three are the SAME VALUE -- TOC `case 0x14` accepts only `d-&gt;cnt != 408` as the decline and `alloc_data()` does `d-&gt;data_len = d-&gt;cnt` -- so the mutant still prints `Transfering 408 bytes` and BOTH the old and new oracles stay green.  The oracle distinguishes that cluster from `length` (`dma_reg[+0x08]`), which this session never writes, and not from its own members. **(2) THE KILL DEPENDS ON LEAVING THE DMA LENGTH REGISTER UNWRITTEN, and that is the sharpest one.**  Write `+0x08 = 408` and `length == data_len`, so `M_lenrev` prints `Transfering 408 bytes` again and leg 2&#x27;s SOLE RED disappears.  The device comment&#x27;s own example is `0x3800`, so a guest that programs a length is ordinary, not exotic.  **The stronger session programs a DISTINCT length and pins BOTH halves of `Transfering %i bytes ... (guest asked for %i)`** -- that is the repair if this is ever bought, and it is a new session rather than an oracle edit. **(3) `accept byte` STILL USES THE SUBSTRING FORM.**  Verified at `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608`: `&quot;ran&quot; if &quot;Transfering&quot; in t else &quot;no&quot;`.  It is a DIFFERENT cell (the SUCCESS arm&#x27;s shadow-clear pair-oracle) and `accept dma` is the print site clause (b) was measured against, so this is not a (b) hole -- **but it is the old form left sitting beside the repair of it**, which is the shape a future reader mistakes for a decision. **(4) `nodata notx` counts `t.count(&quot;Transfering&quot;)`** -- harmless for a decline row wanting 0, and NOT a pin of the number. **(5) THE ROW STILL DOES NOT PROVE 408 BYTES MOVED.**  `accept data` (want `0x10` at the destination) remains the movement oracle; the tightened `accept dma` proves the LOG LINE carries the count.  The contract must not be read as claiming otherwise. DISPOSITION: FILED, not fixed.  The stopping rule takes in-round only a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD; the wrong record in this family was the `EXPECT_ROWS`/clause-(c) conflation and it was corrected in place the same day.  (1) and (2) are contract-honesty items -- they matter exactly if the probe header&#x27;s &quot;prints data_len&quot; bullet is read as &quot;UNIQUELY data_len&quot;, which it should not be.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** COVERAGE DEBT ON THE CLAUSE-(b) REPAIR THAT SHIPPED 2026-09-17.  NOT A FLOOR BREACH -- the clause-(c) question was adjudicated the same day and came back DISCHARGED, and none of these re-opens clause (b) as measured. ***  The repair tightened `accept dma`&#x27;s oracle from SUBSTRING PRESENCE (`&quot;Transfering&quot; in t`) to COUNT-BEARING (`&quot;Transfering 408 bytes&quot; in t`), and three legs proved the breach was real (leg 3: SHIPPED oracle + M_lenrev = 62/62, the escape).  What the tightened form still cannot see, enumerated by the reviewing seat and verified here: **(1) `d-&gt;data_len` -&gt; `d-&gt;cnt` or a hardcoded `408`.**  On this staging those three are the SAME VALUE -- TOC `case 0x14` accepts only `d-&gt;cnt != 408` as the decline and `alloc_data()` does `d-&gt;data_len = d-&gt;cnt` -- so the mutant still prints `Transfering 408 bytes` and BOTH the old and new oracles stay green.  The oracle distinguishes that cluster from `length` (`dma_reg[+0x08]`), which this session never writes, and not from its own members. **(2) THE KILL DEPENDS ON LEAVING THE DMA LENGTH REGISTER UNWRITTEN, and that is the sharpest one.**  Write `+0x08 = 408` and `length == data_len`, so `M_lenrev` prints `Transfering 408 bytes` again and leg 2&#x27;s SOLE RED disappears.  The device comment&#x27;s own example is `0x3800`, so a guest that programs a length is ordinary, not exotic.  **The stronger session programs a DISTINCT length and pins BOTH halves of `Transfering %i bytes ... (guest asked for %i)`** -- that is the repair if this is ever bought, and it is a new session rather than an oracle edit. **(3) `accept byte` STILL USES THE SUBSTRING FORM.**  Verified at `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608`: `&quot;ran&quot; if &quot;Transfering&quot; in t else &quot;no&quot;`.  It is a DIFFERENT cell (the SUCCESS arm&#x27;s shadow-clear pair-oracle) and `accept dma` is the print site clause (b) was measured against, so this is not a (b) hole -- **but it is the old form left sitting beside the repair of it**, which is the shape a future reader mistakes for a decision. **(4) `nodata notx` counts `t.count(&quot;Transfering&quot;)`** -- harmless for a decline row wanting 0, and NOT a pin of the number. **(5) THE ROW STILL DOES NOT PROVE 408 BYTES MOVED.**  `accept data` (want `0x10` at the destination) remains the movement oracle; the tightened `accept dma` proves the LOG LINE carries the count.  The contract must not be read as claiming otherwise. DISPOSITION: FILED, not fixed.  The stopping rule takes in-round only a MEASURED FALSE PASS or a WRONG RECORD; the wrong record in this family was the `EXPECT_ROWS`/clause-(c) conflation and it was corrected in place the same day.  (1) and (2) are contract-honesty items -- they matter exactly if the probe header&#x27;s &quot;prints data_len&quot; bullet is read as &quot;UNIQUELY data_len&quot;, which it should not be. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/FLOORC_grok_20260917.txt; regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:1608</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">8259cmdhalt</span><span class="ttl">dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep</span><span class="tip">dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep — note: `dev_8259.c` turns an unimplemented PIC command into a GUEST-VISIBLE HALT: the command-port arm does `fatal(&quot;[ 8259: unimplemented command 0x%02x ]&quot;)` and then `cpu-&gt;running = 0`, and **then still returns 1** -- so the access &#x27;succeeds&#x27; while the strand stops.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:140`, `:231`, `:235` (`:173` is inside a comment and is NOT a site). **DISTINCT FROM A HOST DEATH, and the ranking matters:** `cpu-&gt;running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT -- the process lives, the emulated CPU stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to BOTH an `exit(` sweep and an `abort()` sweep, which is why the class survived three censuses. REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED** -- the seat&#x27;s claim is that `DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2 so the `default:` arm is dead, and the live door is OFFSET 0, the command port, taking any OCW that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form -- i.e. `strb r0,[pic]` with r0 = 0 would be enough on CATS, where `bus_isa.c` installs it.  **NONE of that is re-verified here and it is explicitly not claimed.**  A census job is auditing it; this row exists so the finding cannot be lost again while that runs. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt&#x27; seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY &#x27;the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS&#x27; CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu-&gt;running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -&gt; `bus_pci_add(..., &quot;ali_m1543&quot;)` -&gt; `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  **`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in `[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the `default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY &#x27;the access still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS&#x27; CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu-&gt;running` around the handler and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -&gt; `bus_pci_add(..., &quot;ali_m1543&quot;)` -&gt; `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts. — evidence: src/devices/dev_8259.c:47,140,228-236,278; machine_cats.c -&gt; ali_m1543 -&gt; bus_isa_init</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lunafbhalt</span><span class="ttl">dev_lunafb.c halts the CPU from four sites on ordinary framebuffer offsets, on a machine this project actually boots -- and the file had never been queued at all</span><span class="tip">dev_lunafb.c halts the CPU from four sites on ordinary framebuffer offsets, on a machine this project actually boots -- and the file had never been queued at all — note: `dev_lunafb.c` halts the CPU on ordinary framebuffer offsets: the `lunafb_a` and `lunafb_b` `default:` arms and a ROP-not-all-ones write all do `cpu-&gt;running = 0`.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:93`, `:160`, `:166`, `:185`. *** THIS FILE HAD NEVER BEEN QUEUED -- `lunafb` had ZERO mentions in the entire ledger before this row. ***  It is installed unconditionally from `DEVINIT(luna88k)`, and luna88k is a machine this project HAS a rig for and DOES boot, which is what separates it from the ip30 case (advertised but no in-tree boot image). REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED**: the seat&#x27;s claim is that `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` and `BMAP_BMSEL`, so every other offset in that window stops the CPU, and that a comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH is wrong while the `#define`s are right.  It also claims OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` uses BMSEL, which IS implemented, so the boot path is BELIEVED clear of the halt.  **None of that is re-verified here.**  FAULT (member 5) is architecturally available on M88K and `return 0` would deliver it -- today the halt runs first. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt&#x27; seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW&#x27;S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on &#x27;a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS&#x27;, implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit-&gt;machine-&gt;x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt&#x27;s &#x27;CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k&#x27; is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`&#x27;s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW&#x27;S FRAMING WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on &#x27;a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS&#x27;, implying boot reachability.  **`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit-&gt;machine-&gt;x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt&#x27;s &#x27;CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k&#x27; is FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; **the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD&#x27;s `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing `ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`&#x27;s running-latch returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first. — evidence: src/devices/dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101; src/devices/dev_lunafb.c:93,160,166,185</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lunadefaultstop</span><span class="ttl">dev_luna88k.c:923 stops the CPU on any unimplemented address in a ~1.76 GB always-installed window -- named by seats three times since 2026-09-06 and never given a ledger id</span><span class="tip">dev_luna88k.c:923 stops the CPU on any unimplemented address in a ~1.76 GB always-installed window -- named by seats three times since 2026-09-06 and never given a ledger id — note: *** NAMED BY SEATS THREE TIMES AND NEVER QUEUED UNTIL NOW.  This is the records finding as much as the defect. ***  It was named `lunadefaultstop` on 2026-09-06 in the `lunaabsentcpu` pass-2/pass-3 panels with an explicit &#x27;file as a row&#x27;; the CHANGELOG still uses the name (**one** occurrence); the 2026-09-17 exithunt table named the same site with NO id; and the halt-class census named it again.  **Verified: `lunadefaultstop` was ABSENT from ledger.json and had ZERO occurrences in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.** THE DEFECT: `src/devices/dev_luna88k.c:923`, in the unimplemented-address arm -- `// Stop the emulation immediately.` then `cpu-&gt;running = 0; return 0;`.  **The device is ALWAYS INSTALLED** (no x11 conditional, unlike lunafb), and its window runs from 0x3ffffff0 to BMAP_START -- **roughly 1.76 GB**.  One supervisor store to any unimplemented offset in that range stops the CPU **on the machine this project actually boots**. **RANKED #1 of the guest-reachable halts** by the census, above `8259cmdhalt`, precisely because it is unconditional on a booted machine where `lunafbhalt` is X11-only.  Boot to `login:` is clear -- or the rig would not boot -- so the door is a probe, not the PROM path. CLASS: a FUNCTIONAL HALT, not a host death; the process lives and the strand stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent wrong value, and it is invisible to an `exit(` sweep AND to an `abort()` sweep, which is how the class survived three censuses.  *** MY &#x27;~1.76 GB WINDOW&#x27; IS THE MAP, NOT THE DEFECT, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE I USED THE BIG NUMBER AS THE SEVERITY ARGUMENT. ***  The window really is 1,897,267,216 bytes (`0x3ffffff0` .. `BMAP_START`), and the implemented mass inside it is about **148 KB** -- four range handlers (FUSE_ROM 1 KB, NVRAM 0x1FDC, NVRAM_ADDR_88K2 0x1FDC byte-only, TRI_PORT_RAM ~128 KB) plus **34 exact switch addresses**.  But the set that MATTERS is three nested sets, not one: **(1) COLD DEBUGGER, TRANSLATION OFF** -- `APR_V` clear means VA==PA, which is exactly why `luna_intmask_probe.py` already reaches `0x65000000`.  Almost the whole window kills.  **This is the WITNESS population.**  **(2) AFTER THE MMU, THE PAGES OpenBSD ACTUALLY MAPS THAT THIS FILE DOES NOT IMPLEMENT** -- and this is the finding my row did not have.  `pmap_table.c` identity-maps **RW** inside this device&#x27;s window: `OBIO_TAS 0x61000000` (4 KB, nothing implemented), `SOFT_INT_FLAG0 0x6B000000` (4 KB, nothing), `RESET_CPU0 0x6D000000` (4 KB, of which only `+0x10` is serviced), `PIO0C`, and **`PC_BASE 0x90000000` -- 32 MB, nothing implemented**.  So the guest-kernel-reachable unimplemented set is **~32 MB, and 32 MB of it is ONE mapping**.  **(3) THIS IMAGE&#x27;S LOGIN PATH: EMPTY**, as my row said. **So &#x27;the #1 guest-reachable halt on a machine we boot&#x27; is true of (1) and (2) as CAPABILITY and false of (3) as TRAVELLED PATH** -- which is the distinction the ranking rests on and my row blurred.  *`PROM_SPACE` is the sharpest illustration of map-versus-defect: 256 KB of PROM, **one** word implemented, and that one word IS on the boot path.* **FAULT (member 5) BECOMES MECHANICALLY AVAILABLE IF THE HALT GOES, AND IS STILL NOT RIGHT.** Today the halt wins because `#220`&#x27;s latch in `memory_rw.c` -- `if (running_before_device_access &amp;&amp; !cpu-&gt;running) return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;` -- fires BEFORE the `res == 0` -&gt; `m88k_exception(DATA_ACCESS)` block is ever reached.  Delete the halt, keep `return 0`, and `#502`&#x27;s implemented DATA_ACCESS vector does fire.  Four reasons from the TREE not the menu: **(a) this file already models &#x27;illegal address&#x27; and it is NOT `return 0`** -- `case 0x3ffffff0` pokes `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR` into the data CMMU and `break`s, i.e. the access SUCCEEDS, thirty lines above the defect; **(b) the failed-device arm never sets PFSR** while OpenBSD&#x27;s `trap.c` READS `CMMU_PFSR_FAULT(tf_dpfsr)` -- so a naked `return 0` is a HALF-FAULT, an exception without the bus-error status the guest inspects; **(c) the same CPU and the same class were already adjudicated the other way** -- `pcc2defaultkill` `#501` declined unimplemented offsets with a latched `(once)` and `return 1`, and `dev_m8820x.c` `#433` says it in product comments, complain-and-continue, because `#220` assigned HALT to &#x27;the guest asked the machine to halt&#x27; and CONTINUE to &#x27;model gap&#x27;; **(d) FAULT on a MAPPED hole is a kernel PANIC, not a clean probe** -- OpenBSD `T_DATAFLT` with no `pcb_onfault` goes to `panictrap`, and TAS / RESET_CPU0 / PC_BASE are all mapped.  A driver that stores without `badaddr()` dies. *** AND THE WARNING THAT REFRAMES THE WHOLE FIX: deleting the halt and leaving `return 0` would SILENTLY CHANGE EVERY UNIMPLEMENTED ACCESS from &#x27;stop the emulator&#x27; to &#x27;inject DATA_ACCESS&#x27;.  **That is a behaviour change to the guest, not a de-fatalisation** -- and it is precisely the kind of change the addition licence refuses without a measured delta. *** **THE SHIPPING TABLE (proposed, not ratified):** `RESET_CPU_ALL` **KEEPS THE HALT** -- OpenBSD `boot()` writes it to reboot, so the guest asked, which is `#220`/footbridge_reset; the `default:` arm gets **member 3**, latched announce plus **`break` and never an early `return`** because the common tail writes `odata` on reads; TAS / `RESET_CPU0..3` / `SOFT_INT_FLAG` / `PIO0C` are **member 7 FILED, not in-round**, being real registers whose implementation needs a measured delta or a transferred warrant; and FAULT stays **available-but-not-chosen** unless someone measures OpenBSD&#x27;s `badaddr()` against both a `return 0` and a `PFSR_BERROR` poke. *** `RESET_CPU_ALL` IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT MAKES THE SIBLING TEST WORK: it proves this file ALREADY distinguishes a deliberate halt from an accidental one, so the `default:` arm is not &#x27;how this file does things&#x27; -- it is the outlier. ***</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** MY &#x27;~1.76 GB WINDOW&#x27; IS THE MAP, NOT THE DEFECT, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE I USED THE BIG NUMBER AS THE SEVERITY ARGUMENT. ***  The window really is 1,897,267,216 bytes (`0x3ffffff0` .. `BMAP_START`), and the implemented mass inside it is about **148 KB** -- four range handlers (FUSE_ROM 1 KB, NVRAM 0x1FDC, NVRAM_ADDR_88K2 0x1FDC byte-only, TRI_PORT_RAM ~128 KB) plus **34 exact switch addresses**.  But the set that MATTERS is three nested sets, not one: **(1) COLD DEBUGGER, TRANSLATION OFF** -- `APR_V` clear means VA==PA, which is exactly why `luna_intmask_probe.py` already reaches `0x65000000`.  Almost the whole window kills.  **This is the WITNESS population.**  **(2) AFTER THE MMU, THE PAGES OpenBSD ACTUALLY MAPS THAT THIS FILE DOES NOT IMPLEMENT** -- and this is the finding my row did not have.  `pmap_table.c` identity-maps **RW** inside this device&#x27;s window: `OBIO_TAS 0x61000000` (4 KB, nothing implemented), `SOFT_INT_FLAG0 0x6B000000` (4 KB, nothing), `RESET_CPU0 0x6D000000` (4 KB, of which only `+0x10` is serviced), `PIO0C`, and **`PC_BASE 0x90000000` -- 32 MB, nothing implemented**.  So the guest-kernel-reachable unimplemented set is **~32 MB, and 32 MB of it is ONE mapping**.  **(3) THIS IMAGE&#x27;S LOGIN PATH: EMPTY**, as my row said. **So &#x27;the #1 guest-reachable halt on a machine we boot&#x27; is true of (1) and (2) as CAPABILITY and false of (3) as TRAVELLED PATH** -- which is the distinction the ranking rests on and my row blurred.  *`PROM_SPACE` is the sharpest illustration of map-versus-defect: 256 KB of PROM, **one** word implemented, and that one word IS on the boot path.* **FAULT (member 5) BECOMES MECHANICALLY AVAILABLE IF THE HALT GOES, AND IS STILL NOT RIGHT.** Today the halt wins because `#220`&#x27;s latch in `memory_rw.c` -- `if (running_before_device_access &amp;&amp; !cpu-&gt;running) return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;` -- fires BEFORE the `res == 0` -&gt; `m88k_exception(DATA_ACCESS)` block is ever reached.  Delete the halt, keep `return 0`, and `#502`&#x27;s implemented DATA_ACCESS vector does fire.  Four reasons from the TREE not the menu: **(a) this file already models &#x27;illegal address&#x27; and it is NOT `return 0`** -- `case 0x3ffffff0` pokes `CMMU_PFSR_BERROR` into the data CMMU and `break`s, i.e. the access SUCCEEDS, thirty lines above the defect; **(b) the failed-device arm never sets PFSR** while OpenBSD&#x27;s `trap.c` READS `CMMU_PFSR_FAULT(tf_dpfsr)` -- so a naked `return 0` is a HALF-FAULT, an exception without the bus-error status the guest inspects; **(c) the same CPU and the same class were already adjudicated the other way** -- `pcc2defaultkill` `#501` declined unimplemented offsets with a latched `(once)` and `return 1`, and `dev_m8820x.c` `#433` says it in product comments, complain-and-continue, because `#220` assigned HALT to &#x27;the guest asked the machine to halt&#x27; and CONTINUE to &#x27;model gap&#x27;; **(d) FAULT on a MAPPED hole is a kernel PANIC, not a clean probe** -- OpenBSD `T_DATAFLT` with no `pcb_onfault` goes to `panictrap`, and TAS / RESET_CPU0 / PC_BASE are all mapped.  A driver that stores without `badaddr()` dies. *** AND THE WARNING THAT REFRAMES THE WHOLE FIX: deleting the halt and leaving `return 0` would SILENTLY CHANGE EVERY UNIMPLEMENTED ACCESS from &#x27;stop the emulator&#x27; to &#x27;inject DATA_ACCESS&#x27;.  **That is a behaviour change to the guest, not a de-fatalisation** -- and it is precisely the kind of change the addition licence refuses without a measured delta. *** **THE SHIPPING TABLE (proposed, not ratified):** `RESET_CPU_ALL` **KEEPS THE HALT** -- OpenBSD `boot()` writes it to reboot, so the guest asked, which is `#220`/footbridge_reset; the `default:` arm gets **member 3**, latched announce plus **`break` and never an early `return`** because the common tail writes `odata` on reads; TAS / `RESET_CPU0..3` / `SOFT_INT_FLAG` / `PIO0C` are **member 7 FILED, not in-round**, being real registers whose implementation needs a measured delta or a transferred warrant; and FAULT stays **available-but-not-chosen** unless someone measures OpenBSD&#x27;s `badaddr()` against both a `return 0` and a `PFSR_BERROR` poke. *** `RESET_CPU_ALL` IS THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT MAKES THE SIBLING TEST WORK: it proves this file ALREADY distinguishes a deliberate halt from an accidental one, so the `default:` arm is not &#x27;how this file does things&#x27; -- it is the outlier. *** — evidence: regress/artifacts/lunadefaultstop_20260917/DESIGN_grok_20260917.txt; src/devices/dev_luna88k.c; OpenBSD pmap_table.c</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">lunaabsentmaskdecl</span><span class="ttl">named in the 2026-09-06 lunaabsentcpu &#x27;file as rows&#x27; list and never queued -- placeholder; substance owed</span><span class="tip">named in the 2026-09-06 lunaabsentcpu &#x27;file as rows&#x27; list and never queued -- placeholder; substance owed — note: Named alongside `lunadefaultstop` on 2026-09-06 in the same `lunaabsentcpu` &#x27;file as rows&#x27; list, and **never given a ledger id either** -- verified absent from both ledger.json and OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.  Filed as a PLACEHOLDER so the second half of that list cannot be lost the way the first half was; its substance is NOT re-derived here and is owed.  *The point of this row is the pattern, not the defect: two items from one &#x27;file as rows&#x27; instruction, eleven days old, neither queued.*</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — BELIEVED — Named alongside `lunadefaultstop` on 2026-09-06 in the same `lunaabsentcpu` &#x27;file as rows&#x27; list, and **never given a ledger id either** -- verified absent from both ledger.json and OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md.  Filed as a PLACEHOLDER so the second half of that list cannot be lost the way the first half was; its substance is NOT re-derived here and is owed.  *The point of this row is the pattern, not the defect: two items from one &#x27;file as rows&#x27; instruction, eleven days old, neither queued.* — evidence: 2026-09-06 lunaabsentcpu pass-2/pass-3 panels; ledger + bugfile absence verified</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mapledmahang</span><span class="ttl">maple_do_dma_xfer() is for(;;) with its only exit gated on a bit read from guest RAM, so a guest that never sets it hangs the emulator inside a register write -- alive, running, and never coming back</span><span class="tip">maple_do_dma_xfer() is for(;;) with its only exit gated on a bit read from guest RAM, so a guest that never sets it hangs the emulator inside a register write -- alive, running, and never coming back — note: *** AN UNBOUNDED, GUEST-TRIGGERABLE LOOP INSIDE A DEVICE ACCESS HANDLER.  THE EMULATOR NEVER DIES -- IT NEVER COMES BACK. ***  `maple_do_dma_xfer()` is `for (;;)` at `dev_dreamcast_maple.c:409`, and its ONLY exit is `:535` `if (last_message) break;` where `last_message = buf[3] &amp; 0x80` (`:429`) is read **from guest RAM** at `d-&gt;dmaaddr`.  A guest that never sets bit 7 of that byte loops forever; `:532` only advances `addr`.  Triggered by an ordinary register write -- `case 0x18 MAPLE_STATE`, `case 1: maple_do_dma_xfer(cpu, d)` at `:588` -- after MAPLE_ENABLE and MAPLE_DMAADDR.  Machine: **dreamcast**. **THIS IS A THIRD CLASS AND THE TREE HAS NO NAME FOR IT.**  An `exit(`/`abort()` is a HOST DEATH; `cpu-&gt;running = 0` is a FUNCTIONAL HALT where the process lives and the strand stops. **This is neither: the process is alive, the strand is running, and the user&#x27;s machine is gone.**  No sweep in this project looks for it, because there is no keyword -- which is exactly the property that let the `abort()` class survive three censuses. **NOT A DUPLICATE, verified:** `mapledmaaddr` is about the DMAADDR read value and `mapledmaadvance` about address advancement past the command.  Neither is the loop bound. There IS an `exit(1)` at `:426` inside the loop, on a different condition -- so the function has a terminating path and still has no trip cap. DISPOSITION NOT DECIDED HERE.  The obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop), but the bound is a design question: a legitimate maple chain has a real maximum and picking it needs the Comstedt reverse-engineering note the function header cites, not a guess.  **The addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred warrant, never a number that feels safe.**  *** THE BOUND IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THIS TREE, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN A FAILURE TO FIND ONE. ***  Comstedt&#x27;s own DMA paragraph -- the page this file&#x27;s header names -- says the controller *&#x27;will send ANY NUMBER of requests&#x27;*.  The &#x27;configurable amount of time&#x27; in that sentence is **per request**, and it is ALREADY in the model as `d-&gt;timeout` (MAPLE_SPEED bits 16-31), **stored and never read inside `maple_do_dma_xfer`**.  On hardware a timeout writes 0xFFFFFFFF at the result address and CONTINUES to the next descriptor -- it does not end the list.  **`d-&gt;timeout` therefore cannot be turned into a frame cap without inventing a meaning the register does not have.** **24 IS THE ENDPOINT CENSUS, NOT THE LIST CEILING** (4 ports x 6 units): a legal DMA may send DEVINFO *and* GETCOND to the same unit, or a burst of VMU BWRITEs.  Linux&#x27;s `MAPLE_MAXPACKETS 8` and the 16 KiB `MAPLE_DMA_SIZE` that Linux and KallistiOS both allocate are **SOFTWARE** batching, not ASIC limits, and neither is in this tree&#x27;s model. *** AND IT CORRECTED THE DISPOSITION I WROTE INTO THIS ROW WHEN I FILED IT. ***  I wrote *&#x27;the obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop)&#x27;*.  **The seven-member option set is the WRONG MENU here: every member presumes an ACCESS with a value to refuse, and this is a LIST WALK whose only exit is a guest bit.**  Bounding the walk is a NEW member -- *&#x27;do not dress it up as member 3 applied to a loop and then pick 24&#x27;*. **THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT, GREPPED RATHER THAN ARGUED, AND IT DOES NOT TRANSFER:** `dev_sgi_mec.c`&#x27;s `for (;;)` IS bounded -- by `dma_ptr_nr &gt;= 4` (the hardware has four DMA pointers) AND `j &gt;= MAX_TX_PACKET_LEN`, and `#203` added the outer cap after an inner `break` left the outer loop writing past the array.  **But that bound comes from the HOST STORAGE the fill writes into, and maple writes GUEST RAM at `receive_addr` -- there is no host buffer to cap against.**  *And that answers the sighunt&#x27;s open question: there is NO other `for (;;)` and no `while (1)` in `src/devices/*.c`; the sighunt&#x27;s second one is mec, and mec is bounded.* **THE DISPOSITION, WITHOUT INVENTING N:** detect the **MISSING LAST**, not &#x27;too many devices&#x27; -- the defect is a list that never terminates, not a list longer than 24.  **DECLINE THE WHOLE DMA**, which is the shape this same function already uses for a malformed DMA: the unaligned `addr &amp; 0x1f` check prints and RETURNS with no `SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT`.  *** TRUNCATING MID-LIST AND THEN FIRING DMADONE WOULD HAND THE GUEST A SILENT PARTIAL TRANSFER, and the unaligned path pointedly does not do that. ***  Latch the announce with `(once)` -- the unaligned path `fatal()`s EVERY time with no latch, which is floodclass.  **Do NOT `exit(1)`** (that converts a hang into a host death, the thing this queue is removing).  **Do NOT `cpu-&gt;running = 0`** (wrong class -- that is `lunafbhalt`/`8259cmdhalt`).  Do not make the DMA asynchronous in this round: that IS the hardware shape and would stop taking the host away, but it is a rewrite, and on real silicon a no-LAST list may still hang the Maple ASIC. **`:426` IS NOT A MODEL FOR MISSING LAST.**  All four `exit(1)`s in this function (`:426` GUN bit, `:459` multiple units, `:520` GETCOND function, `:529` unknown command) are UNIMPLEMENTED-PATH TRIPWIRES -- member 7&#x27;s tell, a stub with no predicate refusing a value. *** BWRITE IS THE TELL: it is `fatal` + `break` OF THE SWITCH, and then the loop CONTINUES. So a no-LAST list of BWRITE HANGS while a no-LAST list of an unimplemented command DIES -- the same loop, two different classes, decided by `cmd`. ***  The author used `exit(1)` for &#x27;I have not written this opcode&#x27;, not for &#x27;this list is malformed&#x27;. **Q4 -- IT CAN BE WITNESSED, AND AN EXISTING PROBE IS A LIVE HAZARD.**  Death-keyed oracles are all blind here: `exit(`, `abort()`, WIFSIGNALED and EOF-on-pty none of them fire.  *** AND `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py`&#x27;s `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` AFTER `rd(10.0)` WOULD BLOCK FOREVER ON THIS BUG -- that probe cannot be the detector, and if it ever reaches this path it hangs the harness rather than reporting anything. ***  The instrument is SIGKILL-after-deadline plus a LAST-set control; the enable and alignment checks both return before the loop, so they are the liveness controls. **WHAT WOULD SETTLE N:** measuring the real ASIC with lists of 24, 25 and 512 frames and one with LAST never set -- noting that on hardware Maple DMA is **ASYNCHRONOUS** (STATE=1 while in progress, the SH4 keeps running), so the question is what the ASIC does with no LAST, not what feels safe on the host.  **The synchronous modelling is itself part of why this presents as a host hang.**</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE BOUND IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THIS TREE, AND THAT IS THE ANSWER RATHER THAN A FAILURE TO FIND ONE. ***  Comstedt&#x27;s own DMA paragraph -- the page this file&#x27;s header names -- says the controller *&#x27;will send ANY NUMBER of requests&#x27;*.  The &#x27;configurable amount of time&#x27; in that sentence is **per request**, and it is ALREADY in the model as `d-&gt;timeout` (MAPLE_SPEED bits 16-31), **stored and never read inside `maple_do_dma_xfer`**.  On hardware a timeout writes 0xFFFFFFFF at the result address and CONTINUES to the next descriptor -- it does not end the list.  **`d-&gt;timeout` therefore cannot be turned into a frame cap without inventing a meaning the register does not have.** **24 IS THE ENDPOINT CENSUS, NOT THE LIST CEILING** (4 ports x 6 units): a legal DMA may send DEVINFO *and* GETCOND to the same unit, or a burst of VMU BWRITEs.  Linux&#x27;s `MAPLE_MAXPACKETS 8` and the 16 KiB `MAPLE_DMA_SIZE` that Linux and KallistiOS both allocate are **SOFTWARE** batching, not ASIC limits, and neither is in this tree&#x27;s model. *** AND IT CORRECTED THE DISPOSITION I WROTE INTO THIS ROW WHEN I FILED IT. ***  I wrote *&#x27;the obvious shape is a trip cap with a latched announce (member 3 applied to a loop)&#x27;*.  **The seven-member option set is the WRONG MENU here: every member presumes an ACCESS with a value to refuse, and this is a LIST WALK whose only exit is a guest bit.**  Bounding the walk is a NEW member -- *&#x27;do not dress it up as member 3 applied to a loop and then pick 24&#x27;*. **THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT, GREPPED RATHER THAN ARGUED, AND IT DOES NOT TRANSFER:** `dev_sgi_mec.c`&#x27;s `for (;;)` IS bounded -- by `dma_ptr_nr &gt;= 4` (the hardware has four DMA pointers) AND `j &gt;= MAX_TX_PACKET_LEN`, and `#203` added the outer cap after an inner `break` left the outer loop writing past the array.  **But that bound comes from the HOST STORAGE the fill writes into, and maple writes GUEST RAM at `receive_addr` -- there is no host buffer to cap against.**  *And that answers the sighunt&#x27;s open question: there is NO other `for (;;)` and no `while (1)` in `src/devices/*.c`; the sighunt&#x27;s second one is mec, and mec is bounded.* **THE DISPOSITION, WITHOUT INVENTING N:** detect the **MISSING LAST**, not &#x27;too many devices&#x27; -- the defect is a list that never terminates, not a list longer than 24.  **DECLINE THE WHOLE DMA**, which is the shape this same function already uses for a malformed DMA: the unaligned `addr &amp; 0x1f` check prints and RETURNS with no `SYSASIC_TRIGGER_EVENT`.  *** TRUNCATING MID-LIST AND THEN FIRING DMADONE WOULD HAND THE GUEST A SILENT PARTIAL TRANSFER, and the unaligned path pointedly does not do that. ***  Latch the announce with `(once)` -- the unaligned path `fatal()`s EVERY time with no latch, which is floodclass.  **Do NOT `exit(1)`** (that converts a hang into a host death, the thing this queue is removing).  **Do NOT `cpu-&gt;running = 0`** (wrong class -- that is `lunafbhalt`/`8259cmdhalt`).  Do not make the DMA asynchronous in this round: that IS the hardware shape and would stop taking the host away, but it is a rewrite, and on real silicon a no-LAST list may still hang the Maple ASIC. **`:426` IS NOT A MODEL FOR MISSING LAST.**  All four `exit(1)`s in this function (`:426` GUN bit, `:459` multiple units, `:520` GETCOND function, `:529` unknown command) are UNIMPLEMENTED-PATH TRIPWIRES -- member 7&#x27;s tell, a stub with no predicate refusing a value. *** BWRITE IS THE TELL: it is `fatal` + `break` OF THE SWITCH, and then the loop CONTINUES. So a no-LAST list of BWRITE HANGS while a no-LAST list of an unimplemented command DIES -- the same loop, two different classes, decided by `cmd`. ***  The author used `exit(1)` for &#x27;I have not written this opcode&#x27;, not for &#x27;this list is malformed&#x27;. **Q4 -- IT CAN BE WITNESSED, AND AN EXISTING PROBE IS A LIVE HAZARD.**  Death-keyed oracles are all blind here: `exit(`, `abort()`, WIFSIGNALED and EOF-on-pty none of them fire.  *** AND `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py`&#x27;s `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` AFTER `rd(10.0)` WOULD BLOCK FOREVER ON THIS BUG -- that probe cannot be the detector, and if it ever reaches this path it hangs the harness rather than reporting anything. ***  The instrument is SIGKILL-after-deadline plus a LAST-set control; the enable and alignment checks both return before the loop, so they are the liveness controls. **WHAT WOULD SETTLE N:** measuring the real ASIC with lists of 24, 25 and 512 frames and one with LAST never set -- noting that on hardware Maple DMA is **ASYNCHRONOUS** (STATE=1 while in progress, the SH4 keeps running), so the question is what the ASIC does with no LAST, not what feels safe on the host.  **The synchronous modelling is itself part of why this presents as a host hang.** — evidence: regress/artifacts/sighunt_20260917/MAPLEBOUND_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">guestloopdenial</span><span class="ttl">four guest-controlled loop bounds at denial scale across sgi/ip32, dreamcast and mvme88k -- bounded, but 16M-to-4GiB of host traffic inside a single guest access</span><span class="tip">four guest-controlled loop bounds at denial scale across sgi/ip32, dreamcast and mvme88k -- bounded, but 16M-to-4GiB of host traffic inside a single guest access — note: *** FOUR GUEST-CONTROLLED TRIP COUNTS LARGE ENOUGH TO DENY THE EMULATOR, ACROSS THREE MACHINES.  Bounded -- so not the `mapledmahang` class -- but bounded at denial scale. ***  Found by the signal-death hunt with an explicit denominator: `for (` 289, `while (` 60, `do {` 6, `for (;;)` 2 across `src/devices/*.c`. **(1) SGI MTE linear fill, `dev_sgi_re.c:1477`** -- `dstlen = dst1 - dst0 + 1` on uint32, so `dst1 &lt; dst0` UNDERFLOWS to ~2^32; the loop at `:1639-1677` subtracts at most 4096 per pass, giving ~2^20 passes of up to 4 KiB of `memory_rw` -- **roughly 4 GiB of traffic** from guest `mte_reg[]` values plus a CRIME_DE_START access.  Machine sgi/ip32. **(2) SGI MTE tiled fill/copy, `:1516-1574`** -- `for (y = y1; y != y2+dy; y += dy)` with 12-bit coordinates and no trip cap: **4096 x 4096 = 16,777,216** `horrible_getputpixel` calls, often two `memory_rw` each.  Same trigger. **(3) PVR / SH4 DMAC, `dev_pvr.c:171-204`** -- the hardware convention that TCR 0 means 16M is modelled faithfully (`:202-204`), so a guest programming DMAC ch2 with count 0 and writing PVR_MODE gets **16,777,216 transfers, one `memory_rw` each, inside one store**.  Machine dreamcast. **(4) osiop SCRIPTS MOVE, `dev_osiop.c:433`** -- `xfer_byte_count` is a 24-bit guest field, so ONE MOVE is up to **16,777,215** byte-at-a-time host accesses.  `MAX_SCRIPTS_PER_CHUNK` 256 caps how many MOVEs run per tick and does nothing about the size of one.  Machine mvme187/mvme88k. **WHY THIS IS ONE ROW AND NOT FOUR:** they are the same defect shape -- a guest-supplied count or coordinate pair used directly as a loop bound with no cap and no yield -- and the disposition is the same question for all four (cap, yield, or accept as faithful).  **(3) is the interesting one because the 16M IS the hardware contract**, so a cap there would be a deliberate divergence from the spec rather than a fix, and that is a different argument from the other three.  Filed as a class so the argument happens once.  *** THE HEAP-OVERFLOW CLASS IS CLOSED AS EMPTY, WITH ITS DENOMINATOR, AND THE SEAT REFUSED THE FINDING MY OWN BRIEF SUGGESTED. ***  I pointed it at `512 * count` on a 32-bit `int`, which overflows at count 8,388,608 and would give a SMALL allocation followed by a LARGE write -- a heap overflow, far worse than a denial.  **Measured: `count` is 1-256, so `512*256 = 131072` and the wrap is UNREACHABLE.**  Its own words: *&#x27;a manufactured WDC heap overflow would be a wrong record.&#x27;* **DENOMINATOR:** 130+ `malloc`/`calloc`/`realloc`/`zeroed_alloc` calls in `src/devices/` and ~15 in `src/cpus/` production code, the large majority `malloc(sizeof(struct ..._data))` at DEVINIT or init-time name buffers -- host/config, not guest.  **16 guest-derived sizes across 10 files**, every one traced to its writer.  Result: **0 overflow, 15 capped or field-width bounded, 1 unbounded growth** (the mp IPI queue, filed as `mpipirealloc`). Each cap named rather than asserted: le is a 12-bit field plus a 12-1900 reject and a `size_t` aggregate check; 21143 an 11-bit field with an `int` sum clamped to 65536; gdrom a 16-bit `cnt` / 24-bit sector count with an explicit 64 KiB cap before `alloc_data()`; ps2 `0xffff * 16`; pvr Z 10-bit geometry gated by the 8 MiB VRAM check; pvr TA a doubling allocator; and `dev_fb.c` carries `#156`&#x27;s `size_t` overflow check **before** the malloc.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE HEAP-OVERFLOW CLASS IS CLOSED AS EMPTY, WITH ITS DENOMINATOR, AND THE SEAT REFUSED THE FINDING MY OWN BRIEF SUGGESTED. ***  I pointed it at `512 * count` on a 32-bit `int`, which overflows at count 8,388,608 and would give a SMALL allocation followed by a LARGE write -- a heap overflow, far worse than a denial.  **Measured: `count` is 1-256, so `512*256 = 131072` and the wrap is UNREACHABLE.**  Its own words: *&#x27;a manufactured WDC heap overflow would be a wrong record.&#x27;* **DENOMINATOR:** 130+ `malloc`/`calloc`/`realloc`/`zeroed_alloc` calls in `src/devices/` and ~15 in `src/cpus/` production code, the large majority `malloc(sizeof(struct ..._data))` at DEVINIT or init-time name buffers -- host/config, not guest.  **16 guest-derived sizes across 10 files**, every one traced to its writer.  Result: **0 overflow, 15 capped or field-width bounded, 1 unbounded growth** (the mp IPI queue, filed as `mpipirealloc`). Each cap named rather than asserted: le is a 12-bit field plus a 12-1900 reject and a `size_t` aggregate check; 21143 an 11-bit field with an `int` sum clamped to 65536; gdrom a 16-bit `cnt` / 24-bit sector count with an explicit 64 KiB cap before `alloc_data()`; ps2 `0xffff * 16`; pvr Z 10-bit geometry gated by the 8 MiB VRAM check; pvr TA a doubling allocator; and `dev_fb.c` carries `#156`&#x27;s `size_t` overflow check **before** the malloc. — evidence: regress/artifacts/allocsize_20260917/HUNT_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">probeblockingwait</span><span class="ttl">maple_dmaaddr_probe.py uses a BLOCKING waitpid, so on the mapledmahang path it would hang the harness forever instead of reporting -- the mirror of the false-green class, and outside probedeathoracle&#x27;s WNOHANG-keyed census</span><span class="tip">maple_dmaaddr_probe.py uses a BLOCKING waitpid, so on the mapledmahang path it would hang the harness forever instead of reporting -- the mirror of the false-green class, and outside probedeathoracle&#x27;s WNOHANG-keyed census — note: *** `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py` CALLS `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` -- A BLOCKING WAIT -- AFTER `rd(10.0)`, AND `mapledmahang` IS A PATH ON WHICH THE EMULATOR NEVER EXITS. ***  If that probe ever drives a maple DMA list with no LAST bit, the blocking wait **never returns and the harness hangs**, reporting nothing at all.  **This is the mirror of the false-green class closed today as `probedeathoracle`.**  That one was about a probe reporting ALIVE for a dead process; this is a probe that cannot report anything because it waits forever on a process that will not die.  Both come from keying an oracle on process death without a deadline -- and `probedeathoracle`&#x27;s census keyed on `WNOHANG`, so a BLOCKING `waitpid` was outside its population by construction. **The remedy is the same shape as the one already applied: a DEADLINE.**  A blocking `waitpid` in a harness that can drive a non-terminating guest path needs SIGKILL-after-N rather than patience.  Filed rather than fixed: it is a real hazard on a path nothing currently drives, and the fix belongs with whichever round builds the `mapledmahang` witness, because that round is the one that will first make the path reachable.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** `regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py` CALLS `os.waitpid(pid, 0)` -- A BLOCKING WAIT -- AFTER `rd(10.0)`, AND `mapledmahang` IS A PATH ON WHICH THE EMULATOR NEVER EXITS. ***  If that probe ever drives a maple DMA list with no LAST bit, the blocking wait **never returns and the harness hangs**, reporting nothing at all.  **This is the mirror of the false-green class closed today as `probedeathoracle`.**  That one was about a probe reporting ALIVE for a dead process; this is a probe that cannot report anything because it waits forever on a process that will not die.  Both come from keying an oracle on process death without a deadline -- and `probedeathoracle`&#x27;s census keyed on `WNOHANG`, so a BLOCKING `waitpid` was outside its population by construction. **The remedy is the same shape as the one already applied: a DEADLINE.**  A blocking `waitpid` in a harness that can drive a non-terminating guest path needs SIGKILL-after-N rather than patience.  Filed rather than fixed: it is a real hazard on a path nothing currently drives, and the fix belongs with whichever round builds the `mapledmahang` witness, because that round is the one that will first make the path reachable. — evidence: regress/maple_dmaaddr_probe.py; regress/artifacts/sighunt_20260917/MAPLEBOUND_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">mpipirealloc</span><span class="ttl">dev_mp.c reallocs the IPI queue larger on every IPI write with no cap, and only the target CPU&#x27;s own read drains it -- host heap grows by accretion</span><span class="tip">dev_mp.c reallocs the IPI queue larger on every IPI write with no cap, and only the target CPU&#x27;s own read drains it -- host heap grows by accretion — note: *** AN UNCAPPED HOST ALLOCATION THAT A GUEST GROWS BY ACCRETION -- THE SUCCESS-CASE DENIAL, NOT A FAILED MALLOC. ***  `dev_mp.c:198-200`: a write to `DEV_MP_IPI_ONE` or `DEV_MP_IPI_MANY` does `d-&gt;n_pending_ipis[i]++` and then `CHECK_ALLOCATION(d-&gt;ipi[i] = realloc(d-&gt;ipi[i], d-&gt;n_pending_ipis[i] * sizeof(int)))` -- **with no cap of any kind**.  Verified at HEAD. **NOTHING DRAINS THE QUEUE EXCEPT THE TARGET CPU READING IT.**  The only consumer is `DEV_MP_IPI_READ` at `:211`, which decrements `n_pending_ipis[cpu-&gt;cpu_id]` -- i.e. the *receiving* CPU&#x27;s own read.  A guest that sends IPIs and never reads them grows the host heap four bytes at a time, **with a `realloc` on every single write**, so the cost is both resident memory and repeated copying. REACHABILITY: **the test machines ALWAYS add it** -- `machine_test.c:148` and `:333` both `device_add(&quot;mp addr=... irq=...&quot;)`.  So this is not a device nobody installs. **SEVERITY LOW, RANKED BELOW `guestloopdenial`, and the reason is the shape rather than the size:** this is not a 2 GB one-shot allocation.  It is growth by accretion -- many writes, four bytes each -- so it is slower to matter than a tight loop and needs sustained guest cooperation.  It sits in the same family as `guestloopdenial` (the guest takes the host away without killing it) but arrives by a different road: **resident memory grows instead of a loop spinning.** *** NOTE WHAT `CHECK_ALLOCATION` DOES AND DOES NOT BUY HERE.  It turns a FAILED realloc into a clean `exit(1)`, which is why this is not a crash -- but it does nothing at all about a long run of SUCCESSFUL ones, which is the whole defect.  A guard against failure is not a guard against growth. ***  **ADJACENT, AND FLAGGED HONESTLY AS OUTSIDE THE DIRECTORIES THE HUNT WAS ASKED ABOUT:** `scsi_transfer_allocbuf()` in `src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c` is the malloc that SCSI controllers actually use, and devices pass guest lengths into it.  READ/WRITE is capped at **64 MiB** by `#163`&#x27;s `SCSI_MAX_XFER_BYTES` with a 64-bit multiply from `#133`.  But `dev_mb89352.c`&#x27;s `transfer_count` (a `size_t` fed from the 24-bit TCH/TCM/TCL register triple) is used as `want_len` for DATA OUT **and for the CDB**, giving a command buffer of up to **16,777,215 bytes** on a FIELD-WIDTH cap with no software cap behind it.  Machine: luna88k.  Recorded here rather than filed as its own row because it is a bound, not an overflow, and it belongs with whoever next touches mb89352 -- which `b120` already owns.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** AN UNCAPPED HOST ALLOCATION THAT A GUEST GROWS BY ACCRETION -- THE SUCCESS-CASE DENIAL, NOT A FAILED MALLOC. ***  `dev_mp.c:198-200`: a write to `DEV_MP_IPI_ONE` or `DEV_MP_IPI_MANY` does `d-&gt;n_pending_ipis[i]++` and then `CHECK_ALLOCATION(d-&gt;ipi[i] = realloc(d-&gt;ipi[i], d-&gt;n_pending_ipis[i] * sizeof(int)))` -- **with no cap of any kind**.  Verified at HEAD. **NOTHING DRAINS THE QUEUE EXCEPT THE TARGET CPU READING IT.**  The only consumer is `DEV_MP_IPI_READ` at `:211`, which decrements `n_pending_ipis[cpu-&gt;cpu_id]` -- i.e. the *receiving* CPU&#x27;s own read.  A guest that sends IPIs and never reads them grows the host heap four bytes at a time, **with a `realloc` on every single write**, so the cost is both resident memory and repeated copying. REACHABILITY: **the test machines ALWAYS add it** -- `machine_test.c:148` and `:333` both `device_add(&quot;mp addr=... irq=...&quot;)`.  So this is not a device nobody installs. **SEVERITY LOW, RANKED BELOW `guestloopdenial`, and the reason is the shape rather than the size:** this is not a 2 GB one-shot allocation.  It is growth by accretion -- many writes, four bytes each -- so it is slower to matter than a tight loop and needs sustained guest cooperation.  It sits in the same family as `guestloopdenial` (the guest takes the host away without killing it) but arrives by a different road: **resident memory grows instead of a loop spinning.** *** NOTE WHAT `CHECK_ALLOCATION` DOES AND DOES NOT BUY HERE.  It turns a FAILED realloc into a clean `exit(1)`, which is why this is not a crash -- but it does nothing at all about a long run of SUCCESSFUL ones, which is the whole defect.  A guard against failure is not a guard against growth. ***  **ADJACENT, AND FLAGGED HONESTLY AS OUTSIDE THE DIRECTORIES THE HUNT WAS ASKED ABOUT:** `scsi_transfer_allocbuf()` in `src/disk/diskimage_scsicmd.c` is the malloc that SCSI controllers actually use, and devices pass guest lengths into it.  READ/WRITE is capped at **64 MiB** by `#163`&#x27;s `SCSI_MAX_XFER_BYTES` with a 64-bit multiply from `#133`.  But `dev_mb89352.c`&#x27;s `transfer_count` (a `size_t` fed from the 24-bit TCH/TCM/TCL register triple) is used as `want_len` for DATA OUT **and for the CDB**, giving a command buffer of up to **16,777,215 bytes** on a FIELD-WIDTH cap with no software cap behind it.  Machine: luna88k.  Recorded here rather than filed as its own row because it is a bound, not an overflow, and it belongs with whoever next touches mb89352 -- which `b120` already owns. — evidence: src/devices/dev_mp.c:198-200,211,223-232; src/machines/machine_test.c:148,333; regress/artifacts/allocsize_20260917/</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">changelogroundgap</span><span class="ttl">seven rounds shipped code with no CHANGELOG round block -- four unmentioned anywhere in the file, and two of them are the rounds the vacuity floor was minted from</span><span class="tip">seven rounds shipped code with no CHANGELOG round block -- four unmentioned anywhere in the file, and two of them are the rounds the vacuity floor was minted from — note: *** SEVEN ROUNDS SHIPPED CODE WITH NO CHANGELOG ROUND BLOCK, AND FOUR OF THEM ARE UNMENTIONED ANYWHERE IN THE FILE.  Measured 2026-09-17 against a live denominator of 293 round-block headings. ***  The ethos line is *&#x27;a CHANGELOG.md round block per correction&#x27;*, and `#436 #439 #441 #443 #466 #487 #493` each have **zero** headings matching their number.  Twelve commits across the seven, every one verified present in `refs/heads/main`. *** NONE IS A VOID NUMBER, WHICH IS THE CHECK THAT MAKES THIS A DEFECT RATHER THAN A GAP IN A COUNTER. ***  This project HAS a void number (`#289`), so a skipped heading is not automatically a breach -- it is one only if the number shipped.  All seven did, and three are ordinary defect fixes: **`#441 sh4bcr`** (*two guest instructions ended the emulator process*), **`#443 sh4pcicexit`** (*one guest LOAD ended the host, on 116 of 137 offsets*), and **`#493 wdcaltoffset`** (*a rung-3 witness for a live sev-high defect*). *** AND THE SHARP PART: `sh4bcr` AND `sh4pcicexit` ARE THE TWO ROUNDS THE VACUITY FLOOR WAS MINTED FROM. ***  The carrier cites them by name as its founding evidence -- *&quot;sh4bcr&#x27;s first detector passed a mutant reinstating the very host kill its round removed; sh4pcicexit&#x27;s passed the original kill verbatim&quot;* -- so **the most-cited exemplars in the whole rule set have no round block in the file a reader would go to.**  A future reader re-deriving the floor&#x27;s warrant finds the rule, finds the rounds named, and finds nothing in the CHANGELOG under either number. **THE GAP IS DIRECTIONAL AND IT IS THE INVERSE OF THE USUAL ONE.**  This session&#x27;s other records findings are all *written down but wrong*; this one is *shipped but not written down*, which no gate looks for -- section H checks seats were fired, G checks an answer was recorded, and `check_cited_artifacts.py` checks a cited path exists.  **Nothing asks whether a round that shipped has a block at all.** **DISPOSITION: recorded, NOT reconstructed.**  Writing seven retroactive round blocks from commit messages would manufacture records, which is worse than the gap -- so ONE consolidated *assessed-not-changed* entry names every round and its commits, and the reconstruction is filed for a round that can do it from the rounds&#x27; own artifacts. *The checker that would close this is a ledger-row-to-CHANGELOG-heading existence test, forward-only from a cutoff -- the `check_bugfile_sync.py` idiom.  FILED, not built: it is a tooling change owing two negative controls, and this file&#x27;s own rule is that such a change ships as its own harness round rather than as a rider.*  DERIVER, because a count in a durable record names the command that made it and the tree it ran against (HEAD at the time of writing, working tree clean of CHANGELOG edits):
15452:[2026-10-17] the tools/cxlaunch.sh landing CLOSES this row&#x27;s NUL half (grep -a; controls X8, a real NUL-bearing wall, 6 -&gt; 8) and leaves its empty-brief half OPEN, with a second road measured on the landing&#x27;s v5 pass (Fable 5.1, with the fake codex): a MISSING brief, or a RELATIVE one that does not resolve from the project root, reaches codex as an EMPTY prompt -- the launcher reads it after cd &quot;$PROJ&quot;, checks its size only against the argv cap (before the cd, from the caller&#x27;s directory) and neither its existence nor a minimum size (GPT-6 Astra, v6 pass) -- and an answer to it is reported &#x27;seat answered&#x27;, exit 0; the 2026-10-17 relative launch (the main loop&#x27;s own) exited 1 only because codex refused.  A relative OUT dir breaks too.  The v5 pass ruled it disclose-and-FILE; it is filed HERE, the defect&#x27;s own row.  The fix stays #544&#x27;s (the peer&#x27;s round, e8a2bc: a 64-byte floor and a verdict-line gate, staged in _scratchpad/w544/), which now rebases on the landed launcher; offered to it (Fable 5.1): read the brief before the cd into a variable and refuse an empty one (BODY=$(cat &quot;$BRIEF&quot;) &amp;&amp; [ -n &quot;$BODY&quot; ] || exit 9), closing the missing and relative roads with the empty one.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c tok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">∅</span><span class="tip">2026-09-19 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] Filed from #539&#x27;s pass 1 / pass 2 panels (see the row title for the seat and artifact).</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g