n></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ppc527residual</span><span class="ttl">#527&#x27;s five residuals, moved here from `ppcrldimimask` at its closure: the SH=63/MB=1 full-mask rldimi row, the wrapping stale-rS alias row, the Rc=1-only insertion skip, CENSUS_SHRINK and R_NOGUARD -- coverage debt and named measurements on regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py and ppc_rldimi_probe.py, none a floor breach</span><span class="tip">#527&#x27;s five residuals, moved here from `ppcrldimimask` at its closure: the SH=63/MB=1 full-mask rldimi row, the wrapping stale-rS alias row, the Rc=1-only insertion skip, CENSUS_SHRINK and R_NOGUARD -- coverage debt and named measurements on regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py and ppc_rldimi_probe.py, none a floor breach — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 by #527&#x27;s closure-records commit.  Fable 5.1&#x27;s 2026-09-12 closing ruling (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/fable_adjudication_20260912.md) graded each clause (e) or a named measurement, not owed before #527&#x27;s commit, and asked that they be FILED; the 2026-10-02 disposition (regress/artifacts/idlessaudit_20261002/AGY_FLAGS_DISPOSITION.md, flags #10-#14) then made `ppcrldimimask` their carrier -- &#x27;the carrier owes its recorded work whenever its round runs&#x27;.  This row carries them now, so that row could close (GPT-6 Astra, #527&#x27;s closure check).  The five, specified in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under `ppcrldimimask` -- SHIPPED: (1) ADD a full-mask patterned rldimi row at SH=63, MB=1 from 0xDEADBEEFCAFEF00D, want 0xEF56DF77E57F7806 (costs a table 4 and pin 29 -&gt; 30); (2) an alias row at SH=40, MB=40 for a stale rS re-read on WRAPPING masks (codex&#x27;s arithmetic, not built); (3) an insertion skipped only when Rc=1 survives both probes (both CONTRACTs disclose it); (4) CENSUS_SHRINK -- one run of the fixed build with a shortened census, the literal pins&#x27; unique channel; (5) R_NOGUARD -- delete rlwimi&#x27;s `sh ?` guard, predicted 28/28, load-bearing for definedness since the fix narrowed tmp (Fable).</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ip30stallgreen</span><span class="ttl">ip30_abort_probe.py reads a load-stalled session as a run whose remaining accesses never happened: session() returns alive=True for a child its 12 s poll kills, holding only the transcript read before the poll -- so on V2_assigntick, xbit rev2&#x27;s session stalling with exactly one ISR announce captured leaves all 41 gate-14 checks green</span><span class="tip">ip30_abort_probe.py reads a load-stalled session as a run whose remaining accesses never happened: session() returns alive=True for a child its 12 s poll kills, holding only the transcript read before the poll -- so on V2_assigntick, xbit rev2&#x27;s session stalling with exactly one ISR announce captured leaves all 41 gate-14 checks green — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 by #566 (GPT-6 Astra and Grok 4.7, pass 2 and F2).  A stall BEFORE a session&#x27;s access leaves its `alive&#x27; and `no&#x27; rows green -- the kill rows, `acc 30 rd&#x27;, `tick 49&#x27;, the read arms&#x27; `txt&#x27; rows -- and its `yes&#x27; row red (the write-arm and bit-50 tick sessions carry one); a count row that wants ONE announce (latch 1x, latch 28wr, latch 30wr, latch tick, xbit rev, xbit rev2) reads green whenever exactly one was captured, so V2_assigntick goes all-green when xbit rev2&#x27;s session stalls with one ISR announce captured and every other session passes.  The reap after the SIGKILL is a blocking waitpid and gate_arm.sh puts no timeout on the probe.  Repair shape: a per-session completion marker every row requires -- a probe change, owing a kill table.  Disclosed in the gate block&#x27;s comment.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ip30abortresidual</span><span class="ttl">#531&#x27;s residuals, carried here from ip30abortkill for its close: V7_nocounter (delete `d-&gt;reg_0x20000 += 1000&#x27;: 32/32 in kill table 4) and the width axis V8_len4 (`&amp;&amp; len == 4&#x27; on the 0x10020 write guard: 33/33 in kill table 5), both disclosed coverage debt; and three questions #531 filed and never disposed of -- the announce string&#x27;s truth at the 0x10020/0x10028 write arms, the per-instance latch axis, fall-through after a non-exiting fatal().  None a floor breach</span><span class="tip">#531&#x27;s residuals, carried here from ip30abortkill for its close: V7_nocounter (delete `d-&gt;reg_0x20000 += 1000&#x27;: 32/32 in kill table 4) and the width axis V8_len4 (`&amp;&amp; len == 4&#x27; on the 0x10020 write guard: 33/33 in kill table 5), both disclosed coverage debt; and three questions #531 filed and never disposed of -- the announce string&#x27;s truth at the 0x10020/0x10028 write arms, the per-instance latch axis, fall-through after a non-exiting fatal().  None a floor breach — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 with ip30abortkill&#x27;s closure records, so that row can close without dropping them.  (1) V7_nocounter -- the probe&#x27;s CONTRACT has it under MEASURED ESCAPES (MH_nocounter): the counter is UNCONDITIONAL, above the de-fatalised guard, so fix-independent (the fable5 ruling of 2026-09-18); measured on the 32-row instrument, not re-run at 33.  The shipped `acc 20000&#x27; row cannot see it by construction: its oracle accepts any readback - 0x1234 that is a non-negative multiple of 1000 (`_d20000 &gt;= 0&#x27;), so N = 0 passes -- table 4&#x27;s V7 log shows that row green (GLM 5.3 predicted the opposite at the closure check).  (2) V8_len4 -- under DOES NOT CATCH: the guarded body never tests len, so by the pcc2widthkills bound it is a single-operand marginal; cheap -- len takes at most four values and the probe already has guest_store64/guest_load64.  (1) and (2) are COVERAGE DEBT above the floor, FILED as the floor&#x27;s own rule says; not owed before ip30abortkill&#x27;s close.  Grok 4.7 dissented at the closure check: they need no carrier and stay disclosed in the CONTRACT (grok_close531.txt).  (3) three questions #531&#x27;s CHANGELOG block filed (`Also filed:&#x27;, under RECORDED, NOT RESOLVED) and regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/PRETABLE3.out lists the first two of -- the announce string&#x27;s TRUTH (`interrupt-status rewrite not implemented&#x27; is printed by the 0x10020 and 0x10028 write arms, which keep their stores; glm and kimi), the per-INSTANCE latch axis (N = 1 per device instance, the tree ships sgi_ip30_2, every row runs against one instance; kimi), and fall-through after a non-exiting fatal() (deepseek).  #531&#x27;s F13 ruling silenced the read arms and settled none of the three; this row does not claim them repaired, and they were on no ledger row (GPT-6 Astra, the closure check).  Owed when this row&#x27;s round runs: for (1) and (2), a row each or a recorded decision not to; for (3), an explicit disposition of each.  dev_sgi_ip30.c&#x27;s stale comment counts are #561&#x27;s held places 7-10, carried by heldrecedits; the load-stall green is ip30stallgreen.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">heldrecedits</span><span class="ttl">#560 and #561 HELD 30 ruled corrections on 2026-10-06 that no ledger row carries: 29 src/ comments (#560&#x27;s 12, #561&#x27;s 17; each owes est/ byte-identity and build-tree propagation) and #560&#x27;s `hrec extra&#x27; comment in regress/diff_ieee_store.c (:296-301) -- the bug file&#x27;s #560 and #561 sections end `Still owed&#x27;</span><span class="tip">#560 and #561 HELD 30 ruled corrections on 2026-10-06 that no ledger row carries: 29 src/ comments (#560&#x27;s 12, #561&#x27;s 17; each owes est/ byte-identity and build-tree propagation) and #560&#x27;s `hrec extra&#x27; comment in regress/diff_ieee_store.c (:296-301) -- the bug file&#x27;s #560 and #561 sections end `Still owed&#x27; — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 with ip30abortkill&#x27;s closure records.  The lists are OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md&#x27;s `### #560&#x27; section (HELD (20)) and `### #561&#x27; section (HELD (17): every src/ comment, each with its ruled replacement under regress/artifacts/recordfix_20261002/adjudication_20261006/EDITS_561.json key `held&#x27;); #560&#x27;s rulings are its ADJ_FINAL.md and ADJ_HREC_FINAL.md.  This row carries the 30 of those 37 that no ledger row carried: the 29 src/ comments -- #560&#x27;s 12, dev_pcc2.c&#x27;s among them (held then because the peer session&#x27;s uncommitted work sits in that file), and #561&#x27;s 17 -- and #560&#x27;s `hrec extra&#x27; (diff_ieee_store.c:296-301).  #560&#x27;s other seven held sites (hrec 2, 9, 10, 19a-c, 20) are hrecordowed&#x27;s members hbundle_regress15:F7, hbundle_tools1:F5/F6 and hdiffieeestore:F6/F7, and stay there (GPT-6 Astra, the confirm).  Four of the 29 are #561&#x27;s places 7-10, dev_sgi_ip30.c&#x27;s stale comment counts, which ip30abortkill&#x27;s closure check first proposed to move onto ip30abortresidual -- a split of one held set (Grok 4.7).  The lists are as of 2026-10-06, and #565 has since corrected hrec 2&#x27;s claim, so the round reconciles each item against HEAD before applying it.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">untokenedanswers</span><span class="ttl">stage_tokens.is_answer() counts an untokened entry as an ANSWER even when it records a non-answer, for every seat and since August: a keyword correlate finds 497 matches among entries counted as answers, on 129 rows, 81 of them closed -- actual non-answers and known false positives together -- among them deliberate roster exclusions that section H&#x27;s nine-seat count then reads as seats that answered</span><span class="tip">stage_tokens.is_answer() counts an untokened entry as an ANSWER even when it records a non-answer, for every seat and since August: a keyword correlate finds 497 matches among entries counted as answers, on 129 rows, 81 of them closed -- actual non-answers and known false positives together -- among them deliberate roster exclusions that section H&#x27;s nine-seat count then reads as seats that answered — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 by fableheldform -- from broader keyword reconnaissance motivated by GLM 5.3&#x27;s finding on its design pass; GLM&#x27;s own settling fact, a keyword-free review of every counted Fable entry, REMAINS OWED.  Grok 4.7 found an instance on a row closed that morning: ppcgateimageskip&#x27;s review stage reads 9/9 through seven untokened non-answers -- five owner-roster exclusions, GLM deliberately not fired for that records question, and Fable held after its wall (GPT-6 Astra&#x27;s confirm).  Census: regress/artifacts/fableheldform/design_20261018/CENSUS_wide.txt (by seat: kimi 129, agy 96, fable5 75, codex 75, opus5 37, minimax 33, grok 23, glm 17, deepseek 12; by month: 2026-08 132, 2026-09 301, 2026-10 64), a keyword net -- 497 MATCHES among entries counted as answers, which include actual non-answers and known false positives (genuine answers that quote the keywords); not a count of the class.  fableheldform fixed only its own slice (Fable&#x27;s walls since the owner&#x27;s 2026-10-17 hold, plus one of 2026-10-16).  Owed: (1) a STRUCTURAL check, forward-only from a cutoff, that reds a NEW entry recording a non-answer (NOT FIRED, SEAT FAILURE, walled, stalled) without a stage token -- keyword-based, so it must say so and carry negative controls; (2) an owner ruling on how a deliberately excluded seat is recorded: the non-answer token makes a narrowed-roster stage read short under section H&#x27;s nine-seat count, while an untokened note makes it read as answered; (3) the historical entries are corrected row by row when a round touches them, not swept.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">ptyprobepins</span><span class="ttl">A commit that adds a pty probe can leave gate 6&#x27;s readiness-census pins stale, and only the weekly battery sees it: #564 (e7fc97e6) committed sgi_mte_decline_probe.py -- promptless -- and moved neither EXPECT_PTY_PROBES nor the grandfathered list, so the 2026-10-18 battery would have reddened gate 6 (examples before it: #500&#x27;s probe, the 24 probes of the 2026-09 hygienecensus drift, and the earlier 37 -&gt; 41 drift and #498&#x27;s 41 -&gt; 42 repair that gate_hygiene.sh&#x27;s own comment records)</span><span class="tip">A commit that adds a pty probe can leave gate 6&#x27;s readiness-census pins stale, and only the weekly battery sees it: #564 (e7fc97e6) committed sgi_mte_decline_probe.py -- promptless -- and moved neither EXPECT_PTY_PROBES nor the grandfathered list, so the 2026-10-18 battery would have reddened gate 6 (examples before it: #500&#x27;s probe, the 24 probes of the 2026-09 hygienecensus drift, and the earlier 37 -&gt; 41 drift and #498&#x27;s 41 -&gt; 42 repair that gate_hygiene.sh&#x27;s own comment records) — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 by #567, which found it while re-deriving its own pins: tools/derive_hygiene_pins.sh reported the pty census 76 vs its pin 74 and the promptless census 44 vs 43, and refused --write while sgi_mte_decline_probe.py stood neither grandfathered nor prompt-detecting.  #567 grandfathered it on the 2026-09-19 precedent and wrote the pins.  The landing spot is STRUCTURAL: a precommit section that runs the deriver in report mode and refuses a commit whose tree leaves a census off its pin -- forward-only, controlled both ways (a pty probe added without its pin reds; with it, greens).  The design question it owes: the precommit sees the STAGED tree while the pin counts what lies in the WORKING tree (owner ruling 2026-09-23, &#x27;count all&#x27;), so the check must be defined against one of them.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sgimtereadiness</span><span class="ttl">regress/sgi_mte_decline_probe.py (#564) is a PROMPTLESS pty probe -- fixed waits (settle 1.5 s, 0.22 s between commands), no readiness read -- grandfathered by #567 so gate 6 stays green; converting its readiness is its own round</span><span class="tip">regress/sgi_mte_decline_probe.py (#564) is a PROMPTLESS pty probe -- fixed waits (settle 1.5 s, 0.22 s between commands), no readiness read -- grandfathered by #567 so gate 6 stays green; converting its readiness is its own round — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 by #567 (gate_hygiene.sh&#x27;s fourth grandfather block names it, with its provenance).  Fixed waits are load-sensitive: on a slow host a command can be sent before the debugger prompt returns.  Same disposition as the 20 probes grandfathered on 2026-09-19 (their block begins `alpha_harness_probe.py&#x27;).</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">batterystandby</span><span class="ttl">The weekly battery dies when the laptop enters Modern Standby: on 2026-10-18 a power-source change at 18:13 put the machine into Modern Standby during gate 3 of 16, the scheduled task&#x27;s process was terminated (last result 0x8007042B), the verdict file holds START only, and nothing re-ran it -- tools/nightly_battery.sh holds no wake request</span><span class="tip">The weekly battery dies when the laptop enters Modern Standby: on 2026-10-18 a power-source change at 18:13 put the machine into Modern Standby during gate 3 of 16, the scheduled task&#x27;s process was terminated (last result 0x8007042B), the verdict file holds START only, and nothing re-ran it -- tools/nightly_battery.sh holds no wake request — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-18 by the main loop (087a90) from the System log (Kernel-Power 105 and 506 at 18:13:18-26) and the dead run&#x27;s log, _scratchpad/nightly/battery_20261018_180001.log, whose last write is 18:12:10.  The WSL VM survived (up since 2026-10-16); the Windows-side driver did not.  The active plan&#x27;s AC standby is `never&#x27; and its DC standby 180 s, so this bites only off AC.  Re-triggered by hand the same night.  The remedy is an OWNER decision about the machine&#x27;s power policy -- a wake request held for the run&#x27;s duration, or a DC policy change -- not a round&#x27;s.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">fbctrlvec128</span><span class="ttl">dev_fbctrl.c&#x27;s DATA and PORT arms take a 16-byte store as its LOW 64 bits: testppc&#x27;s stvx/stvxl reach the device with len 16 (default_test() installs fbctrl), the callback receives all 16 bytes, and memory_readmax64() builds idata from the low half -- so a 128-bit COMMAND value whose low half is 0-2 is accepted and runs (a low half of 1 runs SET_RESOLUTION), and a 128-bit selector whose low half is 0-7 is accepted: #567&#x27;s and #549&#x27;s width defect one width out</span><span class="tip">dev_fbctrl.c&#x27;s DATA and PORT arms take a 16-byte store as its LOW 64 bits: testppc&#x27;s stvx/stvxl reach the device with len 16 (default_test() installs fbctrl), the callback receives all 16 bytes, and memory_readmax64() builds idata from the low half -- so a 128-bit COMMAND value whose low half is 0-2 is accepted and runs (a low half of 1 runs SET_RESOLUTION), and a 128-bit selector whose low half is 0-7 is accepted: #567&#x27;s and #549&#x27;s width defect one width out — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-19 by #567 from its pass 2 (GPT-6 Astra&#x27;s F1, source-derived: X(stvx) and PPC_31_STVXL hand memory_rw a 16-byte buffer, DATA at +0x10 in the 0x20 window is not truncated, memory_readmax64() keeps the low half on a big-endian guest; testppc installs the device through default_test(), machine_test.c&#x27;s line 154 -- line 337 is testmips&#x27;s own install) and its F2 pass (GPT-6 Astra, Grok 4.7 and GLM 5.3: FILE, not this round -- a PowerPC stvx oracle lies outside #567&#x27;s MIPS detector and a len &gt; 8 refusal would ship unwitnessed; Grok 4.7 and Astra corrected the mechanism the probe&#x27;s contract had stated: the device is NOT spared the prefix, it receives it and the conversion drops it).  Disclosed as NOT CLAIMED (6) in regress/fbctrl_cmd_probe.py.  LOW: no host kill follows (only 0-2 run), but a wide value is accepted as its low part, the class #567 closed at 64 bits.  Owed: a rung-3 witness on testppc (a 16-byte store at DATA and at PORT), then one disposition for BOTH arms in one round -- a refusal of len &gt; 8 before the conversion, or an acceptance documented as the architecture&#x27;s.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gate16fence</span><span class="ttl">regress/gate_mips_folds.sh launches #549&#x27;s fbctrl_port_probe.py and #567&#x27;s fbctrl_cmd_probe.py with no timeout: a wedged probe hangs gate 16 and with it the weekly battery (a visible hang, not a false pass)</span><span class="tip">regress/gate_mips_folds.sh launches #549&#x27;s fbctrl_port_probe.py and #567&#x27;s fbctrl_cmd_probe.py with no timeout: a wedged probe hangs gate 16 and with it the weekly battery (a visible hang, not a false pass) — witness: none — note: filed 2026-10-19 by #567 from its pass 2 (GLM 5.3&#x27;s Q6), upheld as FILE at F2 by GPT-6 Astra, Grok 4.7 and GLM 5.3.  Not a false pass: a probe that never prints its DONE line reds the block&#x27;s parse check, and a binary that runs nothing reds `sessions clean&#x27;; the cost is a battery that never reaches its verdict.  A harness round rather than a rider, because the fence belongs to both blocks (and to any probe launch a gate makes unfenced): wrap each launch in timeout(1), sized from a measured run, and red on its exit 124 -- controlled both ways (a stub that sleeps past the fence reds; the real probe greens).</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="divider"><th colspan="10">closed</th></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">d1</span><span class="ttl">d1  Make drive_guest.py use $LOGDIR, not the fixed /tmp/gxregress path</span><span class="tip">d1  Make drive_guest.py use $LOGDIR, not the fixed /tmp/gxregress path — commits: f92e5a1 7cfa466 0ad64b5 — Closed on the adjudicator&#x27;s finding that it was already complete. Its own PASS-WITH-GAPS regress verdict was reached by RUNNING the evidence, not reading it, and every gap it named is filed as a row. — witness: harness:the mutation census and the R1 export/hardcode kills, each re-killed BY HAND by the flagship seat (export deleted -&gt; no FAIL; driver re-hardcoded -&gt; no FAIL), gate 2 PASS 131 twice</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — It found that the item was never executed. It made it a hard prerequisite of all mutation runs. — evidence: _scratchpad/adjudication_r420_fable.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — REAL - and the planned fix would be INERT: LOGDIR is never exported by any regress script, so os.environ.get in the driver reads nothing and keeps the hardcoded path. BOTH an export (or an argv pass at gate_crossfamily.sh:42) AND the driver change are needed. The gate&#x27;s own shell redirection does honor LOGDIR, which is what hides this. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_batchA_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — the driver hardcodes the shared log directory and the caller does not explicitly propagate LOGDIR — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — drive_guest.py hardcodes /tmp/gxregress in drive() and ignores LOGDIR from the environment. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — `drive_guest.py` hardcodes `/tmp/gxregress` and `lib.sh` does not export `LOGDIR` — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/grok_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — REAL — drive_guest.py:197-199 hardcodes /tmp/gxregress; LOGDIR never read; gate_hygiene grades $LOGDIR/drive_*.log — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/kimi_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — REAL — but honoring $LOGDIR alone is insufficient without a caller change too — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/glm_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — UNKNOWN — UNKNOWN — cannot read file:line; hardcoded /tmp/gxregress is plausible but unverified. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/deepseek_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — REAL — a configurable log path that the script silently ignores is a test-isolation bug. (packet-only resend after the mixed-brief stall; P3 resolved) — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/assess/minimax_resend_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — export at lib.sh:24 + os.environ.get at :197-199, ONE carrier shared by producer and grader. Rejects argv (a SECOND carrier - writer and grader could diverge) and a call-site prefix (the census must then remember it, and forgetting is silently inert - the defect class under repair). DETECTOR TRAP NAMED: do NOT use `env LOGDIR=...`, which exports from outside and stays green with lib.sh&#x27;s export missing - that tests half the defect. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/fable5_r1_n5_n6_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — export at lib.sh:24 AND `os.environ.get(&quot;LOGDIR&quot;) or &quot;/tmp/gxregress&quot;` at :197 - MEASURED, the or-form is REQUIRED: the .get(default) form that the other eight seats proposed writes to the FILESYSTEM ROOT when LOGDIR is empty. Also measured: the driver-only fix is CONDITIONALLY inert (dead for `LOGDIR=x; ./run.sh`, live for an exported value) - worse than uniform. A GATE-5 ROW IS MEASURED VACUOUS (in the default battery LOGDIR equals the hardcoded literal); the detector must be an offline selftest with a private LOGDIR and a synthetic rig name, asserting the FILES not the driver&#x27;s LOG= line (a half-fix kept LOG= correct while the raw log escaped). — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_dispatches/opus_r1_research_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — export LOGDIR from lib.sh and derive both driver log paths from it. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/codex_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — export LOGDIR in lib.sh; driver reads os.environ.get with the same default. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/agy_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — export LOGDIR from lib.sh; the driver joins that directory for both pty logs. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/grok_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — MANUAL — export at lib.sh:24 AND os.environ.get at :197-199, proven by a fake-emulator selftest wired into gate_offline.sh (a new selftest_logdir.py, not a gate-5 row). — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/kimi_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — packet: export at lib.sh:25 + os.environ.get at :196-199. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/glm_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — packet: export LOGDIR; driver reads it with the /tmp/gxregress default. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/deepseek_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — BELIEVED — packet: export in lib.sh; resolve both pty paths via os.environ.get. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/d1/research/minimax_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c hold"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">⏸</span><span class="tip">2026-08-15 — CONFIRMED — Implemented and committed as f92e5a1 with its detector. Gate 2 PASS (122 checks) including the two new rows; the detector was negative-controlled in BOTH directions - no/no FAIL on the unfixed tree before the edit, yes/yes PASS after. Awaiting the panel&#x27;s review of the actual diff. — evidence: pipeline/evidence/_closure/r1_research_closure_2026-08-15.md</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — CONFIRMED — CLOSED AS ALREADY-COMPLETE, and verified before closing rather than taken from the hold. Nobody proposed dropping these -- the adjudicator found them while asked what should be closed that no seat had raised, and its point stands: FINISHED WORK HAD SAT OPEN FOR FIVE DAYS.
16315:*** THE &#x27;no new oracle&#x27; PRONG IS DOING LESS WORK THAN THE CLUSTER PANEL ASSUMED: *** a host-log counter and an IRQ counter are two static ints in the SAME 130-line harness -- the seat has both in one file. The prong is real but near-free here, so what genuinely separates these rows is FIX DESIGN, not oracle count. Worth carrying into future reopening-rule calls. — evidence: cluster repartition by fix design rather than oracle count</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">fbwitness</span><span class="ttl">fbwitness  The footbridge now HAS a probe witness, dissolving the &#x27;only a rig is missing&#x27; blocker on four datasheet-backed defects</span><span class="tip">fbwitness  The footbridge now HAS a probe witness, dissolving the &#x27;only a rig is missing&#x27; blocker on four datasheet-backed defects — commits: 6014b7e7 — CLOSE (assess) — witness: harness:regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py (6014b7e7) -- 10/10 in 1.9 s: a real guest ldr/str reaches the device through real address decode; TIMER_1_LOAD reads back masked to 24 bits by the handler, a one-instruction kill arm dies at 0x188 while the isolation arm survives; the MMU trap negative-controlled three ways (C2 reads CP15 c1 back, C3 shows the two arms differ, C4 is an inequality) — note: [2026-10-02] WITNESS FIELD ADDED by #557 (section K fired when the commits field was filled: a row closed with commits names its witness, and this row had none because its field was empty): a harness round, so `harness:&lt;what proved it&gt;`, taken from the closing commit&#x27;s own message and claiming nothing it does not.  [2026-10-02] COMMITS FIELD FILLED by #557 from the census of closed rows with no commit (regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/grok/grok_closedcensus.txt; Astra&#x27;s git log, regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/aconf_census/codex_aconf_census.txt): 6014b7e7 -- the first committed footbridge witness; touches regress/footbridge_sites_probe.py.  </span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — CLOSE (assess). It dissolves a false blocker, and its first-draft failure (0x0 everywhere with the RAM control green) already lives in the carrier as ladder clause (ii). The four datasheet-backed defects become ELIGIBLE; order them by SEVERITY per the corrected ranking clause, not by rung.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-08-19 — MEASURED. A cats cold-debugger probe reaches the footbridge through real address decode: TIMER_1_LOAD readback 0x345678 carries the handler&#x27;s own masking signature, and a one-instruction arm difference kills the emulator at dev_footbridge.c:495-497. Consequences: fbdiv&#x27;s reachability is upgradable MANUAL -&gt; CONFIRMED (its record says in terms &#x27;R6 is THE ONLY CODE ROUND WITH NO REACHABILITY WITNESS OF ANY KIND... there is no m8online analog&#x27; -- this IS that analog); and cats/netwinder appear NOWHERE in regress/*.sh or regress/*.py (verified), so the probe needs porting into regress/ to be worth anything long-term.  [panel_20260819_072053]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-20 — MANUAL — WALL DISCHARGED -- re-fired 2026-08-20 after codex answered a health test (CODEX_ALIVE_20260820, and it got the arithmetic right). Verified a REAL review, not the echo that preceded it: delta over the brief is well past the ~688-byte banner and the usage-limit string is absent from the tail.
17135:CORRECTED AFTER THE CODE COMMIT, by pass 2: the guard-placement claim cited #447&#x27;s silent-wrong-rate hazard, which CANNOT OCCUR AT THIS PREDICATE -- pclock == 0 makes every prescaler case 0.0 and timer_hz[] is already 0.0 from the DEVINIT memset. The placement is still right, for diagnostic consistency (the debug() line is unlatched and would print per-STORE). And `m_below` was misattributed to that claim: it is the missing-`break` mutant caught by `unlatch`, and NOTHING in the kill table measures the guard&#x27;s position. Also: the read-back channel is itself a FABRICATION -- real SH-4 always latches TCR, so a guest doing read-modify-write silently loses its update, and `unlatch` pins that as correct. It is the lesser of two fabrications, not an architectural channel, and the record now says so. — witness: probe:regress/sh_pclock_probe.py (rung 3, 16 rows, SHIPPED). MEASURED 6/16 on the true pre-fix build and 16/16 post-fix, SAME SCRIPT BOTH SIDES. The pre-fix build was made from `git show HEAD:src/devices/dev_sh4.c` into a private clone and grep-confirmed to still contain `INTERNAL ERROR: pclock`, NOT from build/ -- a seat measured build/gxemul&#x27;s mtime moving underneath it mid-review, so a run it believed was pre-fix was post-fix and nothing in the output distinguished them. Controls: LIVENESS (guest store then guest load of an asymmetric 0x12345678) and DEVICE SIGNATURE (IPRD reset value 0xda74, which RAM cannot produce and an unmapped address answers with `non-existant paddr`), both green on both sides and both in their OWN sessions so neither touches TCR.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — FOUND BY CHECKING A SITE THAT READS AS UNREACHABLE, at the owner&#x27;s explicit request. &#x27;INTERNAL ERROR ... Aborting&#x27; is construction-fault wording, which is why four rounds of SH-4 exit(1) work passed over it.
17141:THREE machines, not one: testsh reproduces too (machine_test.c:512 also names SH7750), which puts the defect inside the harness&#x27;s own test surface. The first testsh attempt was VOID -- it reused landisk&#x27;s scratch address and the store never executed, so &#x27;testsh does not reproduce&#x27; was the obvious wrong conclusion; rule 4 caught it. The control is the same store on landisk, which prints &#x27;[ sh4 timer 0 clock set to 2083333.312500 Hz ]&#x27; and runs on.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Liveness token returned. Found the baresh error INDEPENDENTLY of grok, plus four more citation defects of mine: dev_sh4.c:1963-1973 is off by one (`case SH4_TCR0:` is :1964); &#x27;five lines below&#x27; is ~3 for the comment and ~22 for the code; the probe cited machine_hpcsh.c:76 for RAM when the dev_ram_init calls are :77-80; and SCRATCH[&#x27;testsh&#x27;] was a dead entry that reads like coverage. *** IT ALSO FOUND A STALE INSTRUCTION IN THE CARRIER *** -- the SH4_VAL_* enum conversion was still listed as an outstanding obligation and had already been discharged, which would have made a future round redo finished work. Supplied the pass-2 hazard that shaped the diff: the guard must stay ABOVE the prescaler switch or timer_hz is set to pclock/4 == 0 and debug() announces it. Verified fatal() does not exit, that break leaves the relative_addr switch before `d-&gt;tcr[timer_nr] = idata`, and that timer_hz is consumed only as a numerator (:461). Named the four concrete places the &#x27;this machine cannot run timers&#x27; fact must live, following the machine_test.c:97-120 #456 precedent. Refused to settle the SH-3 question from memory after searching hpc_bootinfo.h, machine.h, HISTORY and doc/TODO.html.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** THE ONLY SEAT THAT CAUGHT THE ROUND&#x27;S FALSE PANEL CLAIM, AND IT WAS THE SEAT BEING MISCOUNTED. *** It read the shipped comment&#x27;s &#x27;a panel of nine seats reached this 8-1&#x27;, observed it was written while this seat was still running, and noted it does not vote B -- an L4 wrong record about the panel itself, inside a round about a guard nobody checked. It ALSO measured that build/gxemul&#x27;s mtime moved underneath it mid-review, so its own &#x27;pre-fix&#x27; run was post-fix and indistinguishable in the output; the round&#x27;s witness was re-derived from `git show HEAD:` because of that. Further measured findings, all rebuilt in a private clone with build/ and est/ untouched: -E baresh reproduces (third machine); -e jornada690 reproduces; all five machines carry the device (IPRD=0xda74 on each); the accept coverage was ASYMMETRIC (dreamcast had frequency but no readback -- row added); the guard-only growth mutant reddens all four accept rows while every kill row stays green; and Option D works mechanically (-C SH7708R needs no rebuild, store hits non-existant paddr, banner byte-identical) but must be REJECTED because arch-3 removes the entire SH-4 register block, SH-3 interrupt handlers are /* TODO */ stubs at cpu_sh.c:527-533, and cpu_sh.c:224 uses a leftover UTLB loop counter as templ.line, inert only because that handler is a stub. Its verdict was OPTION C (B plus a synthetic clock for baresh/testsh); the B half shipped, the second half is filed as sh4testclock on the round-scope rule.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Verdict OPTION B with the sharpest single design catch of the round: a lone accept row pinned at landisk&#x27;s 33.33 MHz is passed by a mutant that HARDCODES 33333333, so the detector cannot tell &#x27;reads the machine&#x27;s clock&#x27; from &#x27;agrees with landisk&#x27; -- two clock values are required. Also refuted the testsh-clock proposal on scope: testsh&#x27;s value is precisely as the regression case for an arch-4 CPU whose board omitted pclock, and giving it a clock would delete the only test of the missing-clock path. Named the guard-growth mutant (move `break` outside the block) and confirmed rung-3 admissibility.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Verdict OPTION B, consistent with the majority; no separate finding acted on.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — First seat to catch that machine_test.c:535 is baresh, not testsh -- a THIRD affected machine the brief never named, which widened the defect. Verified the four-link chain against GXEMUL-SEC rather than accepting the brief. Verdict OPTION B. NOTE ON SEAT HEALTH: sampled at 123 bytes it looked exactly like a dead seat; it was MID-ANSWER and returned 12.7 KB. The carrier&#x27;s sample-twice rule earned its keep again.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Verdict OPTION C. Contributed the distinction the round kept: testsh and baresh are SYNTHETIC, so their parameters are the project&#x27;s own DEFINITION rather than a hardware FACT, and assigning a pclock there invents nothing -- an argument that does NOT transfer to hpcsh. Graded PARTIAL rather than UPHELD because the scope half was refuted by two seats on the project&#x27;s own round-scope rule and is filed rather than merged. It also asserted from memory that the real Jornada 680 used an SH-3; that is UNVERIFIABLE in this tree and was explicitly not acted on.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Verdict OPTION B. Framed the rejection of A cleanly in three angles (functional, aliasing, reset-value). No formatted VERDICT line at the head -- FORMAT miss, recorded as such and never as agreement.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Verdict OPTION B. Named the failure mode of A correctly. Recovered from the previous round&#x27;s REFUTED headline.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Verdict OPTION B, with the strongest statement of the trade in the panel: &#x27;loud-broken outranks silent-wrong, because silent-wrong is the kind of defect that ships to users and survives for years.&#x27; That sentence is in the shipped comment.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4prio0</span><span class="ttl">sh4prio0  Interrupt priority 0 ends the host -- a documented, RESET-DEFAULT state; one guest store on -E testsh and no guest write can prevent it</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">sh4prio0  Interrupt priority 0 ends the host -- a documented, RESET-DEFAULT state; one guest store on -E testsh and no guest write can prevent it — commits: dc0c9d5 0288328 — HOLD: regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry — Author self-review disclosed. Shipped as #455 with a 7-row detector: 2/7 pre-fix, 7/7 post-fix, both controls green in both directions, vacuity floor (a)(b)(c) all measured green, gate 10 PASS at 80 checks. The accept row is discharged by MEASURED KILL (6/7 under `if (prio &lt;= 1) return;`), which the de-escalation clause requires. TWO escapes remain FILED as coverage debt: clearing SH_INT_ASSERTED and deleting the guard outright -- no row reads the pending bit or the arbitration state. — witness: probe:regress/sh_intprio_probe.py (rung 3, 7 rows, SHIPPED). One guest store to the mp IPI register on an unmodified `-E testsh` kills the host synchronously, so `step` sees it: measured 2/7 pre-fix, 7/7 post-fix with a LIVENESS control (asymmetric 0x12345678) and a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control (DEV_MP_MEMORY -&gt; 0x04000000) green in both directions. The earlier &#x27;step 40000 on the TMU route&#x27; design was REFUTED before it was built -- that route is host-timer driven and debugger.c stops host timers across a step, so it would have scored a correct pre-fix build ALIVE. Two TMU rows cover it FREE-RUNNING instead, and they kill a partial fix that repaired only the testsh route. The accept row is failable by measured kill: 6/7 under `if (prio &lt;= 1) return;`.</span></span><span class="state st-closed" title="regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — REFUTED against two manual sentences quoted verbatim (19.2 at :39886, 19.3.1 at :40192) and corroborated four ways. Route B is the ranking-changer: machine_test.c:99 wires the whole test-machine interrupt tree to IRQ15, which cpu_sh.c:255&#x27;s loop never assigns AND whose own formula yields 0 -- so the SH test machine&#x27;s interrupt controller cannot be used at all. Fix is a bare `return`; the sh4_val_first latch idiom deliberately does NOT apply, because priority 0 is a MODELLED state and not an unmodelled feature, so a latched diagnostic would fire on correct guest behaviour.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Scoped the defect and UPGRADED it: found route B, where one guest store on -E testsh ends the host and no guest register write can prevent it. Also corrected the carrier&#x27;s step-visibility rule -- correctly, as far as it went; grok then narrowed it further.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — Named the partial-fix mutant class -- a guard that repairs only the route the witness drives. Built and measured: it scored 4/4 before the TMU rows existed, and 4/6 after. Also named the `fatal(); return;` mutant that the quiet row now kills.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SHIP-AS-DESIGNED. Verified every manual citation in the brief by opening it; originated nothing and got nothing wrong. Its role here is verifier, not finder. Outcome UNVERIFIED: its claims were read but not individually checked, and &#x27;answered&#x27; is a different fact from &#x27;was right&#x27;.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — *** REFUTED THE ENTIRE WITNESS DESIGN, and the carrier fact it rested on. *** Found that debugger.c:800 stops host timers with `step` returning at :835-836 BEFORE timer_start() at :840, so the TMU route cannot assert under stepping. The drafted `step 40000` witness would have scored a CORRECT PRE-FIX BUILD ALIVE and failed vacuity floor clause (a) in silence. Verified by opening the file.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SHIP-AS-DESIGNED. Endorsed the fix as faithful to the manual -- priority 0 is a modelled state meaning masked, and the reset value of IPRA-IPRC. Outcome UNVERIFIED: its claims were read but not individually checked, and &#x27;answered&#x27; is a different fact from &#x27;was right&#x27;.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN. Called the exit(1) a host kill triggered by CORRECT guest behaviour, and endorsed the bare return. Outcome UNVERIFIED: its claims were read but not individually checked, and &#x27;answered&#x27; is a different fact from &#x27;was right&#x27;.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: SHIP-AS-DESIGNED. Argued the bare return against both alternatives: silence is the DEFINED behaviour of priority 0, so a diagnostic would fire on correctness. Outcome UNVERIFIED: its claims were read but not individually checked, and &#x27;answered&#x27; is a different fact from &#x27;was right&#x27;.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN. Agreed on every substantive point and specifically flagged that the existing comment is wrong, not merely the code. Outcome UNVERIFIED: its claims were read but not individually checked, and &#x27;answered&#x27; is a different fact from &#x27;was right&#x27;.  [answer: panel_20260822_162603]</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">HELD</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;HELD&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-22 — HELD for the flagship regress seat. THE QUESTION, stated so it does not have to be reconstructed from the row&#x27;s subject: #455 ships with TWO measured escapes FILED as coverage debt rather than fixed -- M4 clears SH_INT_ASSERTED and returns, and M5 deletes the guard outright. Both score 4/4 against the shipped rows because NO row reads the pending bit or the arbitration state. The disposition rests on the detector being above the vacuity floor, which it is: (a), (b) and (c) all measured green. (1) Is FILE the right call for both, or does M4 warrant an in-round row, given that the round&#x27;s own comment argues leaving SH_INT_ASSERTED set is REQUIRED rather than merely correct -- because clearing it would make sh4prioraise unfixable? A sole carrier of a required property with no row is the L3 shape. (2) The accept row&#x27;s failability was discharged by a MEASURED KILL (6/7 under `if (prio &lt;= 1) return;`). The rule asks for the least-observable growth form that is non-equivalent at the site; I argued guard-only IS that form because there is no latch operand at all -- the fix is a bare `return`. Is &#x27;no latch operand, so guard-only by default&#x27; sound, or does the absence of a latch mean the rule needs a different reading at sites like this? That is a RULE question and changes how future de-fatalising rounds are built.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4irq15</span><span class="ttl">sh4irq15  machine_test.c picks the one INTEVT line that is priority 0 by construction -- and sh4prio0&#x27;s fix turns that from loud to silent</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">sh4irq15  machine_test.c picks the one INTEVT line that is priority 0 by construction -- and sh4prio0&#x27;s fix turns that from loud to silent — commits: 9d5fd69 — HOLD: regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry — Author self-review disclosed. Shipped with a detector whose rows were each justified by a MEASURED mutant kill, and gate 10 green at 89 checks. Two rows kill different families: reverting to IRQ15 is caught by `deliv` alone, while IRQ13 and IRQ0 both DELIVER and are caught by `imask` alone. This fix silently voided two of #455&#x27;s rows; they were repurposed rather than dropped, and #455&#x27;s priority-0 coverage moved to the landisk TMU rows. — witness: probe:regress/sh_intprio_probe.py rows `irq14 deliv` / `irq14 imask` (rung 3, SHIPPED). One guest store to the mp IPI register on an unmodified -E testsh, free-running with a breakpoint at VBR+0x600: measured NOT DELIVERED pre-fix, DELIVERED post-fix, with two negative controls (no trigger store -&gt; no spurious breakpoint; IMASK=15 -&gt; refused). The imask row brackets the level to exactly 1, which is what pins IRQ14 rather than &#x27;some line delivered&#x27; -- IRQ13 and IRQ0 both deliver and were measured escaping a delivery-only witness.</span></span><span class="state st-closed" title="regress HELD for the flagship seat -- see the queue entry">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-29 — CONFIRMED — VERDICT ON THE KILL-RECORD TIME-INDEXING RULE: **SOUND, AND NOT ONE INSTANCE.**
17174:WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM: `scifonly` and `ipronly` build byte-for-byte identical binaries, so the entire behaviourally-witnessable payload is the SCIF re-source. The other nibbles make the model&#x27;s table match the manual for sources nothing can currently raise -- worth doing, but a judgement about scope, not a measurement. Escapes FILED as sh4iprunpinned (eight shapes, five seats). — witness: boot:landisk (rung 4) + probe:regress/sh_iprbcd_probe.py (rung 3, 14 rows, SHIPPED). The boot witness is load-bearing: an instrumented 90s `-E landisk` boot of openbsd76-landisk-bsd.rd shows the guest writing IPRC=0x00c0 (SCIF priority 12) as `scif0: cons` attaches, while the model delivered priority 3 on all fifteen SCIF asserts. Probe measured 6/10 pre-fix (vector 1,1,1,0,0,1,0,1,0,1, reproduced identically twice) and 14/14 post-fix, with a LIVENESS control (0x12345678 through a guest store) and a DEVICE-SIGNATURE control (IPRD reset value 0xda74 via P4) green in both directions. NOTE the `naive` row -- write one IPR, expect delivery -- is GREEN pre-fix and ships as a LABELLED row rather than a witness; what discriminates is IMASK BRACKETING.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-22 — SCOPE CORRECTED AGAINST MY OWN BRIEF, which had merged sh4iprbcd and sh4prioraise into one round. The seat judged it TWO rounds and is right on three grounds I checked: the semantic classes differ (one consumes the wrong REGISTER SET, the other fails to refresh DERIVED STATE); the witness shapes differ in operation ORDER, so the brief&#x27;s own &#x27;one witness covers both&#x27; claim refutes itself the moment the witness is written down -- A is write-IPR-then-assert, B is assert-while-masked-THEN-write; and **the project had already adjudicated it**, in the sh4prioraise filing itself, which says &#x27;a second design question, its own round&#x27;. Merging would have silently overturned a filed disposition, and the same rule had just been used to EXCLUDE a second site in #456 -- so bending it here would have been bending it for convenience. [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">HELD</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;HELD&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-23 — HELD -- IN FLIGHT, not skipped. The measuring seat is building and running right now; it is the one seat a panel cannot parallelise, because it compiles. Its brief asks the two questions the reading seats could not settle between them: which sources a probe can actually DRIVE (synchronous vs host-timer, per #455&#x27;s discriminator), and whether either rig&#x27;s guest ever writes IPRC -- which decides whether re-sourcing SCIF silently kills its interrupt path, the de-escalation shape. Recorded as held rather than left blank, because in flight and never fired must not look alike. [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Answered 8.4 KB with no parseable verdict line -- a FORMAT miss, recorded as such and NOT as a seat failure. Its content was not individually checked, so the outcome stays UNVERIFIED rather than being assumed from the round&#x27;s direction.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — VERDICT: SHIP-AS-DESIGNED. Verified every cited claim against the source tree and the manual, as it reliably does; originated no independent finding. The verifier role again -- rarely first to a defect, rarely wrong.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN, and it found the thing that matters most: *** THE PROPOSED WITNESS IS ONE THE UNFIXED BINARY ALREADY PASSES. *** It also named the wrong-SCIF-register problem for every CPU this tree models, flagged that &#x27;read Table 19.6&#x27; is insufficient because several sources have no SH4_INTEVT_* constant and IPRD aliases the encoded-IRL table, and pointed at the stale function comment at cpu_sh.c:232-245. Four findings, all checkable, in 9 KB.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN, 108 KB -- AND THIS ANSWER ONLY EXISTS BECAUSE THE OWNER NOTICED. The first invocation WALLED mid-answer (403, usage limit) after 36 KB, ending immediately after &#x27;especially where intc_intpri08 gets written, which decides Q1&#x27; -- it had identified the crux and was cut off. panel.sh could not see that wall at all (it only caught the echo-only shape); seat_verdicts.py could, by position. Re-fired an hour later it answered in full. A wall is a MOMENT, not a state.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN, but its headline answer to Q1 is FALSE and confidently so: &#x27;There is no contradiction. INTPRI08 *is* IPRC&#x27;, with a mapping table of INTPRI00-0F onto IPRA-D. *** REFUTED BY ENUMERATION: `grep -oiE &quot;INTPRI[0-9A-F]{0,2}&quot;` over the SH7750 manual returns INTPRI00 sixteen times and NOTHING ELSE -- no INTPRI04, 08 or 0C exists in either manual. *** The table was invented. Graded REFUTED on the load-bearing claim; the seat is packet-fed and cannot check repo or manual facts, which is exactly why its answers are never counted as evidence about them.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN. Correct and economical on Q1 -- &#x27;Table 19.6 is authoritative for the SH7750 family: SCIF priority is IPRC[7:4] ... INTPRI08 is a different register block and does not supply SCIF priority on these parts&#x27;, with the right variant note (IPRD is the SH7750S/R-only one). 2.2 KB, and the shortest correct answer in the panel. It answered ONE ROUND on scope, which the records seat then refuted from the filing.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — VERDICT: DEFECT-IN-DESIGN. Right that the answer is part-dependent in general and right that the code is correct for at most one part -- but it built that on INTC2/INTPRI existing for some variant, and no INTPRI register other than INTPRI00 appears in either manual in this tree. The instinct was sound and the specific register claim is unsupported here.  [answer: panel_20260822_230128]</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Liveness token returned. Caught FOUR citation errors in this round&#x27;s own corrections, all verified: :40142 -&gt; :40144; the source-row range :40083 excluded the H-UDI row at :40081 that it was citing; :40247 -&gt; :40248; and dev_sh4.c:2379-2381 -&gt; :2386-2388, WHICH THIS ROUND HAD SHIFTED ITSELF by inserting a comment above the line minutes after citing it correctly. Also made the scope call that closed sh4prioraise, and asked for a dead-marker on the SH7760 table whose attribution sits thirty lines away -- the same distance defect the round had just fixed one table higher.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** THE HIGHEST-VALUE FINDING OF THE ROUND, AND THE ONLY ESCAPE THAT WAS NOT COVERAGE DEBT -- BECAUSE IT COMPILED AND MEASURED. *** `m_sciftxonly`, one identifier narrowing the IPRC SCIF loop to TXI alone, MEASURED 14/14 against the detector as it then stood. Unlike every other escape found this round it reaches a LIVE line: SCIF RXI (0x720) is asserted by dev_sh4.c:250-256 on RIE &amp;&amp; DR and connected at :1912 -- console input on landisk. It measured the consequence rather than arguing it: iprio_720 = 0x1f/delivered on the fix versus 0x10/refused on the mutant. Fixed in-round as a MEASURED FALSE PASS on live functionality; four rows now pin the loop&#x27;s whole membership. It ALSO independently rebuilt m_iprdread and reproduced 13/14 red-on-`iprd hold`, discharging vacuity-floor clause (b) for that pair from a second hand; found that `m_irqramp` passes the twelve-row detector and is killed only by the IPRD rows, proving they earn their keep beyond IPRD; supplied the SH7750 `fixed levels` footnote at :39956-39959 that is a STRONGER IPRD arm than either the round or any other seat had; noted that the SCIF rows print UNSTAGGERED and so are layout-immune corroboration of the headline claim; and caught that the rescan lift RE-ARBITRATES rather than being verbatim. Every mutant lived in a private WSL clone; build/ was never written and no .MUTANT sentinel was needed.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Answered substantively at 25,756 bytes -- squarely in the size range of its own quota-wall signature, so it was checked by CONTENT and found clean. Named the `i&lt;=SH_INTEVT_SCI_TEI` -&gt; `i&lt;` escape (valid, FILED) and objected to raw mutable settings exposure; the two arbitration temporaries were removed on that objection plus minimax&#x27;s.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Enumerated the uncovered-source escape family precisely and by slot -- RTC PRI 0x4a0, REF 0x580/0x5a0, the SCI block 0x4e0-0x540, H-UDI 0x600, GPIO 0x620, DMAC 0x640-0x6c0 -- with the correct reason each escapes. FILED as sh4iprunpinned rather than fixed, per the stopping rule.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — HIGHEST-YIELD SEAT OF THE ROUND. Three wrong records, each verified by opening the file: (1) the function&#x27;s OWN header table still said Reserved in ipra[3:0], iprb[7:4] and iprc[7:4] -- the three cells the diff maps -- twenty lines above the new code, so a reader trusting it would put SCIF back on INTPRI08; (2) &#x27;of all the Table 19.6 sources only SCIF is drivable at all&#x27; is false, TMU asserts at dev_sh4.c:585 from a host timer, and THE PROBE&#x27;S OWN HEADER had the accurate synchronous-vs-host-timer wording the whole time; (3) int_to_assert/int_level were exposed with a justification NO ROW HONOURS. It also nominated the ask-again mutant that measured true. Emitted no formatted VERDICT line at the top -- a FORMAT miss, recorded as such, not a seat failure.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Supplied the framing the round was missing: in IRLM=0 the hardware IGNORES IPRD, so reading it is NON-CONFORMANT even absent the measured PCIC masking -- the masking is the symptom, non-conformance the disease. Independently reached glm&#x27;s conclusion that the reset-value coincidence is suggestive rather than load-bearing. Both are in the comment now.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Correctly demoted this round&#x27;s own IPRD reset-value argument: the MEASURED variant score is load-bearing and the H&#x27;DA74 coincidence is secondary. Acted on -- the comment now records it as suggestive, NOT load-bearing. No formatted VERDICT line; FORMAT miss, not a seat failure.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** ITS HEADLINE SHIP CONDITION WAS WRONG AND TOOK TWO MINUTES TO CHECK. *** It required the SCIF loop change from `i&lt;=SH4_INTEVT_SCIF_TXI` to `i&lt;=SH4_INTEVT_SCIF_TEI`, calling the current form an off-by-one leaving SCIF TEI at priority 0. SH4_INTEVT_SCIF_TEI DOES NOT EXIST: sh4_exception.h:104-107 defines exactly four SCIF vectors, ERI/RXI/BRI/TXI, so the loop already covers all four and the edit would not compile. It had transplanted SCI&#x27;s vector set (which does end in TEI at 0x540) onto SCIF. Its IPRD analysis and its GPIOI escape were both correct -- a confident wrong headline from an otherwise-good seat.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Reached grok&#x27;s conclusion independently on int_to_assert/int_level being unearned detector surface, and asked for the IRLM refusal in dev_sh4.c to be cross-referenced to the IPRD decline in cpu_sh.c so a future change that accepts IRLM=1 meets the linked decision. Both done. The inlined-brief rule continues to hold for this seat.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">sh4prioraise</span><span class="ttl">sh4prioraise  Raising a priority never re-examines an already-pending request</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">sh4prioraise  Raising a priority never re-examines an already-pending request — commits: 7e5c0f6 bb05ce7 — *** CLOSED IN #458, WHICH THE ASSESS PHASE HAD RULED A SEPARATE ROUND. THE RULING WAS OVERTAKEN BY AN IMPLEMENTATION FACT, AND THE CHANGE IS RECORDED RATHER THAN MERGED QUIETLY. *** The pass-1 seat refused the merge correctly on the evidence it had -- different semantic classes, witness shapes differing in ORDER, and this row&#x27;s own filing saying &#x27;a second design question, its own round&#x27;. What that ruling could not know: rewriting priority nibbles of a table that may already hold a pending request leaves int_to_assert STALE, so the rescan is load-bearing FOR THE REGISTER FIX ITSELF. Measured corroboration: `rescanonly` builds identical to `pre` -- the rescan fixes nothing observable ALONE -- which is exactly why it cannot be deferred without leaving #458&#x27;s own table able to go stale. Separable as filings, not as code.
17181::334 JOINED, AND THE JOIN FAILED A TEST IT COULD HAVE BEEN RECORDED AS PASSING. It and the COND_DATA_AVAIL site are one predicate family, and shipping three of four would leave a device where a declined read is safe in one state and lethal in another. But the reopening rule prices &#x27;no new oracle&#x27;, and reaching :334 needed a new opcode, a new loop shape and a new state manipulation. Recorded as DELIBERATELY OVERRIDDEN rather than met. — witness: probe:regress/gdrom_data_probe.py (rung 3, 23 rows, SHIPPED). MEASURED 5/23 pre-fix and 23/23 post-fix, SAME SCRIPT, with the pre-fix build rebuilt from `git show HEAD:` into a private clone and grep-confirmed to still contain the old fatal text -- NOT from build/, which a seat had measured moving underneath it mid-review the previous round. All 17 pre-fix FAILs read `died`; none failed by a wrong value. Controls: LIVENESS (guest store then guest load), SH4 DEVICE SIGNATURE (IPRD reset 0xda74), and a GDROM-SPECIFIC mapping proof (a served GDROM_BUSY read rather than a paddr fault) -- all three green on both sides and each in its OWN session, so no control touches GDROM_DATA.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Caught that the pass-1 brief said THREE exit sites when the arm has FIVE -- my quote elided two behind ellipses, and the de-escalation clause enumerates every predicate that gated death, so a wrong count made that enumeration incomplete BY CONSTRUCTION. Also caught my overclaim that &#x27;a driver probing the device cannot avoid this&#x27;: drivers probe STAT/BUSY/COND, and NetBSD&#x27;s gdrom.c writes COND=0xa0 before ever touching DATA, so its first touch is legal by construction. At pass 2 it ruled on the re-pointed-regex question -- a pre-fix RED is NOT sufficient, because pre-fix reddens by PROCESS DEATH before any count is taken, so what defends a re-point is the surviving-but-wrong control. Its :334 FILE ruling was overturned by measurement and it was asked to defend that; it agreed on the merits while correctly noting the &#x27;no new oracle&#x27; test was failed, which is now recorded as an override.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] *** FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY FOUND THE SAME LIVE MUTANT, and that convergence is the finding. *** codex, glm, deepseek and minimax each arrived at `odata = 0xffff` -&gt; `odata = 0` at the declined-read sites passing all 14 rows, by four different routes, because NO ROW PINNED THE RETURNED VALUE -- the detector checked that the diagnostic fires once and that the process survives, and nothing else. kimi and grok asked for the same value-pinning row without naming the mutant. When four packet-fed and file-reading seats converge on one gap from different directions, that is not six opinions; it is one measurement taken six times.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP WITH CHANGES. *** NAMED THE ASK-AGAIN MUTANT THAT PASSED ALL 14 ROWS: *** change only the no-data branch&#x27;s `odata = 0xffff` to `odata = 0` and leave the diagnostic still claiming 0xffff -- exit behaviour, diagnostic text, count and latching all identical, so every row passes WHILE THE DIAGNOSTIC LIES. Also confirmed the deleted-arm and shared-latch mutants were now dead (the repaired oracle catches deletion; the 8-bit-store two-site case exposes a shared bit), and pushed back on a comment claiming named macros ENFORCE uniqueness -- they make reuse easier to audit, which is a weaker and truer claim.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP (GO). Verified the diff eliminates all four host-fatal initial-touch sites in GDROM_DATA, that per-site latch bits isolate the diagnostics, and that buffer bounds hold. Specifically checked the repaired `same site twice` rows now run TWO sequential accesses in one session, so they exercise the latch bit&#x27;s 0-&gt;1 transition rather than asserting a count that one access would also satisfy -- the vacuity bug that round had just fixed.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP WITH CHANGES. Named the escape that mattered most: restoring exit(1) on a WRONG-WIDTH READ ONLY passed all 14 rows, because a rebuild had wrapped the width rows around stores and lost the load variants pass 1 had. Also caught that :334 was shipped-but-undriven, that the accept row never read the register, and that no row pinned the declined VALUE -- four findings, four rows.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP WITH CHANGES. Asked whether any row asserts BOTH diagnostics from the double-touch store, and asked for a value-pinning row on a declined read (== 0xffff) -- the same gap three other seats reached independently. Also raised a PRE-EXISTING, explicitly non-blocking issue and was careful to scope it: an ASan build driving a read with an odd `data_len` reads `data[data_len]` one past, and the 16-bit path is IDENTICAL before and after this diff, so it must not be attributed to #460 later.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP. Reached the same ask-again mutant independently (`odata = 0xffff` -&gt; `0` at both declined-read sites passes all 14 rows) and stated the reason exactly: no row pins the RETURNED VALUE, so the suite cannot distinguish correct 0xffff pull-up emulation from a 0 return. Also argued the :334 join was required rather than optional -- fixing one branch would leave a latent exit(1) in a sibling branch of the same if-else tree.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP WITH CHANGES. Gave the join its clearest justification: COND_DATA_AVAIL and overread are the same predicate family -- &#x27;read when there is nothing to give&#x27; -- so fixing one would leave a declined read safe in one condition and lethal in another. Reached the same `odata = 0xffff` -&gt; `0` mutant independently.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_085034] SHIP. Its verdict was written `VERDICT: **SHIP**`, which the committed extractor COULD NOT READ -- that is the incident that opened verdictbold, so this seat&#x27;s answer would have been filed as a format miss. Confirmed the 0xffff-&gt;0 mutant and additionally suggested `|=` -&gt; `=` if no cross-site re-trigger row exists. Correctly classified the live mutant as a DETECTOR GAP, not a fix defect.  [RECORDED LATE, 2026-08-23: this panel was read and ACTED ON at the time -- its findings are in the pass-2 receipt for 98a3408 -- but the individual seat cells were never written. The gap surfaced only when a later round CITED the directory, pulling it into check_seats_read&#x27;s scope, and a first attempt to close it stamped one note across three seats, which section O correctly refused. These notes are from re-reading the files.]</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">verdictbold</span><span class="ttl">verdictbold  seat_verdicts.py cannot read a VERDICT line wrapped in markdown emphasis, so a correct answer is filed as a format miss</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">verdictbold  seat_verdicts.py cannot read a VERDICT line wrapped in markdown emphasis, so a correct answer is filed as a format miss — commits: 3ad72e0 e9f8c0c 8eeeea2 — *** THIS CLOSURE NOTE WAS ITSELF A WRONG RECORD AND A REGRESS SEAT REOPENED THE ROW FOR IT. Corrected in place. *** It said &#x27;closed with 17 selftest rows and 8 mutants&#x27;, credited commits 3ad72e0 and e9f8c0c only, and repeated a dead-class claim that had gone false. All three are fixed here: the row closes at 3ad72e0 e9f8c0c 8eeeea2 with **24 rows and 13 mutants, every mutant red**. Crediting the 17/8 state was the worst of the three, because 8eeeea2&#x27;s own commit message measured that state a NET REGRESSION (silently-wrong 57 -&gt; 79 across 406 modern seat answers) -- the closure credited the version its own round had condemned. AUTHOR SELF-REVIEW DISCLOSED (badge A) on the fix; every escape was found by an independent seat and then measured. WHAT THE TOOL DOES: it decides whether a panel is recorded as split or unanimous, so its failures propagate into every round record. It shipped for its whole life with no test. Defects found and fixed: markdown emphasis unreadable (a LIVE seat&#x27;s correct verdict filed as a format miss); a single-word capture that collapsed SHIP WITH CHANGES to SHIP and every OPTION A/B/C to OPTION, so EVERY SPLIT PANEL THIS PROJECT RAN WAS RECORDED AS UNANIMOUS; `VERDICT: Ship with changes` returning the single letter &#x27;S&#x27; -- not None, an actual token that would have been reported as the verdict; mid-sentence prose swallowing a real answer via first-line-wins; and a line-start-only anchor that rejected 52 of grok&#x27;s 58 answers, blanking a seat by the tool rather than by the seat. The general fix is REPORTING disagreement rather than resolving it -- last-wins was measured no better, and on this round&#x27;s own pass 2 it reports codex as DO NOT SHIP by reading an illustrative example. — witness: probe:tools/pipeline/seat_verdicts.py --selftest</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Found the false positive that mattered most and it was still live after the first round of fixes: `My verdict: the code is fine and the records are honest.` -&gt; `THE CODE IS FINE`. Case-insensitive matching made every mid-sentence &#x27;verdict:&#x27; a candidate, and first-line-wins means one sentence of preamble outranks the seat&#x27;s real answer -- *** AND THIS ROUND&#x27;S OWN BRIEFS ARE ABOUT THE VERDICT EXTRACTOR, so the very next panel would have hit it. *** Prescribed the line-start anchor and checked it against every row first. Also caught that all five case-insensitivity rows tested the ACCEPT side only, so the selftest was green while the extractor turned prose into a verdict -- a row set that only proves what SHOULD match is half a detector.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** MEASURED THE FIX A NET REGRESSION, over 953 real seat files in 144 panel dirs. *** Scored committed against mine on the 406 modern answers: silently-wrong rose 57 -&gt; 79. The loosening genuinely fixed its two target shapes and CONVERTED LOUD FAILURES INTO QUIET ONES -- a mangled token or a bare None is something a human sees and checks; a plausible verdict lifted from the wrong line is not. Named the exact live instance (panel_20260823_085034/kimi.txt:4, a bullet re-rendering the brief&#x27;s menu, which defeats all three guards and reported SHIP for a seat that wrote SHIP WITH CHANGES three times). *** AND MEASURED THAT LAST-WINS IS NOT THE FIX *** -- on this round&#x27;s own pass 2 codex quotes a DO NOT SHIP inside an illustrative example below its real answer, so last-wins reports codex as DO NOT SHIP; it declined to claim its own 94%-vs-91% edge, saying the proxy was itself wrong on an inspected case. Recommended REPORTING the disagreement instead, which is this file&#x27;s own doctrine applied to itself and needs no guess about which line is the answer. Adopted. Also: the check_score_claims pin count was a DISPLAY not an assertion, and the whole check can be disarmed for a probe with no behaviour change at all by quoting the pin literal, since regress/lib.sh:82 compares as a string either way. Also found a live duplicate-stem collision (`idle` pinned 9 in gate_arm.sh and 3 in gate_m88k_rounding.sh, last-write-wins by sorted filename).</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP WITH CHANGES. Named the same-line-prose case (`VERDICT: SHIP I think this is ready.` -&gt; `SHIP I THINK THIS`), conflicting verdicts, and fenced-code quoting as missing rows -- the first two measured true and are FILED as verdictprose; the split reporting makes them loud rather than silent. Its structural ask is the sharpest: provenance for a score claim should come from SYNTAX, not numerical proximity -- an explicit docstring marker rather than a distance heuristic. That is what the &#x27;&lt;N&gt;-row&#x27; era escape now does, arrived at independently. Asked that section U not be blocking until parsing is fail-closed and the checker has tests; the checker now has both a pinned coverage floor and negative controls, so the substance is met even though the check stays hard.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  GO-WITH-CHANGES. Named `**VERDICT**: SHIP` -- emphasis on the LABEL rather than the value -- which the fix had missed entirely, plus quoted values and mixed case. All measured true and all fixed. Also flagged probe-stem resolution and collision bugs in check_score_claims.py; the collision was real and live (`idle` pinned 9 in gate_arm.sh and 3 in gate_m88k_rounding.sh).</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP WITH CHANGES. *** FOUND THE IRONY THAT MATTERED: the docstring EXAMPLE in check_score_claims.py was itself a stale score claim *** -- a real pin with a real number, inside the tool built to stop numbers rotting, and &#x27;nothing reads that file, so it will rot in the tool that exists to stop this&#x27;. De-numbered to a placeholder. Was explicitly honest that its shell invocations were blocked, so it did NOT run the selftest and took those figures from the brief -- a READING, declared as one, which is the form this project asks for.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP. Proposed a numerator sanity guard (`int(a) &lt;= int(b)`) as a one-line kill for the confirmed false-positive case, and quoted the tool&#x27;s own docstring back at it -- a check that false-positives is how a check gets ignored. Endorsed clause (d) with scope amendments and asked for the docstring to DISCLOSE its blind spots (numerator blindness, the +/-6 window, the chilling effect) rather than imply full coverage; the PASS line now states how many stems actually have a docstring checked.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP WITH CHANGES. Called the date false-positive BLOCKING and was right -- `measured 2026/08/23` returned (2026, 08) and would have been compared against a row count. Also asked the PIN regex be hardened against single-quoted gates or the double-quote assumption documented. Endorsed the selftest explicitly for the right reason: that it caught a defect in its own spec on the first run is a sign it discriminates rather than rubber-stamps.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP WITH CHANGES. *** MEASURED THE &lt;=6 WINDOW INDEFENSIBLE USING THIS PROJECT&#x27;S OWN #458 INCIDENT: *** pin 31 against a docstring claiming 10/12 is a difference of 19, so the check ignores it -- &#x27;that is exactly the class of error the check was built for&#x27;. Proposed flagging any denominator less than the pin, which is what shipped. Also named lowercase and the em dash as missing verdict forms; both measured true and both fixed.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_173510]  SHIP WITH CHANGES. Its objection to clause (d) is the one the flagship seat independently reached: the &#x27;reads like the same check&#x27; boundary is not ship-ready, and it should either be tightened or accepted as a ONE-ROUND EXPERIMENT with the wording settled before the next round opens. Recorded as a rule question for the flagship queue rather than resolved here. Also asked the docstring state the date false-positive class explicitly so a future operator does not chase a date mismatch.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** REOPEN, on two MEASURED wrong records -- and the first is the sharpest finding of the round because it shows an absence record expiring. *** (1) THE M6 &#x27;DEAD CLASS&#x27; RECORD WENT FALSE WITHIN ONE COMMIT. At e9f8c0c a mutant aimed at a leading emphasis class could not be made to fail, because re.search was UNANCHORED -- true, measured, and correctly recorded as an absence. Then 8eeeea2 ANCHORED the label and introduced a NEW leading class in the anchor branch, broader than the one that died (it also admits `-`, `&gt;`, `#`), and nothing re-ran the death measurement. The seat checked out e9f8c0c to verify the original warrant, then measured HEAD: dropping the new class REDDENS `label emphasis`. The class was alive, load-bearing, and covered by NO mutant -- one of only four rows the entire shipped table could not redden, the other three being deliberate controls. Re-verified independently before acting; M6 reinstated against the class that actually exists, comment rewritten as death-then-resurrection. (2) This closure note credited the 17/8 state that 8eeeea2 had measured a net regression, and omitted 8eeeea2 from the commit list. Corrected above. ALSO NAMED AND FIXED: the sentence-boundary branch had no mutant, so the grok row&#x27;s only provable failure ran through M2&#x27;s multiword capture -- attributable to the WRONG feature. M13 added; it also documents the 52-of-58 seat-blanking as a single-edit restoration. ON THE RULE QUESTION -- ADOPT, and it is not a generalisation from two cases: a mutant that will not die has exactly three explanations (mis-built mutant, dead target, mis-cut row), this round measured which one all three times and got three DIFFERENT dispositions, and the carrier already treats non-dying mutants as results twice (#449 measured EQUIVALENT, #451 the opposite). MULTI-PART AS DEFAULT: NO -- default stays smallest-single-edit, with multi-part licensed in exactly two shapes: verbatim reinstatement of what shipped (floor clause (b) applied to a harness tool), and reach-the-guard composites where EVERY added edit is first measured GREEN ALONE. That second condition answers the unfalsifiability objection: an unconstrained composite can be steered until it fails for an unrelated reason; a composite whose parts are proven inert can only fail through the interaction being claimed. ON verdictstate: FILING WAS CORRECT, not a floor breach -- the floor grades the detector shipped WITH THE FIX, and the fix is verdict_of. It attacked the state machine anyway across all 981 corpus files: ALL 78 WALLED CLASSIFICATIONS ARE GENUINE, ZERO FALSE WALLS, so no measured false pass exists to license L2. It did find one live mis-bucket on 9 real files (a 429 routed to &#x27;transport error&#x27; when it is wall-shaped, and the remedies differ), filed into verdictstate with 78/9/0 as the golden baseline.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdefault</span><span class="ttl">gdromdefault  both `default:` arms of dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c ran exit(1) -- roughly 248 of 256 control offsets and 27 of 32 DMA offsets were host-fatal to a read OR a write at any width</span><sup class="b b-A">A</sup><span class="tip">gdromdefault  both `default:` arms of dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c ran exit(1) -- roughly 248 of 256 control offsets and 27 of 32 DMA offsets were host-fatal to a read OR a write at any width — commits: ba30e64 a7b103d — AUTHOR SELF-REVIEW DISCLOSED (badge A) on the fix; every escape was found by an independent seat and then MEASURED. Three panels ran (pass 1, pass 2, pass 3) plus two flagship reviews. 9/32 pre-fix, 32/32 post-fix, both measured AT 32 rows (pre461f.out); gate 10 PASS at 190 checks run alone (gate461f.log), 179 before this round. *** THE HOLD IS THE STORY OF THIS ROUND. *** The owner chose to hold it uncommitted for the Fable seat across two usage-limit failures, and that decision paid for itself four times: codex&#x27;s pass-2 DO-NOT-SHIP on a write alias the fix would have CREATED; opus5&#x27;s four mutants at a full 27/27; kimi&#x27;s per-direction diagnostic finding; and fable5&#x27;s undischarged L3 obligation, which no other seat named across two full panels. THE DEFECT: both `default:` arms ran exit(1) -- 21 control offsets sampled, 19 killed the host. Fixed with #443&#x27;s latched-bitmap idiom plus a decline. THE FIX ALMOST SHIPPED A WORSE DEFECT THAN IT CURED: the DMA tail stores through TRUNCATING division, so a declined byte write at +0x05 landed in dma_reg[1] (the DMA destination), with the same alias reaching length and count. `case 0x18` reads count and enable to arm, then destination and length -- so a guest could arm and AIM a transfer entirely through declined offsets. PRE-FIX those accesses exited, so the fix would have created the capability, and the round&#x27;s own comment claimed the opposite. Closed by an alignment guard whose soundness is an INJECTIVITY PROOF (on multiples of 4, off -&gt; off/4 is injective, so an aligned offset reaching the arm cannot share an index with any implemented case), independently re-derived by a seat before it was believed. FOUR MUTANTS SCORED A FULL 27/27 at pass 2 and are now dead. The one that mattered: `&amp; 3` -&gt; `&amp; 1`, one character, re-opening the alias -- and `dma alias`, the row written to pin that very repair, was STRUCTURALLY BLIND to it because it uses an odd address both guard forms drop. That measurement is what the owner adopted as vacuity-floor clause (d) NULL-REPAIR RED. FILED, NOT FIXED: gdromdmawidth (an ALIGNED narrow write to an IMPLEMENTED offset clobbers all 32 bits -- same family, and this round&#x27;s new alignment rule explicitly does NOT cover it, so a future reader applying that rule would conclude the site is safe) and gdromcondlies. Eight exit(1) sites remain live in the file and are named individually, because half a census reads as a whole one. — witness: probe:regress/gdrom_default_probe.py</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — *** SEAT ALIVE -- REVIVED BY THE OWNER&#x27;S /login, NOT BY THE CALENDAR, WHICH IS THE THIRD TIME THIS PROJECT HAS MEASURED THAT PATTERN. *** Two firings earlier the same day returned the usage limit; the owner ran /login and the next firing returned BOTH liveness tokens (prompt nonce and a verbatim CHECKPOINT.md quote), so prompt delivery AND file access are proven rather than assumed. VERDICT: SHIP WITH CHANGES. Q1 -- INDEPENDENTLY RE-DERIVED the alignment rule&#x27;s injectivity proof and verified it against the real case labels in BOTH switches, including the load-bearing set that case 0x18 reads and the 8-byte-access path; also checked the CONTROL window needs no such rule because its default arm has no store tail. Q2 -- `both windows` is an acceptable sole carrier (measured sole kill, not a vacuous one), but it drove READS ONLY, so a DIRECTION-ASYMMETRIC cross-wiring would pass all 31 rows; upgraded in-round under L1 to four accesses expecting 2/2, at zero new-session cost. Q3 -- SEVEN WRONG RECORDS, every one confirmed by grep or by reading before it was acted on. The sharpest: the gate comment&#x27;s boast that its numbers were &#x27;MEASURED at the final row count, not carried over&#x27; was ITSELF carried over and false three ways (final count is 32 not 27; no 7/27 measurement ever existed; the honest pre-fix number is 8 because dma seed passes pre-fix) -- the third generation of the frozen-number defect, inside the sentence memorialising the first two. Also: the refuted &#x27;two fields that authorise a transfer&#x27; claim stood verbatim in THREE files because mechanical rule 2 (grep for siblings) was not run; the &#x27;neither subsumes the other&#x27; heading survived in two places after its own body called it false; &#x27;accept8 ... one session&#x27; is refuted by the loop eight lines below it; &#x27;nine rows use pair()&#x27; when there are fifteen call sites; a gate claim that a host kill is &#x27;otherwise unreachable&#x27; when it is reachable through the front door via two implemented cases; and the detector heading wrong in every field, with &#x27;gate 10 PASS&#x27; never true at any pin at or above 25. Q4 -- FOUND THE ROUND&#x27;S UNDISCHARGED DE-ESCALATION OBLIGATION (L3): accept8 exists for the control window because three narrowing mutants scored full marks, and the DMA window got the same fatal-&gt;survive fix with NO accept row. Traced it: delete `case 0x18:` and the DMA trigger silently dies forever, where before this round that deletion was a loud exit on first touch. `dma accept` added. Also caught the gate&#x27;s named-row list at 27 entries against 32 rows, missing exactly the five highest-value rows -- every one added BECAUSE a mutant escaped. And that the exit census names 4 of 8 survivors, which reads as a whole census.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — PASS-2 KILL TABLE AT THE 31-ROW PIN, measured in a private clone restored cmp-clean afterwards: fix 31/31; pre-fix HEAD 8/31 (23 rows red -- the round&#x27;s headline number, MEASURED at the shipped row count rather than carried over from a smaller detector); m_dmarevert (verbatim reinstatement at the DMA site) 22/31, so floor clause (b) is satisfied at BOTH fixed sites; m_oob 23/27 with scan uniq = 15. *** THE FOUR ESCAPES THIS SEAT FOUND AT 27/27 ARE NOW ALL KILLED: *** m_align1 29/31 (dma align16, dma rdleak), m_rdleak 30/31 (dma rdleak SOLE killer), m_wrongarr 30/31 (both windows SOLE killer), m_ctlwrongarr 28/31. m_align1 is the one that matters -- a ONE-CHARACTER edit, &amp; 3 -&gt; &amp; 1, re-opened the alias the round had just closed, and `dma alias` (the row written to pin that very repair) was STRUCTURALLY BLIND to it because it uses an odd address that both guard forms drop. That measurement is what the owner adopted as vacuity-floor clause (d) NULL-REPAIR RED.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3. Asked that an explicit write-direction condition be added around the DMA arming at `case 0x18`, and said the &#x27;+0x18 semantic gap should be closed before treating gate 10 as a shipping record&#x27;. *** CHECKED IN TWO MINUTES RATHER THAN JUDGED, AND IT IS ALREADY SATISFIED. *** The arm tests `idata != 0` with no direction test of its own -- so the concern is well-aimed -- but `idata` is declared `= 0` and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` 140 lines above, so on a read it keeps its initialiser and the arm cannot fire. A real, local guarantee, not an accident of memory_rw memsetting the caller&#x27;s buffer. NO CODE CHANGE; the probe comment now names the actual property, because a guard 140 lines from what it protects is correct and hard to see, and a careful seat did not see it. Its other calls were right: the store asymmetry is NOT a defect (control has no shadow-register convention, DMA does, so each matches its own window), and the bitmap arithmetic is correct in the final state -- while noting `sizeof(arr) * 8` is brittle under a future element-type change, which is exactly the mutant the measuring seat was asked to build.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3, GO. Verified the whole final state by citation rather than assertion: the unaligned guard at dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c:878-882 quoted verbatim, the bitmap arithmetic (`1u &lt;&lt; (k &amp; 31)`, word `k &gt;&gt; 5`, bound via `sizeof(arr) * 8`), all 32 rows enumerated in the gate, and -- the one worth having -- it independently re-derived that the nine pre-fix survivors all touch IMPLEMENTED offsets, which is the round&#x27;s own explanation for why 9 and not 8.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3 -- IN FLIGHT at the time this was recorded, not absent. Its file showed the opening plan and the first four tool steps: reading both switch tails, the remaining exit(1) sites, `relative_addr`&#x27;s type, how memory_rw treats unaligned 32-bit accesses, and whether the DMA accept path can arm on a read. That last question is the one codex raised and it was settled independently while grok was still working. Recorded as MID-ANSWER rather than as a seat failure, which is the distinction this project has mis-made before: a short file is not a dead seat.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3. Did not assert a verdict it had not earned -- it named FOUR measurements (M1-M4) whose outcomes would decide between SHIP and an escape, and said so explicitly. *** M4 CHECKED IMMEDIATELY AND CONFIRMED: *** it asked for one-line confirmation that the DMA `odata` preload and the tail store index the SAME slot -- :749 is `odata = d-&gt;dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)]` and :889 is `d-&gt;dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)] = idata`, character-identical index expressions, so `return 1 -&gt; break` has no silent read-path variant. Its M2 (whether `dma accept` and `dma seed` jointly assert quiescence AND write/readback at all five implemented offsets) is the sharpest residual and is the same gap a flagship seat raised about read-only accept rows.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3. *** FOUND THE MACRO&#x27;S REAL CONSTRAINT, and it is the worst kind of brittleness because the obvious sanity check passes: *** `sizeof(arr) * 8` is element-type-AGNOSTIC and computes a correct BIT COUNT for any array, but gdrom_unimpl_first takes `uint32_t *bits` and indexes `bits[k &gt;&gt; 5]` with `1u &lt;&lt; (k &amp; 31)`. A uint8_t[] would give the right count and the wrong indexing. Now stated in the source (codex flagged the same brittleness independently; C11 _Static_assert is unavailable in this C99 tree). Also asked for a `relative_addr &amp; 3` guard on the CONTROL arm as insurance -- DECLINED WITH A REASON rather than overlooked: that arm has no store tail, so the guard would be DEAD CODE, and this round already rejected one dead bounds check for exactly that reason. The invariant it depends on is now written down instead, with the instruction that adding a tail requires re-deriving the injectivity argument there.</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3, ship. *** THE ONLY SEAT ASKED FOR A NEW 32/32 MUTANT THAT SAID PLAINLY IT COULD NOT FIND ONE, and enumerated what it had eliminated: *** `&amp; 3`-&gt;`&amp; 1` caught by `dma align16`, the write-restricted guard caught, cross-keyed arms caught, and `break` instead of `return 1` re-opens the alias and is caught. An honest empty result with its search space named is worth more than a speculative find, and it is the packet-fed seat that gave it. Also reached the correct read-safety answer for +0x18 independently (&#x27;guarded on idata/write direction&#x27;).</span></span></td><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — [panel_20260823_213058] PASS 3, ship. Reached the control-window invariant independently of glm and asked for the same thing -- that the assertion be COMMENTED, both to document the current invariant and to flag that the injectivity argument needs re-deriving if a store tail is ever added. Done, in those words. Also asked that the injectivity reasoning sit next to the bitmap helper for a maintainer who adds an implemented DMA offset or resizes the bitmap; it is at the DMA arm, which is where the truncation it defends against lives.</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromdmadefault</span><span class="ttl">gdromdmadefault  the second `default:` kill in the same device, in the separately-registered DMA window -- named in no filing and no brief</span><span class="tip">gdromdmadefault  the second `default:` kill in the same device, in the separately-registered DMA window -- named in no filing and no brief — commits: 7c9dd9e — Verbatim reinstatement at the DMA site (m_dmarevert) measured 22/31, killing NINE rows (an earlier draft said five, which was the 27-row-era count from a different table -- two eras fused in one sentence) -- vacuity-floor clause (b) satisfied at BOTH fixed sites, which is what licenses closing this as part of #461.  COMMITS SET 2026-08-25 from a substance read of git. This row was raised by a seat ENUMERATING the file and joined #461/#462 on a MEASUREMENT; the fix and its detector shipped at `7c9dd9e`, which touches `src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c` and adds `regress/gdrom_default_probe.py` -- the probe this row&#x27;s `witness` field already names. The field was empty because the work shipped under the joined round&#x27;s id, which is the duplicate-close shape; naming the carrying commit is strictly better than an empty field, which cannot be told from &#x27;not recorded&#x27;. — witness: probe:regress/gdrom_default_probe.py — note:   *** L4 -- `dma alias` WAS ATTRIBUTED TO THE WRONG ARM IN THE PROBE&#x27;S OWN HEADER.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  `gdrom_default_probe.py` said the two byte stores into the DMA window were `dma alias` (+0x05) and `arm bytetrig` (+0x18) &quot;and BOTH were on the ARMING arm&quot;.  **`+0x05` falls to the DMA window&#x27;s `default:` arm**, and the DEVICE&#x27;S OWN SOURCE says so in a comment: *&quot;A byte access at +0x05 does not match `case 0x04` -- the switch tests&quot;* the whole address.  **THE CONCLUSION IS UNCHANGED AND THE CORRECTION STRENGTHENS IT:** the point is that no byte store ever reached the NO-DATA arm, which is still true; what was wrong is the tally of where the two byte stores DID land.  With `dma alias` on `default:`, implemented-case byte coverage was ONE row, not two -- **thinner than claimed, not thicker**. *Named by the codex seat in BOTH panel_20260917_140502 and panel_20260917_142508, in answers that were recorded nowhere until today.  The file it contradicts is the device&#x27;s own source, which had the right statement in a comment all along -- the structure-knew-and-prose-did-not shape, inside one round&#x27;s own artifacts.*  *** SECTION O REPAIR 2026-09-17: the seven seat cells on this row carried ONE STAMPED NOTE from `panel_20260917_100431`, a SIX-ROW late review.  Each is now that seat&#x27;s own answer, read from its own file. ***  **This was not tidying.  TWO REAL DEFECTS were sitting inside the stamp and neither had a cell of its own**: the `sh4_dmac_transfer` CHCR header L4 (grok, corrected in place) and `gdromdmaflood` (deepseek, filed MED and corroborated from source by codex).  *Section O&#x27;s boilerplate cluster and section G&#x27;s unread review are the same defect from two sides -- the stamp is what made the findings unfindable.*  **The seven answers were genuinely different**: seven distinct verdicts, from codex&#x27;s DO NOT RATIFY to minimax&#x27;s explicit refusal to overturn anything from the packet, and they disagreed about where the defects live -- glm called all four of its findings RECORDS defects, deepseek and codex found a product one.  A stamped note renders that as one voice.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c ok"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">✓</span><span class="tip">2026-08-23 — Found by a seat ENUMERATING the file rather than reading the round&#x27;s scope list, which said &#x27;two DMA sites&#x27; where the file has three. Joined #461 on a MEASUREMENT, not an inference: the window died on committed HEAD and died again on a build that fixed only the control window. Its stated join condition -- &#x27;add them, measure them RED on committed HEAD first&#x27; -- was met, not waived. Ships with nine DMA rows plus `both windows`. — record: 2026-09-23 — [NOT A SEAT ANSWER] Filed by GXEMUL [087a90] (main loop) from the 2026-09-23 records audit (regress/artifacts/stateaudit_20260923/; seats codex GPT-6 Astra + grok 4.7 x2 rounds, fable 5.1 last, identity verified).  Entry [11]&#x27;s &#x27;FOUR APPLIED&#x27; is true of two sites and false of a third.  The `dma alias` correction IS at regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:160-166 and :1608-1610 (working tree only; HEAD has neither) and was MISSED at :1186-1187, which still says &#x27;BOTH are on the ARMING arm&#x27; -- and &#x27;:1186&#x27; calls that sentence &#x27;true when written&#x27;, which it never was (dma alias is a default:-arm store at +0x05).  A sibling miss, found by the audit&#x27;s reverse reconciliation and corrected in its wording by Fable 5.1 (the audit&#x27;s own brief had called it &#x27;never applied&#x27;, and both round-2 seats ratified that).  OWED with the uncommitted repairB/U5 work, by its owner; not edited by the audit.</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** M2_gdromnodatawidth: ONE ADDED LINE SCORES 60/60 -- A TOTAL ESCAPE. ***  `idata = 0;` -&gt; `if (len == 4) idata = 0;` IN THE NO-DATA ARM.  MEASURED by the Opus 5 seat in a scratch clone (/tmp/gxopus917): baseline NAMED (HEAD 9c91999), rebuilt in-clone before mutant 1, clone-local .MUTANT sentinel, cmp against SEC plus [binary -nt source] verified on every build, GXEMUL-SEC untouched.  Artifacts preserved durably at regress/artifacts/seataudit_20260917/opus_measured_20260917 -- and its README FLAGS base_gdrom.raw as CONTAMINATED (59/60, CONTROL=DEAD, run concurrently with the lca_isalane kill table); base2_gdrom.raw is the valid 60/60 baseline.  SIBLING PARITY, and it is the pre-table check&#x27;s own row firing: the probe contains EXACTLY TWO byte stores into the DMA window -- `dma alias&#x27; (+0x05) and `arm bytetrig&#x27; (+0x18) -- and BOTH are on the ARMING arm.  **No byte-width store has ever reached the NO-DATA arm, which is the arm the fix was primarily written for.**  NON-EQUIVALENCE CONTROL (byte trigger at +0x18, count=1, enable=1, d-&gt;data NULL, read back): base reads 0x0 (declined AND cleared), M2 reads 0x5a -- THE TRIGGER STUCK IN THE SHADOW, so +0x18 READS BUSY FOREVER.  That is precisely the failure the fix&#x27;s own comment calls &#x27;THE idata = 0 IS LOAD-BEARING AND THE OBVIOUS FIX OMITS IT&#x27;, re-opened at byte width.  The #462 kill table (14 mutants) contains NO WIDTH MUTANT AT ALL.  Proposed repair costs ZERO new sessions and is the CLASS-CLOSURE form rather than one cell per pass: make the SECOND store in `nodata twice&#x27; a byte store, closing {arm} x {width} AND {arm} x {latch state} in one diff.  *** FLOOR-vs-DEBT is before the Fable adjudicator, not graded here. *** — evidence: regress/artifacts/seataudit_20260917/opus_measured_20260917/M2_gdrom.raw, base2_gdrom.raw (the VALID baseline), ctrl_base_m2.txt / ctrl_m2.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** L4 -- `dma alias` WAS ATTRIBUTED TO THE WRONG ARM IN THE PROBE&#x27;S OWN HEADER.  Corrected 2026-09-17. ***  `gdrom_default_probe.py` said the two byte stores into the DMA window were `dma alias` (+0x05) and `arm bytetrig` (+0x18) &quot;and BOTH were on the ARMING arm&quot;.  **`+0x05` falls to the DMA window&#x27;s `default:` arm**, and the DEVICE&#x27;S OWN SOURCE says so in a comment: *&quot;A byte access at +0x05 does not match `case 0x04` -- the switch tests&quot;* the whole address.  **THE CONCLUSION IS UNCHANGED AND THE CORRECTION STRENGTHENS IT:** the point is that no byte store ever reached the NO-DATA arm, which is still true; what was wrong is the tally of where the two byte stores DID land.  With `dma alias` on `default:`, implemented-case byte coverage was ONE row, not two -- **thinner than claimed, not thicker**. *Named by the codex seat in BOTH panel_20260917_140502 and panel_20260917_142508, in answers that were recorded nowhere until today.  The file it contradicts is the device&#x27;s own source, which had the right statement in a comment all along -- the structure-knew-and-prose-did-not shape, inside one round&#x27;s own artifacts.* — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_142508/codex.txt; src/devices/dev_dreamcast_gdrom.c decode comment</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S OWN S0 PREMISE *** -- pcc2c040assertrow HAS a dedicated brief (regress/artifacts/pcc2c040kill/ASSERTROW_brief.md) that codex answered. Also CONFIRMED the fatal()-is-a-print premise from debugmsg.c, and found the gdrom Transfering fatal unlatched on the success path. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/agy.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE REPAIR CHAIN CLOSES.  grok pass 3, asked to be HARDER on its own remedy than on pass 2. ***  THE ZERO WITH ITS DENOMINATOR, in the seat&#x27;s words: swept {no-data, arming, success} x {byte len=1, word len=4} at first-store / latch FRESH; all six cells have a row that holds them, each checked against the row&#x27;s actual opcodes rather than against the matrix prose; M2 61/62 reds only `nodata byte&#x27;, M3 61/62 reds only `accept byte&#x27;, base 62/62, artifacts named per leg and NOT RUN.out.  No empty CLAIMED cell; no clause-(d) clean-sweep from reading.  *** AND IT WAS NOT A RATIFICATION -- it found SIX same-commit L4s, and the sharpest is exactly the failure the brief asked it to hunt: I implemented its WORDS rather than its INTENT. ***  (1) I wrote that the (no-data, byte, SET) latch-scope is &#x27;predicted GREEN ON BOTH NEW ROWS&#x27; -- copied from the pass-2 framing.  FALSE: `accept byte&#x27; is on the SUCCESS arm and THE SUCCESS ARM HAS NO WARN-BIT LATCH, so a no-data latch-scope cannot touch it.  (2) The disclose list DROPPED the sibling cell pass 2 had named -- (arming, byte, SET) -- so I implemented the matrix shape and half the disclose list.  (3) The grading sentence ended &#x27;not as a floor breach&#x27;, which implements &#x27;width is not a (d) axis&#x27; as &#x27;a claimed-cell miss is only debt&#x27;.  CLAUSE (e) IS A FLOOR CLAUSE: a CLAIMED-cell escape is L2, a DISCLOSED-cell escape is outside the contract.  (4) two row comments still present-tense, one listing `dma alias&#x27; as an arming-byte cell when it is a `default:&#x27;-arm store at +0x05.  FOUR APPLIED in this commit; the remaining two (split old M3 from repair-B M3; date the `gdromdmasilent&#x27; escape record) filed as `gdromcontractresidual&#x27;. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/PASS3_enumverify_20260917.txt and BRIEF_enumverify.md; regress/gdrom_default_probe.py contract matrix; regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/repairB_20260917/  [citations re-pointed 2026-09-17 from _scratchpad/ to the durable artifact copies; the _scratchpad originals are the same bytes]</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** THE SHARPEST MUTANT OF THE PANEL: #491/diskimagesign -- swap the ternary arms so lentoread = max(len, remain). Zero warnings, casts intact, and when len &lt; remain lentoread EXCEEDS the caller&#x27;s len -- a real host OOB, invisible to every named detector, because the warning gate is the ONLY detector this row has. *** Also F1: #462&#x27;s &#x27;idata = 0 IS LOAD-BEARING&#x27; is asserted, not derived, once its NetBSD citation was retracted. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_kimi.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Challenged the brief&#x27;s (once)=41/7 census as blind to f532bb6&#x27;s qualified marker -- MEASURED RIGHT, and the shortfall is larger than it said (literal sees 41 of 71). Flagged 54a2eb4&#x27;s &#x27;FAST | MIEN&#x27; annotation and named the settling fact (the PCC2_MIEN value); RUN: PCC2_MIEN is 0x02, the same bit as PCC2_GENCTL_IEN, so the annotation is NUMERICALLY CORRECT and this finding resolves in the code&#x27;s favour. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_glm.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). Same gdrom unlatched-fatal finding, independently. Named the assert=0 -&gt; return one-token mutant that skips INTERRUPT_DEASSERT while pcc2c040assertrow stays green. Repeated the MIEN/IEN flag -- resolved in the code&#x27;s favour, see glm. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_deepseek.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — MANUAL — LATE REVIEW of the six rows the seat audit flagged (panel panel_20260917_100431). *** Called the assert=0 -&gt; return mutant &#x27;the highest-yield Q2 finding in the brief&#x27; *** -- it skips INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d-&gt;cpu_irq) and the shipped rows do not pin deassertion. Also: 54a2eb4 ships NO detector at all, which the brief explicitly asked to be told. Marker-spelling deviation CONFIRMED by measurement. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/ollama_minimax.txt; brief _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/brief_sent.md; seat check _scratchpad/panel_20260917_100431/SEATS.txt</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** REPAIR B IS DISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL.  base 61/61 -&gt; M2 60/61, and the ONLY row that moved is the new `nodata byte&#x27;: got 0x5a/1 against want 0x0/1. ***  Exact match to the prediction written before the run, down to the literal got-string.  `nodata twice&#x27; and `arm bytetrig&#x27; unmoved.  *** THE PAIRED ORACLE IS WHAT MAKES THE RESULT READABLE: `/1&#x27; says the no-data decline fired EXACTLY ONCE, so the byte store REACHED THE ARM and the mutant then declined to clear.  Without that half, 0x5a is ambiguous between &#x27;the mutant left the trigger stuck&#x27; and &#x27;the byte store never executed&#x27;, which need opposite responses. ***  That is the presence pin grok demanded when it REFUSED my in-place edit of `nodata twice&#x27; -- a form that could have been built wrong and still looked right, because on HEAD the second store is invisible.  CONTROLS: kernel present, probe frozen, a guard that ABORTS if the repair is absent, clone baseline ESTABLISHED from SEC, **anchor occurrences: 1** (the mutant hit the no-data arm and only it -- a four-tab anchor would have matched all THREE `idata = 0;&#x27; sites because a five-tab line CONTAINS a four-tab suffix), binary differs from base, clone restored cmp-clean, and GDROM_DEF_CONTROL=OK proving the instrument was live.  **GATE PIN MOVED 60 -&gt; 61 in the same change**, after the base leg measured 61 -- the pin follows the measurement.  It is a `check` (equality, fail-closed ratchet), not a `check_min`. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromdmadefault/repairB_20260917/ -- RUN.out (sentinel KILLREPAIRB_DONE), VERDICT.md, mutants_repairB.py, logs/probe_base.raw 61/61 CONTROL=OK, logs/probe_M2_gdromnodatawidth.raw 60/61</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromcondlies</span><span class="ttl">gdromcondlies (BADVAL PAIR ONLY -- see gdromcondavail for the other defect this id used to cover)  GDROM_COND and GDROM_UNKNOWN_0x88 exit(1) on an unexpected VALUE</span><span class="tip">gdromcondlies (BADVAL PAIR ONLY -- see gdromcondavail for the other defect this id used to cover)  GDROM_COND and GDROM_UNKNOWN_0x88 exit(1) on an unexpected VALUE — commits: c516a9c 439dfd0 105c15c — witness: probe:regress/artifacts/gdromcondlies/witness_20260830/gdrom_cond_witness.py -- RUNG 3, 15/15, both kills reproduced on the COMMITTED build via one guest mov.w each through real decode on an unmodified -E dreamcast; WIFEXITED status 1.  Logs witness_run1.out (GDROMCOND_WITNESS_RESULT=15/15) and extras_run1.out, both sentinelled — note:   *** PASS 1 FIRED 2026-08-30, panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260830_153427`.  NINE SEATS FIRED, NINE ANSWERED -- seven scriptable plus both Agent seats.  NOTHING DOWN. ***  `ollama_kimi` writes no file BY DESIGN since 2026-08-29: the kimi CLI is the seat again and `OLLAMA4` is deliberately blank, so its absence is the CONFIGURED STATE and not a lost seat -- a check that counts files in the panel dir will under-count here, and separately under-counts by two because the Agent seats leave no file at all.  kimi is recorded PARTIAL (still writing at 230 KB when the entries were written).  *** THE ROSTER SENTENCE LIVES HERE, ON THE ROW, BECAUSE IT IS A FACT ABOUT THE PANEL AND NOT ABOUT ANY SEAT. ***  It was first written as a stamped header on all nine seat entries and `check_boilerplate.py` refused the commit -- correctly, and the refusal is worth recording rather than just fixing: **a note shared verbatim by several seats records that they were FIRED, not that they AGREED**, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape arriving in the records instead of in the panel.  Nine identical prefixes would have rendered as nine seats saying the same thing, on the very round whose two Agent seats DISAGREED about scope.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Liveness token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` quote both returned, so prompt delivery and file access are proven rather than assumed.  Re-derived at HEAD rather than transcribing, and verified the device file is UNCHANGED since the witness&#x27;s pinned HEAD `8404520` (`git log 8404520..HEAD -- &lt;file&gt;` empty), so the 15/15 still describes the committed build.  VERDICT PROCEED -- same option-set member at both sites, one round -- **but the design as briefed is missing its two central detector obligations.**  (1) EVERY ACCEPT ROW IN THE WITNESS DISCRIMINATES ON SURVIVAL, and the fix makes survival universally green: carried into the detector unchanged they are VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION -- the CHANNEL check&#x27;s canonical bad row.  (2) The §5(a) constraint&#x27;s own null-repair mutant (`M_condstore`) is killed by NO row anywhere in the plan; the fresh-device COND readback row is the round&#x27;s single most load-bearing row and the brief never names it.  **Converged with opus5 INDEPENDENTLY on both the mutant and the row.**  Ran the PRE-TABLE CHECK against the design now: SIBLING PARITY found that `#490`&#x27;s default arm FIRES the completion event on decline while the DMA arm&#x27;s comment rules the OPPOSITE -- both precedents in this file, disagreeing because the wait states differ -- so the round owes a sentence choosing no-event WITH both citations, and by CLAIM-&gt;ROW that sentence owes a row.  Notes it is a semantic would-consequence claim, exactly the shape the verb-list grep walks past.  GUARD/BODY: `d-&gt;warned` has nine writers, six settable by ordinary guest reads, so the new pair owes its own `crossbit` row (`M3shared` is this file&#x27;s measured cost of skipping it).  Mechanical touchpoints the brief omitted: the disjointness assert at `:447-449` must be extended (compile error until it is -- self-enforcing, good), `GDROM_W_NBITS` grows to 11, and **the new bit must not be named anything CMD-like** because `GDROM_W_CMD` is taken and means the PACKET opcode decline.  §6: FILE both flood lines under `floodclass` -- neither is BADVAL, so the same-semantic-class arm fails and taking `:1089` alone would split the flood FAMILY instead.  **DISAGREES WITH OPUS5 HERE**, and the disagreement is recorded rather than settled by vote.  FOUND WHILE VERIFYING: `cond ef` has NO quiet companion row in the witness -- an asymmetry that becomes load-bearing in the detector. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_153427</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** THIS SEAT REFUTED THE BRIEF&#x27;S CENTRAL PREMISE BY MEASUREMENT, AND THE QUEUE HAD THE ANSWER ALREADY. ***  Built HEAD `cd1ffb6` clean at `/tmp/gxopus_condp1` (both files `cmp`-clean vs SEC, MAKE_RC=0, `[ binary -nt source ]` OK) and ran the COMMITTED `footbridge_sites_probe.py`: `ROWS=10 of 10 FOOTBRIDGE_SITES_PASS`, row K1 HOST-DIED exit=1 on one guest `ldr`.  Swapped the planted word three more times: **4 of 4** sampled footbridge sites end the host, each with its own diagnostic as attribution.  **And row `n6` was already open, witnessed CONFIRMED, ranked 6th of 8 on 2026-08-27.**  Traced the false sentence to `CHECKPOINT.md:15` and noted that `exit_census.py` PRINTS &#x27;WHERE TO LOOK, NOT WHAT IS BROKEN&#x27; -- the brief cited its number while drawing the conclusion the tool disclaims.  DESIGN, all measured on the baseline: §5(a) verified and WORSE than briefed -- `d-&gt;cond` readback returns the stored byte (`write 0xef -&gt; print r9 = 0xef`), `COND_DATA_AVAIL` is `0x08` at `:490`, and `:999` GATES THE WHOLE GDROM_DATA READ PATH on it, so a delete-only fix lets any guest byte unlock that path.  Named the clause-(d) mutant exactly: keep the latched decline and MOVE `d-&gt;cond = idata;` back to the top of the else -- one line, and **every row in the brief&#x27;s plan stays green**; only a COND readback row reds.  Added the clause-(f) anchor requirement: a fresh device has `cond == 0`, so &#x27;write 0x55, expect 0&#x27; is ALSO satisfied by a mutant assigning `d-&gt;cond = 0` on decline -- the row must drive an ACCEPTED `0xef` first, then the declined write, then require `0xef` back.  Second (d) mutant: `== 0xef` -&gt; `(idata &amp; 0xef) == 0xef`, silently widening