ON THE ROUND.  Engaged the other-kill-site question (2 hits) alongside 7 premise hits. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_153427</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — 230 KB and STILL WRITING when this entry was written -- a live `kimi` process in `tasklist`, ending mid-sentence with no wall notice.  **Recorded as a PARTIAL answer, neither a wall nor a completed seat**: its three quota-keyword hits are all it QUOTING ledger rows, which is the honest-correction-vs-assertion collision this project already records for greps, arriving here in a seat file.  What it had produced by then: it went HUNTING on premise 1 rather than ratifying (95 mentions of other kill sites), independently reproduced the §6 flood measurement from `extras_run1.out`, confirmed the `memory_rw.c` outlier characterisation exactly (double-gated, `debugmsg_cpu` not `fatal`, file-static and therefore global), and checked premise 2 against NetBSD trunk `gdrom.c` -- which writes only `0xa0` to COND, never touches `0x88`, and reads COND in `gdrom_intr` testing `0x08`.  **Its caveat is the useful part: `0xb` at `0x88` traces to the boot ROM, not to NetBSD.**  It also read the ledger and noticed `sgirekills`/`widthkillclass` had been opened THAT DAY by the brief&#x27;s own author, and drew the right conclusion -- the audit found others and filed them, so the brief&#x27;s completeness sentence was already stale when the panel fired. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_153427</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — PACKET-FED seat, real answer (verdict opens &#x27;THE TWO KILLS&#x27;), no thinking burn.  Can