ive kill survived because that driver never wrote a non-0xb value, not because such writes are unthinkable. Three seats, three levels: one accepted the premise, one called the inference weak, one ran git log -L. PREMISE 5 ALSO WRONG: removing these guards does NOT delete the announcement -- fatal() remains and keeps logging; only process termination goes. The de-escalation cost is therefore lower than #447/#462, where the fix converted a loud kill into a SILENTLY refused write. DETECTOR TRAPS: do NOT pin d-&gt;stat (reading +0x8c calls update_stat which overwrites it unconditionally -- born vacuous); and every accept row here asserts ABSENCE of a diagnostic at a warn-once site, so a decline row running earlier in the same session sets the latch, suppresses the growth mutant&#x27;s diagnostic, and leaves the accept row GREEN under its own mutant -- each accept row needs its OWN session, which cuts against the probe&#x27;s one-session rule and the round must say which rule governs which row. SITE A HAS NO STATE CHANNEL: its write branch contains no store even for the legal 0xb, so only an absence-of-diagnostic row is available. PREREQUISITE: gdromwarnbits&#x27; pre-set trigger FIRES HERE -- 6 of 8 bits used, uint8_t, and this round needs 3 more (Site A&#x27;s read branch is an unlatched flood today). 1u&lt;&lt;8 truncates to 0 and the diagnostic floods, so the enum-of-bit-positions conversion is due IN-ROUND, not filed.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — Self-review DISCLOSED on five of its own findings, and harder on them as asked: it checked the author&#x27;s REPAIRS rather than re-arguing the findings, and both defects it reports are in repairs.  OVERTURNED one: the deriver `grep -c &#x27;_B,&#x27;` shipped to fix a wrong count returns FIFTEEN, because three mutant-table comments match it and so does THE DERIVER COMMENT ITSELF -- the quoted-marker-re-arms-the-tool class landing inside the repair for a comment-contaminated count.  REFINED F4: the repair is right but was not grepped for siblings, and R7b ships a STAT leg that cannot vary for the identical reason, while the contract claims it does.  Named the STORE-SIDE dead-session class -- seven of twenty-two rows pass if their critical store never runs, because for those rows the fresh state IS the want -- and correctly filed it rather than reopening, since no run has exhibited it.  On D1, where the ruling ADOPTED its own position, it went looking for the strongest card against itself (#460&#x27;s joined-on-the-merits precedent) and distinguished it, then reported that the ruling&#x27;s own discharge was still OWED. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — CONFIRMED — *** PASS 2 ON `105c15c`, AND IT MEASURED THE ROW I HAD JUST SHIPPED TO BE VACUOUS. ***  **R5d was STORE-DEATH VACUOUS on the committed build: delete the store the row exists to test and the detector still reports 24/24, byte-comparable to the round&#x27;s own preserved `baseline_24of24.out`.**  Its want (`printed(t,&#x27;r2&#x27;) == &#x27;0xef&#x27;`) is placed entirely by the ANCHOR store; the 0x09 decline contributed no channel at all.  *** THE PART THAT OUTLIVES THE ROW: the same commit STATES the governing rule in R12&#x27;s own comment -- &#x27;wants an observable ONLY THE STORE CAN PRODUCE&#x27; -- and violates it 130 lines earlier, in a row added by the same diff.  R12&#x27;s repair and R5d&#x27;s introduction shipped together. ***  That is the CHANNEL check of the pre-table block failing at row-writing time while its own sentence sat in the file, which is this project&#x27;s trigger-versus-memory finding arriving inside the artifact meant to fix it.  **The repair already in the worktree is measured GOOD, both directions**: the same cut against the repaired row gives `0xef|absent` vs `0xef|09`, the ONLY FAIL in 28 rows.  So this run supplies the measured kill R5d never had.  **AND IT BOUNDED ITS OWN FINDING RATHER THAN OVERSTATING IT:** R12b is sound (its `ff` channel comes from `COND_RE`, which only the 0xff decline can produce) but **R12b SUBSUMES R5d&#x27;s stated mutant**, since 0xff carries bits 0 and 3 both.  R5d still earns its place -- a conjunction ANDed with `idata &lt; 0x80` escapes R12b -- but the claim &#x27;R5d is needed for the conjunction family&#x27; is STRONGER THAN THE EVIDENCE, and the row comment should say the narrower thing.  R12b&#x27;s third channel (`clear`) is additionally a NULL OUTCOME: `bit()` guards a dead READ but not a dead `GDROM_EVENT_BIT` constant.  *** IT ALSO RETRACTED ITS OWN FIRST RESULT, WHICH IS WHY THE REST IS CREDIBLE. ***  Its opening store-death run cut THREE stores at once and cascaded (rows 1-14 ok, 15-25 FAIL, one exit1); it had begun writing that up as a probe or contention defect when its own control refuted it -- the unmodified probe scores 25/25, CONTROL=OK, on an idle machine.  What survives is a STRUCTURAL observation with NO measured instance, recorded as such: `GDROMCOND_CONTROL=` is emitted a third of the way through the log, over the four control rows only, so a run that degrades AFTER its controls would report CONTROL=OK beside a wall of product FAILs.  **That is the exact hazard the trailing `Zlive end`/`Zdevsig end` pair was added for, reached independently from the other side** -- and the 31-row run now carries `GDROMCOND_CONTROL_END=OK` beside `GDROMCOND_CONTROL=OK`. — evidence: regress/artifacts/gdromcondlies/opus_p2_20260830/</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — 22 F-verdicts.  Its 26% novelty is NOT the echo-wall signature -- an adjudication pass correctly quotes the findings it rules on, and the echo heuristic assumes a review.  Substantively: the old-bit alias coverage must be swept in the kill table OR described as ONE representative alias check rather than OLD-class coverage (which is what the row comment now discloses), and the STAT no-row sentence needs a qualifier -- observational equivalence through a GDROM_STAT read does not establish GLOBAL equivalence unless no other code consumes `d-&gt;stat` first.  Concluded that with CONTROL=DEAD, no clean 20-row run and no kill table, the round is not yet empirically closable -- which matches the commit&#x27;s own record. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Its list of what blocks a GO is exactly the round&#x27;s own: three contradictory documentation records, the unbuilt kill-table driver, no recorded clean reference run, and an owed CHANGELOG entry. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Adjudicated from source rather than from the author&#x27;s dispositions, opening the probe, the COND arms and PRETABLE before ruling. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Adjudicated the findings and re-derived the flood measurement. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Ruled F1-F9 UPHOLD, F10 REFINE, F11 UPHOLD-with-residual, and gave the sharpest form of the D1 refinement: the FILE ruling stands but its discharge does not exist, and PRETABLE&#x27;s present-tense &#x27;the cost is recorded&#x27; is itself an L4.  On Q2 it declined to name a wrong disposition and said why -- none is wrong on its stated logic, two are incomplete, and ALL TEN are unvalidated pending the clean run.  That is the honest answer to a question designed to catch complacency. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — Named the thing all nine seats had missed, and it was a real gap: NO ROW performed a GDROM_DATA read after a decline, so the probe pinned the CAUSE (cond readback) and never exercised the READ PATH the whole fix exists to protect.  Became R12.  Also REFINED F5 (incomplete alias sweep) and F11 (sentinel ambiguity), and marked each verdict READ or REASONED -- the evidence-ladder discipline, unprompted. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">OK</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;OK&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-30 — MANUAL — 11 upheld, 2 refined, 0 overturned.  Its F11 refinement is the one worth keeping: `0xdead`&#x27;s low byte has bit 7 set, so any row extracting a bit OUTSIDE the `bit()` helper could score &#x27;set&#x27; on a dead read -- and it named the premise it could not settle (whether `bit()` is the only extraction path) rather than assuming.  Settled here by grep: it is, and the one other mask is a WIDTH mask whose row reds on the seed anyway.  Its F10 caveat is also right and is filed rather than fixed: the witness SCRIPT still carries the wrong banner, so a future run needs the same README. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260830_163105</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c hold"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">⏸</span><span class="tip">2026-08-30 — awaiting fable5 — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  Regress review of the CLOSURE, not of the fix.  **This is a hold, not a seat failure and not agreement.**  *** THE REASON IT NEEDS ONE: I closed this row on inference from commit messages, days after the round that shipped the fix, and a HIGH row closed by a third party is the closure most likely to be wrong in a way nobody notices -- the shipping round would have known its own scope; I reconstructed it. ***  The specific risk is the SPLIT: this id was divided, with `gdromcondavail` taking the other defect, and #496 relocated a store on the shared `d-&gt;cond` byte that both rows touch.  *A closure that is right about its own pair can still be wrong about what it implies for its sibling.*</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromexitsix</span><span class="ttl">gdromexitsix  the DMA+0x18 pair ends the host from a protocol-correct guest, and the four command-layer exits split out</span><span class="tip">gdromexitsix  the DMA+0x18 pair ends the host from a protocol-correct guest, and the four command-layer exits split out — commits: 80700b3 7c9dd9e 095aa96 fb0cc78 38b07dc c2249eb 42d5ee5 c43ce97 eae8c8c — witness: probe:regress/gdrom_decline_probe.py -- RUNG 3, and this replaces the weaker `detector:`/`none` reading the round had been carrying.  *** ALL THREE LADDER CLAUSES ARE MET AND THE EVIDENCE WAS ALREADY ON DISK. ***  (i) SYMPTOM ON THE PRE-FIX COMMITTED BUILD, GREEN POST-FIX, SAME SCRIPT: probe_PREFIX.log has `fmt surv` and `unk surv` at **exit1** against a want of exit0 (FAIL); probe_base.log has both at **exit0 (ok)**.  (ii) BOTH MANDATED ACCESS CONTROLS GREEN ON THAT PRE-FIX RUN: liveness `live` 0x12345678 -- a guest store then a guest load through RAM -- and device-signature `devsig sh4` 0xda74, the SH4 IPRD reset value, which RAM would read as 0.  One control is not enough and both are present.  (iii) an UNMODIFIED in-tree machine; no `device_add` and no machine introduced in order to reach the site.  Rung 3 is EQUAL to a boot in admissibility, where `detector:` ranks below every access witness -- so this is a real upgrade to the round&#x27;s standing, not a relabelling.  Logs: regress/artifacts/gdromexitsix/r2measure_20260829/. — note: CLOSED 2026-08-30 as a RECORDS-ONLY closure.  Do NOT open a fix round under this id: all six filed sites are disposed at HEAD.  The two live BADVAL exits (:10